1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Theodora is considered a daughter of Nikephoros Kantakouzenos, sebastokratōr. According to the chronicle of their kinsman John VI Kantakouzenos, Nikephoros was imprisoned by orders of Alexios Apokaukos, one of the 
2 CONC main advisors of Anna of Savoy in the civil war against John VI, in 1341. Nikephoros was later released and recorded being the governor of Adrianople in the 1350s. The identity of her mother is unknown. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The available Latin translations of the chronicle mention Nikephoros as a Patruelis of John VI, a paternal first cousin. Assuming this was not an error in translation, Theodora's paternal grandfather would be a sibling of Michael 
2 CONC Kantakouzenos. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BYZANTIUM%2012611453.htm#Kantadied1294MTheodoraAP
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         NIKEPHOROS Kantakouzenos (-1355 or after).  Ioannes Kantakouzenos names "Nicephorum Cantacuzenum, magni domestici patruelem" (referring to the future Emperor Ioannes VI), in a passage dated to [1341], which records that
2 CONC  he was imprisoned[514].  The same source, in a later passage, names "Nicephorum Sebastocratorem Cantacuzenum" as "Matthæus…patruum", in a passage dated to [1353][515].  Sébastokrator.  He was imprisoned by Apokavkos 1341.  Govern
2 CONC or of Adrianople 1352/55.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' ---.  The name of Nikephoros's wife is not known.  Nikephoros Kantakouzenos & his wife had one child:
