1 OCCU EMPRESS CONSORT OF TREBIZOND EMPIRE AND PRINCESS OF GEORGIA
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulkhan-Eudokia_of_Georgia
2 CONT
2 CONT Gulkhan-Eudokia (Georgian: გულქან-ევდოკია) (died 2 May 1395) was the first Empress consort of Manuel III of Trebizond. Her original name was Gulkhan Khatun; Eudokia was her Christian baptismal name.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Gulkhan was a daughter of David IX of Georgia and his wife Sindukhtar Jaqeli. She was also a sister of Bagrat V of Georgia.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandfather was George V of Georgia. The identity of his wife is not known. "The Georgian Chronicles" of the 18th century reports him marrying a daughter of "the Greek Emperor, Lord Michael Komnenos". However the reig
2 CONC ning dynasty of the Byzantine Empire in the 14th century were the Palaiologoi, not the Komnenoi. The marriage of a daughter of Michael IX Palaiologos and his wife Rita of Armenia to a Georgian ruler is not recorded in Byzantine so
2 CONC urces. Neither is the existence of any illegitimate daughters of Michael IX.[2] The Komnenoi did rule however in the Empire of Trebizond. A Michael Komnenos was Emperor from 1344 to 1349. His wife was Acropolitissa. Their only chi
2 CONC ld recorded in primary sources was John III of Trebizond. Whether John III had siblings is unknown.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her maternal grandfather was Qvarqvare II Jaqeli, Prince of Samtskhe.[4] The Jaqeli held the Georgian feudal office of Eristavi, a title that could be "governor of a region" or an "army-commander",[5] roughly equivalent to the Byz
2 CONC antine strategos and normally translated into English as "duke".
2 CONT
2 CONT  Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Gulkhan was first betrothed or married to Andronikos of Trebizond, an illegitimate son of Alexios III of Trebizond. On 14 March 1376, Andronikos fell from a window in the imperial palace and subsequently died from his injuries. Ac
2 CONC cording to Michael Panaretos, only his mother and the Empress mother took part in the funeral procession, and the betrothal was transferred to Manuel III of Trebizond, whom Panaretos describes as the "younger, proper and legitimat
2 CONC e" son of the Emperor. Details like these have led historians to suspect more to the incident.[6] The Europäische Stammtafeln: Stammtafeln zur Geschichte der Europäischen Staaten (1978) by Detlev Schwennicke reports him killed by 
2 CONC being thrown out of a tower.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Panaretos sets forth Gulkhan's chronology in the marriage process. The revised betrothal was negotiated while she was still residing in Iberia: she left her father's kingdom and met the imperial party at Macragialos on 15 August 1
2 CONC 377; together they returned to Trebizond on Sunday, 30 August. On 5 September Gulkhan was crowned Empress, taking the royal name of Eudokia, and she was married to Manuel the next day by the Metropolitan of Trebizond, Theodosius; 
2 CONC the nuptial festivities lasted a week.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Their only known son would reign as Alexios IV of Trebizond.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Empress
2 CONT
2 CONT On 20 March 1390, Alexios III died. Manuel III succeeded him with Gulkhan as his Empress consort. Her term as Empress was brief, ending with her death on 2 May 1395.[9] Manuel would go on to marry Anna Philanthropene.
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/GEORGIA.htm#dauDavitIXMManuelIIITrebizond<br/>
2 CONT b)         '''GULKHAN''' (-5 May 1395).  The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos records that "the son of the emperor, the despot Lord Andronikos the Grand Komnenos" had been betrothed to "the king of Tbilisi´s daughter, the niece on t
2 CONC he sister´s side of Achpougas", that she was then betrothed to "the younger and legitimate and lawful son of the emperor, the younger emperor Lord Manuel the Grand Komnenos", arrived in Trebizond "around…Sep 5" in 1377, was "crown
2 CONC ed in the imperial [chapel] and named Eudokia, as she was previously called Koulkanchat" and married 6 Sep by the "metropolitan of Trebizond Theodosios"[538].  She adopted the name '''EVDOKIA''' in Byzantium.  The Chronicle of Mic
2 CONC hael Panaretos records the death 5 May 1395 of "the empress Lady Eudokia from Iberia, mother of the emperor Lord Alexios"[539].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Betrothed''' to ANDRONIKOS Komnenos, illegitimate son of ALEXIOS III Emperor in Trebizond & his mistress --- (Nov 1355-[murdered] [14] Mar 1376, bur Theoskepastos).  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (Trebizond 6 Sep 1377) as his first wife, MANUEL III Emperor in Trebizond, son of ALEXIOS III Emperor in Trebizond & his wife Theodora Kantakouzene (16 Dec 1363-5 Mar 1412, bur Theoskepastos). 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References 
