1 NOTE {geni:about_me} from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Konstantin Dejanović''' (Serbian Cyrillic: Константин Дејановић), also known as Constantine Dragaš[a] (Константин Драгаш; fl. 1365-1395) was a Serbian magnate that ruled a large province in eastern Macedonia under Ottoman suzer
2 CONC ainty, during the fall of the Serbian Empire. He succeeded his older brother Jovan Dragaš, who had been an Ottoman vassal since the Battle of Maritsa (1371) which had devastated part of the Serbian nobility. The brothers had their
2 CONC  own government and minted coins according to the Nemanjić style. His daughter Jelena married Byzantine Emperor Manuel II Palaiologos in 1392. He fell at the Battle of Rovine (17 May 1395), serving the Ottomans against Wallachia, 
2 CONC fighting alongside Serbian magnates Stefan Lazarević and Marko Mrnjavčević.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine's grandson, last Byzantine Emperor Constantine XI, was named after him, and even used the name Dragaš.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Life
2 CONT * 1.1 Early
2 CONT * 1.2 Reign
2 CONT * 2 Family
2 CONT * 3 Legacy
2 CONT * 4 Notes
2 CONT * 5 References
2 CONT * 5.1 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT =Life=
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Early Life==
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine's father was the despot and sevastokrator Dejan, who had held the Kumanovo-region under the rule of Stefan Dušan (r. 1331-1355). Constantine's mother Teodora Nemanjić was a half-sister of Dušan. His maternal grandparen
2 CONC ts were King Stefan Dečanski (r. 1321-1331) and Queen Maria Palaiologina.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Reign==
2 CONT
2 CONT In ca. 1365, his older brother Jovan Dragaš was holding Štip and Strumica. Jovan was elevated to despot by Emperor Uroš V (before 1373), as Emperor Dušan had elevated Dejan, their father. Ottoman sources report that in 1373, the O
2 CONC ttoman army compelled "Saruyar" (Jovan Dragaš) in the upper Struma, to recognize Ottoman vassalship.[1] Constantine had helped Jovan in ruling the lands, and when Jovan died in 1378/1379, Constantine succeeded, subsequently managi
2 CONC ng to govern large portions of northeastern Macedonia and the Struma valley.
2 CONT
2 CONT He minted coins, as had his brother done.[2] The Dragaš family generously donated to several monasteries on Mount Athos, including Hilandar, Pantaleimon (Rossikon) and Vatopédi.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 10 February 1392 his daughter Jelena married Manuel II Palaiologos. The next day, they were crowned Emperor and Empress by the patriarch.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the battle of Maritsa, they were forced to become vassals of the Ottoman Empire, but they maintained close links with their Christian neighbors, including the Byzantine Empire. In 1395, together with his neighbor and ally, t
2 CONC he Serbian king of Prilep Marko, Constantine Dragaš was killed fighting for their Ottoman overlord Sultan Bayezid I against Mircea cel Bătrân of Wallachia at Rovine, near Craiova. The Ottomans named Constantine's capital Velbažd/V
2 CONC elbužd after him, Köstendil (now Bulgarian Kyustendil).
2 CONT
2 CONT =Family=
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine Dragaš was married twice. The name of his first wife is unknown, but she is not identical with Thamar (Tamara), the daughter of the Emperor (tsar) Ivan Alexander of Bulgaria, who had married a certain despotēs Constant
2 CONC ine. Constantine Dragaš married as his second wife Eudokia of Trebizond, daughter of Emperor Alexios III of Trebizond and Theodora Kantakouzene. By his first wife, Constantine Dragaš had at least one daughter and possibly a son:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Helena Dragases (Jelena Dragaš, nun Hypomone), who married the Byzantine Emperor Manuel II Palaiologos and died on 13 May 1450. Their many children included the last two Byzantine emperors, of whom Constantine XI added the name 
2 CONC Dragaš (in Greek, Dragasēs) to his own. Constantine XI was named after his grandfather.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Legacy=
2 CONT
2 CONT He is venerated in Serb epic poetry as '''Beg Kostadin''' (in poetry he was given a title of beg because he became an Ottoman vassal).[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Notes=
2 CONT
2 CONT #  Name: His name is attested as Serbian ''Konstantin Dejanović'' and'' Konstantin Dragaš'' (Константин Дејановић / Драгаш), in Greek: Kωvσταντίνος Δραγάσης, ''Konstantínos Dragáses'', Bulgarian: Константин Драгаш.
2 CONT #  Titles: Constantine's titles vary in sources, where he is called variously "lord" (sr. ''gospodin'', gr. ''kyr'' or ''authentēs''), and he may have acquired the title of Despot (''despotēs''), perhaps by his son-in-law, the Byz
2 CONC antine Emperor Manuel II Palaiologos.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Edition de lA̕cadémie bulgare des sciences, 1986, "Balkan studies, Vol. 22" p. 38
2 CONT # David Michael Metcalf, "Coinage in South Eastern Europe, 820-1396", Royal Numismatic Society, 1979, p. 322
2 CONT # Donald M. Nicol, "Byzantium and Venice: a study in diplomatic and cultural relations", p. 331
2 CONT # Head, Constance (1977), "+Manuel+and+helena" Imperial Twilight: The Palaiologos Dynasty and the Decline of Byzantium, Nelson-Hall, p. 145, "Constantine" was a good name, Manuel and Helena believed; for one thing, it was the name
2 CONC  of Helena's father.
2 CONT # Čubelić, Tvrtko (1970). Epske narodne pjesme: izbor tekstova s komentarima i objašnjenjima i rasprava o epskim narodnim pjesmama (in Serbian) (6 ed.). p. cxii. Retrieved 19 July 2012. Kostadin — beg Kostadin historijsko lice; si
2 CONC n je Dušanova velikaša Dejana, a bio je gospodar sjeveroistočne Makedonije. Poslije bitke na Marici 1371. postao je turski vazal; zato ga pjesma naziva beg Kostadin
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sources==
