1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Alexios Angelos Philanthropenos''' (Greek: Ἁλέξιος Ἂγγελος Φιλανθρωπηνός) was a Byzantine Greek nobleman who ruled Thessaly from 1373 until c. 1390 (from c. 1382 as a Byzantine vassal) with the title of Caesar.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexios_Angelos_Philanthropenos En], [https://sr.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%90%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B8%D1%98%D0%B5_%D0%90%D0%BD%D1%92%D0%B5%D0%BB_%D0%A4%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%82%D1%80%D0%BE%D0
2 CONC %BF%D0%B5%D0%BD Sr], [https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexios_Angelos_Philanthropenos De]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note:''' His first name was Alexios, surname: Angelos Philanthropenos, title: Caesar, not as: first name: Caesar Alexios Angelos, surname: Philanthropenos.
2 CONT
2 CONT The [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angelos Angeloi] of Thessaly rose to prominence during the reign of the [https://www.geni.com/people/Simeon-Uro%C5%A1-despot-of-Epirus-Thessaly/6000000002188625349 '''Simeon Uroš'''] ruler of Epi
2 CONC rus and Thessaly (r. 1359–1370), as they were related to his wife. When [https://www.geni.com/people/Simeon-Uro%C5%A1-despot-of-Epirus-Thessaly/6000000002188625349 '''Simeon''']  died in 1370, he was succeeded by his son [https://
2 CONC www.geni.com/people/Ioannes-Doukas-ruler-of-Thessaly/5120539876050083627 '''John Uroš'''] (r. 1370–1373). John cared more for religious pursuits than the governance of the state, and entrusted the affairs of state to Alexios, who 
2 CONC held the high Byzantine title of Caesar. Eventually, probably in 1372/73, John retired from public life altogether and entered a monastery, leaving Alexios as the ruler of Thessaly.[1][2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexios was married to [https://www.geni.com/people/Maria-Angelina-Philanthropenos/6000000173488102266 '''Maria Angelina Radoslava'''] (born late 1356 or after, if she is the daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Irina-Hlapen/6
2 CONC 000000010113955774 '''Irene Asanina''']), a daughter of the Serbian general [https://www.geni.com/people/Radoslav-Hlapen/6000000010113983434 '''Radoslav Hlapen'''], and was supported by the powerful local magnates. Around 1382, Al
2 CONC exios sought the protection of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Byzantine_Empire Byzantine Empire], recognizing the suzerainty of the prince [https://www.geni.com/people/Manuel-II-Palaiologos-Byzantine-emperor/600000000067027930
2 CONC 0 '''Manuel Palaiologos'''], who at the time governed Thessalonica as an appanage. Alexios is lastly recorded in 1388, and must have died by c. 1390, when he was succeeded by his son (or perhaps brother), [https://www.geni.com/peo
2 CONC ple/Caesar-Manuel-Angelos-Philanthropenos/6000000008521782674 '''Manuel'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Either he or, more plausibly, [https://www.geni.com/people/Caesar-Manuel-Angelos-Philanthropenos/6000000008521782674 '''Manuel'''], was the grandfather of the mid-15th century Serbian ruler [https://www.geni.com/people/Michael-Ang
2 CONC elovi%C4%87/6000000205117708830 '''Mihailo Anđelović'''] and the Ottoman Grand Vizier [https://www.geni.com/people/Mahmud-Angelovi%C4%87-13th-Grand-Vizier/6000000205117325825 '''Mahmud Pasha Angelović'''].[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''See''': [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philanthropenos Philanthropenos - House of Philanthropenos], [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angelos Angelos - House of Angelos ], [https://www.geni.com/people/Alexios-III-byzantine-emperor
2 CONC /6000000000515214098  Alexios III, byzantine emperor], [https://www.geni.com/people/John-V-Palaiologos-byzantine-emperor/361089507860011643 https://www.geni.com/people/John-V-Palaiologos-byzantine-emperor/361089507860011643], [htt
2 CONC ps://www.geni.com/people/N-Philanthropenos/6000000063730828849 N Philanthropenos], etc ...; http://genealogy.euweb.cz/byzant/byzant4.html , http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BYZANTIUM%2012611453.htm#_Toc359579157 , [https://history-
2 CONC maps.com/story/Byzantine-Empire-Palaiologos-dynasty Byzantine Empire: Palaiologos dynasty 1261-1453]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note:''' Somewhere is exact connection and relationships between Philanthropenos, Angelos, Hlapen families and others. The descendants of Philanthropenos and Angelos had and mentioned with double surname as: Philanthropenos Ang
2 CONC elos (or Angelos Philanthropenos) or just Philanthropenos / or Angelos.
