1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Most likely he married a daughter of Q’varq’vare I as Q’varq’vare II lived a century after Bagrat's death. The Jaqeli family has a complicated genealogy, so Wikipedia's numbering can be a little off at times. Also,
2 CONC  David IX was married to a daughter of Q’varq’vare I, so this marriage would have made the king Bagrat's brother-in-law, thus requiring the king's consent to the marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bagrat_I_of_Imereti '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Bagrat I the Minor''' (Georgian: ბაგრატ მცირე, Bagrat Mts'ire; died 1372), of the Bagrationi dynasty, was king of western Georgian kingdom of Imereti from 1329 until 1330, when he was reduced to a vassal duke by George V of Geo
2 CONC rgia.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Career=
2 CONT
2 CONT Bagrat was the only known son of King Michael of Imereti, on whose death he succeeded in 1329. Still a minor at this time (hence, his moniker mts'ire), Bagrat was compelled to remain in his capital, Kutatisi, as the provinces were
2 CONC  being divided by the rivaling noble factions. In 1330, George V, the resurgent king in eastern Georgia, took advantage of the situation and of being Bagrat's relative and crossed the Likhi Range into Imereti, being welcomed by ma
2 CONC ny Imeretians, worn of persistent violence and anarchy. Imereti was conquered and the integrity of the Kingdom of Georgia restored. Henceforth, Bagrat sat as eristavi ("duke") in Imereti, with his seat in Shorapani, under the tute
2 CONC lage of George V.[1][2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Family=
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1358, Bagrat married, with the approval of David IX of Georgia, a daughter of Qvarqvare II (see note at the top), a Jaqeli atabeg of Samtskhe. They had three sons:[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Alexander I (died 1389), Duke of Imereti (1372–1378), King of Imereti (1387–1389).
2 CONT * George I (died 1392), King of Imereti (1389–1392).
2 CONT * Constantine II (died 1401), King of Imereti (1396–1401).
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Bagrationi, Vakhushti (1976). Nakashidze, N.T., ed. История Царства Грузинского [History of the Kingdom of Georgia] (PDF) (in Russian). Tbilisi: Metsniereba. p. 41.
2 CONT # Rayfield, Donald (2012). Edge of Empires: A History of Georgia. London: Reaktion Books. p. 140. ISBN 1780230303.
2 CONT # Lang, David Marshall (1955). "Georgia in the Reign of Giorgi the Brilliant (1314–1346)". Bulletin of the School of Oriental and African Studies, University of London 17 (1): 87. doi:10.1017/s0041977x00106354.
