1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jahan Shah, the Qara-Quyunlu ruler of AdharbayjSn, set out from Tabriz °,  on May 4, 1468, to annex Diyfir Bakr. His vanguard was surprised  by Amir Hasan Beg in the desert of Mush 8 , He himself was put to death w
2 CONC hile he was  staying behind, and his head was sent to Sultan Abu Sa'Id ij, the Timurid ruler of  Khurasan. His two sons, named Muhammad! and Yiisuf. were also* captured, of whom  the former was put to death and the latter blinded.
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT Hasan 'Ali, another son of Jahan Shah, invoked the help of Sultan Abu Sa'Id,  who advanced from Khurasan, but before his arrival in AdharbayjSn, Hasan 'All  was deserted by his soldiers at Maraud and was thus compelled to flee bac
2 CONC k to MiySna,  where he joined Sultan Abu Sa'Id. Amir Hasan Beg tried to*nake peace, but the  mission proved unsuccessful, and Sultan Abu Sa'Id advanced on him. In the ensuing  battle, on Jan. 30, 1469, Sultan Abu Sa'Id was defeate
2 CONC d and taken pri-  soner. Two days later, he was handed over to Mlrza Yfulgiir Muhammad, the Timurid,  and was put to death on 2 /Feb. 5. 1469. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hasan 'Ali succeeded in making his escape to Hamadan, but was pursued by  Ughurlu Muhammad ibn Amir Hasan Beg, and put to death in Apr.,  1469. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Amir Hasan Beg now became the master of Azarbaijan and the two 'Iraqs,  and fixed his capital at Tabriz.
2 CONT
2 CONT ref:https://archive.org/stream/HistoryOfShahIsmailSafawi-GhulamSarwar/119900259-History-of-Shah-Isma-il-Safawi-Ghulam-Sarwar_djvu.txt 
2 CONT
