1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radoslav_Hlapen
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Radoslav Hlapen''' (Serbian: Радослав Хлапен; fl. 1350–1383) was a Serbian magnate who served Serbian Emperor Stefan Dušan (r. 1331–1355) and Stefan Uroš V (r. 1355–71) as vojvoda (military commander). He took part in the conqu
2 CONC est of Byzantine lands, and was given a region north of Thessaly to govern in the early 1350s.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Origin 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is believed that Radoslav Hlapen is the same person as župan (count) Hlapen (Latin: Clapen) who governed Konavle and the wider Trebinje region in the 1330s.[2] He was possibly the son of župan Radoslav,[2] and thus named Radosl
2 CONC av after his father.[3] Another theory is that he was the son of Syrgiannes Palaiologos.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Byzantine Emperor John VI Kantakouzenos (r. 1347–54) mentioned him as among the most important nobles, and he was called a relative of Dušan.
2 CONT
2 CONT He retired as a monk in the Vodoča monastery where he also was buried. His votive ring was found at the site.[9] He also founded a church in Kučevište, Skopje,[9] a monastery in Ostrovo,[10] and a monastery in Greece.
2 CONT
2 CONT Radoslav Hlapen married Irina (Irene), the widow of caesar Preljub. They had the following issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Maria Angelina Radoslava, married Alexios Angelos Philanthropenos
2 CONT # Jelena, married Prince Marko
2 CONT # Unnamed, married Nikola Bagaš
2 CONT # Unnamed, married John Uroš[1]
2 CONT # Stefan
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Uros John Uroš married a daughter of Radoslav Hlapen, a Serbian lord in Macedonia. According to the manuscript Dell'Imperadori Constantinopolitani, preserved in the papers of Angelo Masarelli, 
2 CONC the father of John's wife was "lord of Drima" ("l Signor Drimi").
