2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00064447&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh III of Cyprus (1235 – 24 March 1284), born Hughues de Poitiers[1], later Hughues de Lusignan (he adopted his mother's surname de Lusignan in 1267), called the Great, was the King of Cyprus from 1267 and King of Jerusalem from
2 CONC  1268 (as Hugh I of Jerusalem). He was the son of Henry of Antioch and Isabella of Cyprus, the daughter of Hugh I. He was a grandson of Bohemund IV of Antioch.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1261 he served as Regent for Hugh II of Cyprus in Cyprus, as the Haute Cour of Cyprus considered him, as a male, a better regent than his mother Isabella. She was, however, accepted as the Regent of Jerusalem in 1263. She die
2 CONC d in 1264, and Hugh became the acting regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem as well as Cyprus. The regency was contested by his first cousin, Hugh of Brienne, who was the son of Mary of Cyprus, the eldest daughter of Hugh I and hence
2 CONC  the senior heir to Cyprus, and heir to Jerusalem after Hugh II. However, the Haute Cour of Jerusalem declared Hugh of Antioch the next regent, as successor to Isabella in proximity of blood.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh II died in 1267 without heirs. As Hugh of Brienne did not advance his claim on the throne, Hugh of Antioch succeeded as uncontested King of Cyprus on 5 December and was crowned at Santa Sophia, in Nicosia, on 24 December. He 
2 CONC claimed the Kingdom of Jerusalem as well in 1267 or 1268 upon the execution of Conradin. However, the throne of Jerusalem was also claimed by Mary of Antioch by proximity of blood to Conradin. The Haute Cour of Jerusalem rejected 
2 CONC her claim and Hugh was crowned King of Jerusalem at Tyre on 24 September 1269.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh and his descendants, the Kings of Cyprus, assumed his mother's surname of Lusignan in 1267, having inherited Cyprus through that family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh disliked dealing with the various factions in the Kingdom of Jerusalem, and left for Cyprus in 1276 in disgust at their defiance of his authority. The next year, his bailiff, Balian of Ibelin, Lord of Arsuf, was ejected by Ro
2 CONC ger of Sanseverino, the bailiff of Charles of Anjou, who had purchased the claim of Mary of Antioch. The kingdom remained under Angevin control for the rest of Hugh's reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is supposed that Thomas Aquinas' work On Kingship was written for Hugh III.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried at Santa Sophia, in Nicosia.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married after 23 January 1255 to Isabella of Ibelin (ca 1241 – 2 June 1324). He had eleven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT John II & I of Lusignan (died 1285) who succeeded him as King of Jerusalem and Cyprus
2 CONT
2 CONT Bohemond of Lusignan (ca 1268 – Tyre, 3 November 1281, buried at the Franciscan Church of Nicosia)
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry of Lusignan (1271 – 31 August 1324) who succeeded John as King
2 CONT
2 CONT Amaury II of Lusignan, Prince of Tyre (died 5 June 1310), Constable of Jerusalem, who displaced Henry and became Regent of Cyprus
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary of Lusignan (1273 – September, 1322 at Tortosa and buried at Barcelona) who married by proxy at Santa Sophia, Nicosia on 15 June 1315 and in person at Girona on 27 November 1315 James II of Aragon (10 August 1267 – 2 November
2 CONC  1327)
2 CONT
2 CONT Aimery of Lusignan (1274-1280 – soon before 9 April 1316), succeeded Guy as Constable of Cyprus in 1303, briefly succeeded Amalric as Regent of Cyprus and Governor of Cyprus on June 6, 1310
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy of Lusignan (1275-1280–1303, probably buried at Nicosia), Constable of Cyprus ca 1291, married on 7 December 1291 Eschiva of Ibelin, Lady of Beirut (1253–1312), parents of: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh IV of Lusignan, King of Cyprus
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of Lusignan (1296-1300 – after 1340), married on 21 July 1322 Eudes de Dampierre, Titular Constable of Jerusalem (died 1330)
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret of Lusignan (ca 1276 – in Armenia, 1296), who married 9 January 1288 Thoros III of Armenia
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice of Lusignan (1277-1280 – after March, 1324), married 1292-1295 or ca 1292/1294 Balian of Ibelin (died 1315/1316 in Kerynia, soon before 19 April 1316), Titular Prince of Galilee and Bethlehem
2 CONT
2 CONT Helvis of Lusignan (died after March, 1324), married Hethum II of Armenia
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of Lusignan (ca 1280–1319), married firstly in 1285/1290 Constantine of Neghir, Lord of Partzerpert (died 1308), and secondly ca 1310 King Oshin of Armenia, who divorced her before or in 1316
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_III_of_Cyprus
