2 CONT
2 CONT Sheikh Junayd (died 1460) was the son of Shaykh Ibrahim. After the death of his father, he assumed the leadership of the Safaviyya from 1447–1460.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Junayd, the Safaviyya was transformed from a Sufi order organized around a saint-ascetic into an active military movement with a policy of conquest and domination. He was the first Safavi spiritual leader to espouse specific
2 CONC ally Shi’i sentiments, and in particular those of the Twelver ghulat. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Junayd was viewed as a divine incarnation by his followers.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his time in Ardabil, Junayd attracted so many disciples that in 1448, Jahan Shah (the Kara Koyunlu prince) drove him into exile to Anatolia and Syria. While there, he engaged in missionary activities and accumulated Turkmen
2 CONC  followers.[5] He then went to the court of Uzun Hassan at Diyarbakır, where he married Uzun Hassan’s sister, Khadija Begum, somewhere between 1456-1459.
2 CONT
2 CONT Junayd was prevented from returning to Ardabil, so he lived at Shirvan where he died in a local skirmish near the Samur River in what is modern Azerbaijan, where he was buried. This led to the beginning of animosity between the ma
2 CONC inly Sunni Shirvanshah and the increasingly heterodox Shi’i Safaviyya.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mausoleum of Sheikh Juneyd is located in the village of Khazra in Azerbaijan. Junayd was succeeded by his son Shaykh Haydar.
