1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Nikola Bagaš (Serbian: Никола Багаш, Latin: Nicholas Baldouin Pagases), or Nikola Baldovin Bagaš (fl. 1354—1384), was a 14th-century Serbian nobleman from the Bagaš noble family, lord (župan) of Edessa[A][1] and Tr
2 CONC ikala.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nikola_Bagas
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1384, before his region would be completely annexed to the Ottoman Empire, Bagaš donated the monastery of Mesonesiotissa near Edessa, together with villages, churches and other property, to the Athonite monastery of Saint Paul 
2 CONC (Agiou Pavlou) based on the request of his brother Antonije.[3] In the document made on that occasion Bagaš did not use any title for himself.[4] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to the daughter of Radoslav Hlapen[5] and received Voden (modern day Edessa in Greece) a dowry[6] probably around 1366—7.[7] Before 1385, Bagaš became a vassal of the Ottoman sultan Murad I.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
