1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Amir Aslan's protector. He is not an Afshar. Governor of Diyabakr and principal general at the battle of Chalderan, where he died. (p.66Frontier Nomads of Iran: A Political and Social History of the Shahsevan) Par 
2 CONC Richard Tapper
2 CONT
2 CONT Muhammad Khan Ustajlu, the newly appointed Safawi governor https://archive.org/stream/HistoryOfShahIsmailSafawi-GhulamSarwar/119900259-History-of-Shah-Isma-il-Safawi-Ghulam-Sarwar_djvu.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT Qaytmas Beg, brother of Amir Beg Mawsilii, held the fort of Qara Ham id before which Muhammad Khan Ustajlu, the newly appointed Safawi governor, encamped to spend the winter of 913/1507-8. Instigated by Qaytmas Beg, the Kurds mole
2 CONC sted the Qizil-Bash camp, whereupon, Muhammad Khan Ustajlu marched into the Kurdish territories, and, in a decisive and sanguinary fight, left 700 Kurds dead on the field of battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alarmed at the success of the expedition, Qaytmas Beg sought help from 'Ala'u'd-Dawla Dhu'l-Qadr, who forthwith sent* 10,000 men under the command of his own sons, Sarii Qapalan and Urduana Beg.
2 CONT
2 CONT To meet this new menace, Muhammad Khan Ustajlu had only 2,000 men, of which 800 represented the skirmishing party of his brother Qara Beg. The battle commenced, and these latter were dismounted by a violent charge of Sard Qapalan,
2 CONC  but, eventually, the rare bravery of Muhammad Khan Ustajlu enabled him to gain a complete victory. The enemy is alleged to have lost 700 officers in battle; 32 others, inclusive of Sarii Qapalan and Urduana Beg, were taken prison
2 CONC er, and immediately decapitated, and trophies of their heads were sent as a present to the Shah at Khuy. The casualty list is no doubt exaggerated, for an army of 10,000 men could hardly have had 732 officers exclusive of those wh
2 CONC o fled from the field of battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT This engagement sealed the fate of Qaytmas Beg. He and his retinue were butchered, when after a short siege the fort of Qara Hamid fell into the hands of Muhammad Khan Ustajlu 9 .
2 CONT
2 CONT To avenge the loss of his sons and his army, "'Ala'u'd-Dawla Dhu'l-Qadr despatched, in the early spring of 913/1508, another force 10 , but of 15,000 11 men, under his two other sons, Kur Shah Rukh and Ahmad Beg. ... Meanwhile, Mu
2 CONC hammad Khan Ustajlu had moved to Mardin, and his  brother Qara, Beg had invaded Jazira, killing and plundering the Kurds.  The advent of a second Dhu'l-Qadr army recalled Muhammad Khan Ustajlu  to Qara Hamld. His 3,000 men were re
2 CONC solved into a right wing commanded  by himself; a centre under Acha Sultan Qajar; and a left wing under Qara  Beg. The 15,000 troops of the enemy comprised a right wing under Kur  Shah Rukh, Murad Beg and Qaytmas Beg; a centre und
2 CONC er Muhammad Beg  ibn 'Aziz Aqa; and a left wing under Ahmad Beg, 'Abdu'llah Beg and  Urkamaz Beg. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The battle began with a repulse from the Qizil-Bash centre to meet  the charge of the enemy's centre ; then the right and left wings of the enemy  advanced simultaneously upon the Qizil-Bashes, whose right and left wings  closed i
2 CONC n upon the centre to sustain the charge. Then followed a fierce,  compact, Qizil-Bash attack, which broke the enemy. The prisoners included  tJrkamaz Beg, Qaytmas Beg, and two grandsons of 'Ala'u'd-Dawla Dhu'l-  Qadr (sons of Kur 
2 CONC Shah Rukh), named Muhammad Beg and 'All Beg, who  escaped the general butchery in which Kur Shah Rukh, Ahmad Beg,  'Abdu'llah Beg, Muhammad Beg ibn 'Aziz Aqa, Murad Beg, and fifty other  officers perished without obtaining quarter
2 CONC . * 
2 CONT
2 CONT The victims' heads were sent \Vith four prisoners and a letter of victory  to the Shah at Haniadan, who was on his way to Baghdad. The prisoners  were released; the grandsons of 'Ala'u'd-Dawla Dhu'l-Qadr received a  pension; and M
2 CONC uhammad Khan Ustajlu was rewarded with a golden belt,  a cap, and robe of honour 
2 CONT ==
2 CONT Mohammad Khan Ustajlu (died 1514) was a Safavid military commander and official of Turkoman origin, who served during the reign of king Ismail I (r. 1501–1524). He played a pivotal role in Ismail I's conquests and expansion in Asi
2 CONC a Minor and Mesopotamia, and functioned as governor of the Diyarbakr Province from 1506 to 1514.[1] Mohammad Khan was killed while serving as a commander at the Battle of Chaldiran.
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Mohammad Khan Ustajlu was a son of Mirza Beg Ustajlu, and had three brothers; Owlash Beg Ustajlu, Evaz Beg Ustajlu and Qara Beg Ustajlu. He was married to one of king Ismail I's sisters.[2][3] When Ismail I returned to Khoy in 150
2 CONC 6, after some skirmishes with 'Ala' al-Dowleh, the ruler of the Zu'l-Qadr, he appointed Mohammad Khan Ustajlu as the new governor (hakem) of Diyarbakr.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT About two months before the decisive Battle of Chaldiran (1514), Ottoman Sultan Selim I (r. 1511–1520) and his army had reached the town of Sivas.[4] Aware of the massive approach, Mohammad Khan Ustajlu, during his retreat from hi
2 CONC s governoral seat in Diyarbakr, devastated the area, which slowed the Ottoman progress towards the east in the ensuing weeks to come.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 23 August 1514, the day the decisive battle took place, Mohammad Khan Ustajlu and Nur-Ali Khalifa were the two Safavid commanders who had first-hand experience with the Ottomans ways of warfare.[5] They both advised to attack a
2 CONC t once, in order to prevent the Ottoman's from establishing their proper defensive positions.[5] Mohammad Khan also counseled against a frontal attack due to the strength of the Ottoman artillery.[5] This advice however, was rejec
2 CONC ted by both Durmish Khan Shamlu as well as by the king himself.[5] Durmish Khan, a high-ranking member of the Qizilbash and a prominent figure at court because of his connections, rudely rebuffed Mohammad Khan and Nur-Ali Khalifa.
2 CONC [5][4] Durmish Khan considered it "cowardly to engage an unprepared enemy".[4] Ismail I chose to endorse Durmish Khan Shamlu's suggestion for the attack and thus the Ottomans were allowed to prepare their defenses at their leisure
2 CONC .[4][6] This would prove costly, contributing to the sound Safavid defeat at Chaldiran.[4][6] Mohammad Khan, who was in command of the left wing of the Safavid army, was killed, and his men fell in disarray.[7]  ref:https://en.wik
2 CONC ipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad_Khan_Ustajlu
