2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT His second marriage was to a distant relative, Zoe Doukaina, a granddaughter of Isaac Komnenos, the son of Emperor John II Komnenos. They had three sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodore Komnenos Doukas, who succeeded his half-brother Michael as ruler of Epirus
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuel Komnenos Doukas, who succeeded Theodore as ruler of Thessalonica
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine Komnenos Doukas, who succeeded Theodore as ruler of Acarnania
2 CONT
2 CONT He had three daughters, none of whose names is known for certain.
2 CONT
2 CONT A daughter, who married Mateo Orsini, count of Cephalonia; their son Riccardo was count of Cephalonia from 1260 to 1304.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another daughter married Michael Kantakouzenos, who, with Theodore Branas, John Petraliphas and others, conspired to overthrow Isaac II and to bring Alexios III Angelos to power in 1195.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also had an illegitimate son by an unnamed mistress:
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael I Komnenos Doukas, born c. 1170, founder and first ruler of the Despotate of Epirus
2 CONT
