1 OCCU Princess of Suavia, Queen of Castilla, aka Beatriz de Suabia, Reina de Castilla y de León entre 1220 y 1235. Fue hija de Felipe, Duque de Suabia y Rey de Romanos, y de Irene Ángelo, nacida ésta de Isaac II Ángelo, Emperador de Con
2 CONC stantinopla., Reina
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elisabeth of Hohenstaufen
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elisabeth_of_Hohenstaufen
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Hohenstaufen (called Beatriz de Suabia in Spanish) (1203 – 5 November 1235 in Toro, Spain), Queen of Castile 1219-1235, Queen of Leon 1230–1235. She was the fourth daughter of Philip, Duke of Swabia and King of German
2 CONC y, and Irene Angelina, daughter of Emperor Isaac II Angelos of the Byzantine Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her father Philip, she became the ward of her cousin, Frederick, King of Sicily (later Emperor Frederick II); he later married her to Ferdinand III, King of Castile and Leon (called the Saint). The marriage was 
2 CONC celebrated at the end of November of 1219 or 1220 in the Royal Monastery of San Zoilo in Carrión de los Condes (Palencia). In Castile, she was known as Beatriz.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/GERMANY,%20Kings.htm#ElisabethMFernandoIIICastiledied1252
2 CONT
2 CONT e)         ELISABETH von Staufen (Nürnberg Mar/May 1205-Toro 5 Nov 1235, bur Cistercian monastery Santa María la Real, transferred 1279 to the Cathedral Santa María, Seville).  The Annales Marbacenses record that one of the four d
2 CONC aughters of King Philipp (second in the list) married "regi Hyspanie" but does not name her[622].  The primary source which confirms her name has not yet been identified.   The Chronicon de Cardeña records that “Rey D. Ferrando” m
2 CONC arried “Doña Beatriz, la sobrina del Emperador de Alemaña”[623].  She was known as BEATRIZ in Castille.  The Crónica Latina records that “el emperador Isaac” was grandfather of “la reina nuestra señora Beatriz, padre…de su madre”[
2 CONC 624].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (Burgos 30 Nov 1219) as his first wife, don FERNANDO III “el Santo” King of Castile, son of don ALFONSO IX King of León & his second wife Infanta doña Berenguela de Castilla (Monte de Valparaíso [30 Jul/5 Aug] 1201-Seville
2 CONC  30 May 1252, bur Seville, Cathedral Santa María). 
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatriz de Suabia (1202 - Toro, 1235), Reina de Castilla y de León entre 1220 y 1235. Fue hija de Felipe, Duque de Suabia y Rey de Romanos, y de Irene Ángelo, nacida ésta de Isaac II Ángelo, Emperador de Constantinopla. Después de
2 CONC  la muerte de Felipe estuvo bajo la tutela de Federico II, Sacro Emperador Romano Germánico; éste dio su autorización para un matrimonio con Fernando III, Rey de Castilla y León (llamado el Santo), unión que se concretó a fines de
2 CONC  noviembre de 1219 ó 1220 en el Real Monasterio de San Zoilo en Carrión de los Condes (Palencia). De dicha unión nacieron:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso el Sabio (1221 – 1284). Rey de Castilla y de León con el nombre Alfonso X; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fadrique (1223 – 1277), ejecutado en secreto por su hermano Alfonso; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando (1225 – 1243/48); 
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonor (1227 – ¿?, murió joven); 
2 CONT
2 CONT Berenguela (1228 – 1288/89), monja en Las Huelgas; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Enrique (1230 – 1304), llamado el Senador; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Felipe (1231 – 1274). Estaba prometido a la iglesia, pero quedó tan prendado de la belleza de Cristina de Noruega (hija de Haakon IV), prometida a uno de sus hermanos, que abandonó sus votos y se casó con ella. No tuvieron hijos; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho (1233 – 1261), Arzobispo de Toledo y Sevilla; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuel (1234 – 1283), Señor de Villena; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria, murió joven en noviembre de 1235. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatriz de Suabia fue sepultada en el Real Monasterio de Santa María la Real de Las Huelgas de Burgos, junto al Rey Enrique I. Su hijo Alfonso trasladó su cuerpo a Sevilla, donde reposaba el de Fernando.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Hohenstaufen
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Hohenstaufen (called Beatriz de Suabia in Spanish) (1203 – Toro, 1235), Queen of Castile 1219-1235, Queen of Leon 1230–1235. She was daughter of Philip, Duke of Swabia and King of Germany, and Irene Angelina, daughter
2 CONC  of Emperor Isaac II Angelos of the Byzantine Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Philip, she became the ward of her cousin, Frederick, King of Sicily (later Emperor Frederick II); he then married her to Ferdinand III, King of Castile and Leon (called the Saint), the marriage taking place at 
2 CONC the end of the November of 1219 or 1220 in the Royal Monastery of San Zoilo in Carrión de los Condes (Palencia). In Castile, she was known as Beatriz.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Children of Elisabeth and Ferninand
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso X (called the Wise)
2 CONT
2 CONT Fadrique of Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando
2 CONT
2 CONT Enrique
2 CONT
2 CONT Felipe. Married Princess Kristina of Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuel
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonor
2 CONT
2 CONT Berenguela.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Tudense affirms that there was another daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth was buried in the Royal Monastery of Huelgas de Burgos, next to King Enrique I. Her son Alfonso transferred her body to Seville, where that of her husband rested.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Elisabeth of Hohenstaufen (called Beatriz de Suabia in Spanish) (1203 – Toro, Spain, 1235), Queen of Castile 1219-1235, Queen of Leon 1230–1235. She was daughter of Philip, Duke of Swabia and King of Germany, and Irene Angelina, d
2 CONC aughter of Emperor Isaac II Angelos of the Byzantine Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Philip, she became the ward of her cousin, Frederick, King of Sicily (later Emperor Frederick II); he then married her to Ferdinand III, King of Castile and Leon (called the Saint), the marriage taking place at 
2 CONC the end of the November of 1219 or 1220 in the Royal Monastery of San Zoilo in Carrión de los Condes (Palencia). In Castile, she was known as Beatriz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth was buried in the Royal Monastery of Huelgas de Burgos, next to King Enrique I. Her son Alfonso transferred her body to Seville, where that of her husband rested.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Elisabeth of Hohenstaufen (called Beatriz de Suabia in Spanish) (1203 – 5 November 1235[1] in Toro, Spain), Queen of Castile 1219-1235, Queen of Leon 1230–1235. She was the fourth daughter of Philip, Duke of Swabia and King of Ger
2 CONC many, and Irene Angelina, daughter of Emperor Isaac II Angelos of the Byzantine Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her father Philip, she became the ward of her cousin, Frederick, King of Sicily (later Emperor Frederick II); he later married her to Ferdinand III, King of Castile and Leon (called the Saint). The marriage was 
2 CONC celebrated at the end of November of 1219 or 1220 in the Royal Monastery of San Zoilo in Carrión de los Condes (Palencia). In Castile, she was known as Beatriz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Elisabeth and Ferdinand:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso X (called the Wise) (1221–1284)  Infante Frederick of Castile (1223–1277)  Ferdinand, died without issue.  Henry  Philip, married Princess Kristina of Norway.  Henry "the Senator", Lord of Ecija.  Sancho, Archbishop of Tol
2 CONC edo from 1251-1261.  Manuel  Eleanor, died in infancy.  Berengaria, nun in Las Huelgas, died in 1279.  Lucas de Tuy affirms that there was another daughter: Maria, died in infancy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth was buried in the Royal Monastery of Huelgas de Burgos, next to King Henry I. Her son Alfonso transferred her body to Seville, where that of her husband rested.
2 CONT
2 CONT References: Charles Cawley, Medieval Lands, Castile 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT See http://trees.ancestry.com/tree/25067072/person/12794562611
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT  Decimo Septima generacion de "De La Cerda" (17 De La Cerda) 
2 CONT
2 CONT El primero de este linaje fue Fernando de La Cerda (¿?, 1275 - ¿?, 1 de junio de 1322). Hijo primogenito de Alfonso de Borgoña,(Alfonso X  "el sabio" Rey de Castilla y Leon) y este ultimo es bisnieto de Federico I de Hohenstaufen 
2 CONC (Friedrich I, en alemán), llamado Barbarroja por el color de su barba (Barbarossa, en italiano, Rotbart, en alemán); Cerca de Ravensburg, 1122 – Río Saleph, 10 de junio de 1190) fue desde 1147 duque de Suabia con el nombre de Fede
2 CONC rico III, desde 1152 Rey de los Romanos y a partir de 1155 emperador del Sacro Imperio Romano Germánico.
2 CONT
