2 CONT https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bagratuni_family_tree
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.m.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Bagratuni_dynasty&wprov=rarw1
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT The name "Bagratuni" derives from Bagarat, a Parthian variant of the Old Iranian name Bagadata ("God-given").[10] Historian Cyril Toumanoff speculated that a general of King Tigranes II of Armenia (r. 95 – 55 BCE) named Bagadates 
2 CONC may have been the earliest known member of the Bagratuni family,[11] which first emerged as nakharars—members of the hereditary nobility of Armenia—in the early 4th century.[9] The Arshakuni (Arsacid) dynasty, which ruled Armenia 
2 CONC from 52 to 428, granted the family heritable rights. The first Bagratuni prince identified by Toumanoff, Smbat I, lived at the time of the Armenian conversion to Christianity (c. 314).[12] Starting with Smbat, the Bagratunis held 
2 CONC the hereditary titles of aspet, meaning "Master of the Horse" or the commander of the cavalry (although this appears to have been purely ceremonial and not an actual military command), and tagadir, which indicated their privilege 
2 CONC of crowning Arshakuni kings upon their accession to the throne.[13] Their domain included the region of Sper in the Çoruh River valley of Upper Armenia, which was famous for its gold and silver, and Tayk. The medieval Armenian his
2 CONC torian Movses Khorenatsi claimed they had an ancestor, Smbat, who came to Armenia from Judea in 6th century BCE, but modern historians regard this as an invention to give a biblical origin to the family.[14] Toumanoff proposed ins
2 CONC tead that the Bagratunis were descended from the Orontids, the first identifiable ruling dynasty of ancient Armenia.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the 7th-century Arab conquest of Armenia, members of the Bagratuni house often held the title of ishkhan (prince) of Armenia, although they were subordinate to a Muslim governor (ostikan) appointed by the caliphs.[3] The per
2 CONC iod of Arab rule in Armenia saw the decline of the power of the Mamikonians at the same time as the Bagratunis gained in prominence, as the Muslim governors favored the latter.[3] During the collapse of Umayyad rule in 748, the Ba
2 CONC gratuni ishkhan Ashot III reluctantly joined with the other Armenian nobles in a revolt against Arab rule. Ashot was blinded on the orders of Grigor Mamikonian after trying to withdraw from the rebellion, which failed after Grigor
2 CONC 's death in 749. Ashot "the Blind" was restored to nominal rule as ishkhan after the Abbasids reestablished Arab rule of Armenia in 750.[3] In 774-775 sparapet Smbat VII Bagratuni led the Armenian nobility in an unsuccessful revol
2 CONC t against the Abbasid Caliphate, although part of the Bagratuni house opposed the rebellion.[15] Smbat was killed at the Battle of Bagrevand along with Mushegh Mamikonian
2 CONT and many other Armenian nobles. After the failed rebellion, the Bagratunis lost control of their domains of Tmorik, Kogovit and their possessions in Vaspurakan, although their losses were less severe than those of the other Armeni
2 CONC an noble families.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT Smbat VII's son Ashot Msaker restored the fortunes of the dynasty in the 9th century by waging war against the local Arab emir while remaining loyal to the Abbasid caliphs. Ashot Msaker (re-)acquired a number of territories for th
2 CONC e Bagratuni family, which were divided[by whom?] between two of his sons: Bagrat II, who received Taron and Sasun along with the new title "Prince of Princes" (ishkhan ishkhanats), and Smbat "the Confessor", who received the title
2 CONC  sparapet and the holdings of Sper and Tayk.[3] Meanwhile, Ashot Msaker's uncle, Vasak, established himself in the Georgian region of Iberia; Vasak's grandson Ashot I became the first ruler of Iberia from the Bagratuni dynasty c. 
2 CONC 813. This branch of the dynasty would rule as kings of Georgia for centuries as the Bagrationis.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT Bagratuni Origins
2 CONT History / Պատմություն
2 CONT What origin theories have you heard? I'm really curious.
2 CONT
2 CONT Movses Khorenatsi's claimed that they're the descendants of Bagrad (a jewish noble) who was ennobled by an Arsacid Armenian King who might have been the brother of Mithridates I of Parthia. But unattested elsewhere. But most moder
2 CONC n Armenian historians disagree with this account.
2 CONT
2 CONT These are all the other theories I could find on the Armenian Bagratuni wikipedia. Did I miss anything?
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT Gine de Bagratuni
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouses, children, grandchildren and great-grandchildren
2 CONT Married to sosa Gagik (II ou III) BAGRATUN, D'Arménie, deceased about 1079 (Parents : M sosa Ashot IV BAGRATUN , D'Arménie †ca 1040 &  F sosa ? ?) with
2 CONT F sosa Eudoxia BAGRATUN , D'Arménie  Married to sosa Reupen Ier `the Great' (King) d'ARMENIA ,ou Roupen (Rupin Ruben) Ier PAKRADUNI; Prince de CILICIA; Lord/Prince de GOBIDAR (Kopitar) & Gorosomol; eponym of RUBENID, D'Arménie †ca
2 CONC  1092 with
2 CONT M sosa Constantine Ier (King) d'ARMENIA ,ou Konstandin (Gosdantin) ROUPONI, Konstantine RUBENID; Prince de CILICIA; Lord de VAGHKA & Partzerpert, D'Arménie †1102 Married to sosa Phocas Ne with :
