1 NOTE In 1393 the Turks conquered Turnovo, the capital of Bulgaria, and took Shishman to Philippopolis or Plovdiv, where he was executed in 1395.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Stratsimir succeeded his father,  Ivan Alexander, to power in the 2nd Kingdom of Bulgaria in 1371.  During his father's lifetime, Bulgaria was divided into two provinces.  Ivan Stratsimir was given Vidin and Northwestern Bulg
2 CONC aria to rule.   Ivan Stratsimir ruled until 1395, when the Turkish (Ottoman) Empire took over from 1396 to 1878. This ended the 2nd Kingdom of Bulgaria.  Modern Bulgaria began in 1879 with Alexander of Battenberg.
2 CONT Ivan Stratsimir's domain was continuiously attacked by the Magyars, and he was forced to fight the Turkish penetration into the Balkans. In 1393 the Turks captured the capital of Turnovo, and took Shishman to Philippopolis (Plovdi
2 CONC v) where he was executed in 1395.  Many Bulgarians then fled to Russia, Romania, and Serbia.
1 NOTE Ivan III Shishman ruled central Bulgaria beginning in 1371, during his father's lifetime.  His reign, as part of the 2nd Kingdom of Bulgaria, ended in 1393.
