1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/JERUSALEM%20NOBILITY.htm#JulienSidondied1275
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         '''JULIEN''' (-[12 Jan 1275/11 Jan 1276]).  The Lignages d'Outremer name "Gille…et Julien" as the two sons of "Bellin…seignor de Seete" & his wife, specifying that Julien was "seignor de Seete"[988].  Lord of Sidon and 
2 CONC Beaufort.  He pledged Sidon to the Templars in return for substantial loans.  "Julien dominus de Saette et Biaufort" sold property to the Knights Hospitallers, with the consent of "uxoris Femiæ", by charter dated Aug 1254[989].  H
2 CONC e attacked the Mongols after their conquest of Damascus in 1260, but Sidon was ravaged in revenge.  The Templars took advantage of the situation by foreclosing on Sidon and Beaufort[990].  The Chronicle attributed to King Hethum I
2 CONC I records that "Julian lord of Sidon sold Danblur, Sidon and Piafort" in [15 Jan 1260/14 Jan 1261][991].  The Chronicle attributed to King Hethum II records that "Julian lord of Sidon died" in [12 Jan 1275/11 Jan 1276][992].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (1252, '''divorced''' 1263[993] ) EUPHEMIE of Armenia, daughter of HETHUM I King of Armenia & his wife Zabel Queen of Armenia (-1309, bur [Trazarg]).  The Lignages d'Outremer name (in order) "Sebile, Femie, Ritta, Ysabeau 
2 CONC et Marie" as the five daughters of "Heiton le fis Constans qui estoit conestable et baill d'Ermenie" & his wife, specifying that "Femie" married "Julien le sire de Saiete"[994].  The contract between "Haitum…rois d'Ermenie é la ge
2 CONC ntile dame de Sayete, dame Margarite" relating to the marriage between "le gentil seignor de Sayete sire Yulian" and "notre filie demoiselle Fémie" is dated 1252[995].  William of Tyre (Continuator) records the marriage of "Julian
2 CONC  sire de Saiete" with "la fille de Heton roi d'Ermenie" in 1252[996].  The Chronicle attributed to King Hethum II records the marriage of "Fimi daughter of the Armenian king Hetum" and "Julian king of France"[997].  The Chronicle 
2 CONC of Amadi records the marriage of "messer Juliano signor di Saeto" and "la figlia del re Haetonte de Armenia", in 1252 from the context[998].  "Julien dominus de Saette et Biaufort" sold property to the Knights Hospitallers, with t
2 CONC he consent of "uxoris Femiæ", by charter dated Aug 1254[999].  She became abbess of Notre Dame de Tyr at Nicosia in 1308[1000].  The Chronicle of Amadi names "madama Sur Fimia, sua ameda…monacha de la Nostra Donna mazore de Hierus
2 CONC alem, che si dice in Cypro Nostra Dame de Sur…signora de Saeto" and "suo nepote messer Alinac, che ottenne dal suo fratello messer Choyssin re de Armenia", specifying that she was "sorella del re Livon et ava della moglie del…mess
2 CONC er Philippo de Iblim el sinescalco, la qual era figlia de la sua figlia"[1001].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mistress''': ([1256/61]) PLAISANCE of Antioch, 
2 CONT widow of HENRI I King of Cyprus, 
2 CONT separated wife of BALIAN Ibelin Lord of Arsur, 
2 CONT daughter of BOHEMOND V Prince of Antioch & his second wife Lucia di Caccamo-Segni ([1237/38]-[22/27] Sep 1261).  Her relationship with Julien of Sidon provoked a Papal Bull urging her to remarry[1002].  According to Runciman[1003]
2 CONC , the mistress of Julien of Sidon was the daughter-in-law of Plaisance, Isabelle Ibelin, but Rüdt-Collenberg attributes the Papal Bull to Pope Urban IV in 1261, well before the time when Isabelle Ibelin could have been involved. 
