1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Maryam Begum (Persian: مریم بیگم‎) was a Safavid princess, who was a daughter of shah Tahmasp I (r. 1524–1576) and in 1577 married the Kia'i ruler Khan Ahmad Khan—they later had an unnamed son and daughter named Ya
2 CONC khan Begum. Maryam Begum died in 1608/9. 
2 CONT ======
2 CONT When Tahmasp I Safavi died in 1576, Ismail, his son, was freed by his Qizilbash supporters, who shortly killed his brother Haydar Mirza Safavi, due to his claim to the throne. They thereafter crowned him as the new shah. After his
2 CONC  accession, he ordered 
2 CONT =Khan Ahmad Khan Gilani=
2 CONT to be released, an order, which, however, was not executed by his men. Ismail II was assassinated the following year, and was succeeded by his older but weaker brother Mohammad Khodabanda, who, at the proposal of his queen Khayr a
2 CONC l-Nisa Begum (who was a close relative of Khan Ahmad Khan) had Khan Ahmad Khan released.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was thereafter taken to Qazvin, where Mohammad Khodabanda gave him one of his sisters (Maryam Begum) in marriage and restored him as the ruler of Bia-pish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Maryam Sultan Begum (1551 – Isfahan, 1608), married firstly Khan Ahmad Khan governor of Gilan, m. (second) as his second wife, Mir Nematu’llah Yazdi Zu’l-Nurain, eldest son of Amir Ghiyas ed-din Muhammad Yazdi Mir-i-Miran
2 CONC , having had issue one daughter and one son, by her first husband.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Panah Ali's paternal great grandfather and namesake Panah Ali bey served at the court of Ganja beylerbeys (governors) in the early 17th century, at the time when the region's was directly controlled by the Safavid Empire of Iran. 
2 CONC He soon retired, married a woman from the Javanshir clan of Karabakh and had a son by the name of Ali (nicknamed Sarija Ali). They lived in their estate located in Arasbar (Arasbaran) (present-day Khojavend and Agdam rayons of Aze
2 CONC rbaijan) but also owned land in Tartar and the northern shores of the Aras River. The Arasbar estate was rebuilt into a castle in Sarija Ali's son Ibrahim Khalil's lifetime and has been known as Ibrahim Khalil Galasi since.
