1 NOTE {geni:about_me} In 1496 inherited all property from his maternal grandmother princess Maryna Trabska-Holshanska-Drutska (ML. 2 and 4).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albertas_Go%C5%A1tautas Albertas Goštautas] (Latin: Albertus Gastold, Polish: Olbracht Gasztołd, Belarusian: Альберт Гаштольд) (c. 1480 – 1539) was a Lithuanian noble of the Goštautai family from eth
2 CONC nic Lithuanian lands of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania. Voivode of Navahrudak since 1508, Voivode of Polotsk since 1514, Voivode of Trakai since 1519 and Voivode of Vilnius since 1522. In 1522 he became Grand Chancellor of Lithuania
2 CONC . He was the initiator and the editor of the First Statute of Lithuania, as a successor of his staunch opponent Mikolaj Radziwiłł, who rivaled him in the precedence in the Council of Lords. His subsequent rival in influence in the
2 CONC  Grand Duchy was Konstanty Ostrogski.
2 CONT
2 CONT Albertas was a son of Martynas Goštautas and father of Stanislovas Goštautas, last male heir of the Goštautai family. He was buried in Vilnius Cathedral, where his tomb remains till present day. He built the Hieraniony Castle (rui
2 CONC ns survive in present-day Belarus).
2 CONT
2 CONT It is thought that Albertas Goštautas, as well as the rest of Goštautai family members, had retained their native Lithuanian language. Influenced by the ideas of the Protestant Reformation, Goštautas was a supporter of the Lithuan
2 CONC ian culture and language in state affairs and had a nationalistic attitude: he segregated non-Lithuanian and Polish-speaking Franciscans, took care of the representatives of Lithuanian literature, such as Abraomas Kulvietis, and s
2 CONC howed distrust to Ruthenian inhabitants of the Grand Duchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is one of the characters on the famous painting by Jan Matejko, Prussian Homage.
2 CONT
