1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maria_Temrjukovna
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Temrjukovna (Мари́я Темрюко́вна, c. 1544 – 1 September 1569) was a Circassian Tsaritsa of the Tsardom of Russia and second wife to Ivan IV of Russia.
2 CONT
2 CONT The daughter of Muslim prince Temrjuk of Kabardia, Maria (originally named Kučenej (Кученей) before her baptism) was presented to Ivan in Moscow after the death of his first wife Anastasia Romanovna. Russian folklore tells of how 
2 CONC Ivan's first wife, before dying, warned him not to take a pagan as a wife. Ivan was so smitten by Maria's beauty, that he decided to marry her immediately. On 21 August 1561, they married, four days before Ivans 31st birthday. Iva
2 CONC n soon came to regret the decision to marry her, on account of his new wife being viewed as illiterate and vindictive. She never fully integrated to the Muscovite way of life, and was considered a poor stepmother to Ivan's two son
2 CONC s Ivan and Feodor. She gave birth to a son named Vasili, named after her father in law on 21 March 1563, though he died on 3 May that same year. Maria was generally hated by her subjects, who believed her to be manipulative and wi
2 CONC tch-like in her behaviour. Some historians[who?] write that it was she who first incited her husband to form the oprichniki. She died on 1 September 1569 at the age of 25. It was widely believed[who?] that she had been poisoned by
2 CONC  her own husband. The Tsar never admitted as such, and had many people tortured on suspicion of assassinating the Tsaritsa.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The daughter of Temryuk (Timur or Teymour) of Kabardia, Maria (originally named Qochenay bint Teymour (Кученей) before her baptism) was presented to Ivan in Moscow after the death of his first wife Anastasia Romanovna. Russian fol
2 CONC klore tells of how Ivan's first wife, before dying, warned him not to take a pagan as a wife. Ivan was so smitten by Maria's beauty, that he decided to marry her immediately. On 21 August 1561, they married, four days before Ivan'
2 CONC s 31st birthday.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan soon came to regret the decision to marry her, on account of his new wife being viewed as illiterate and vindictive. She never fully integrated to the Muscovite way of life, and was considered a poor stepmother to Ivan's two 
2 CONC sons Ivan and Feodor. She gave birth to a son named Vasili, named after her father-in-law on 21 March 1563, though he died on 3 May that same year. Maria was generally hated by her subjects, who believed her to be manipulative and
2 CONC  witch-like in her behaviour. Some historians [1] write that it was she who first incited her husband to form the oprichniki.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died on 1 September 1569 at the age of 25. It was rumored that she had been poisoned by her own husband. The Tsar never admitted as such, and had many people tortured on suspicion of assassinating the Tsaritsa.
