2 CONT
2 CONT His genealogical tree is not well documented. It was first outlined by Karl Hopf in his Chroniques Greco-Romanes (p. 531) and by K. Sathas in the 19th century but a newer study finds that those works have many mistakes and gaps.[1
2 CONC 9] Hopf's genealogy of the Shpata family is "altogether inaccurate".[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT G. Schiró studied the genealogy of Shpata based on the original sources, i.e. the "Chronicle of Ioannina" and the "Chronicle of Tocco", but also on the Venetian archives. He proposed that Pietro Bua had not only three sons but fou
2 CONC r and that John had only daughters. His daughter Irene married three times. He believes that the family was extinct with the death of Jakob in 1416. Other people, mainly condottieri, with the name "Bua" are not blood relatives of 
2 CONC this family but this name was used by many as first name since it became famous.[21] However this is refuted by modern scholars, Gjin Bua Shpata was a member of Bua tribe and founder of Shpata family.[22][23]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to a woman who is unknown in the historical record.[24] His daughter, Irene was married (before April 1381 ) to a Marchesano of Naples, Morean baron, baillie of Achaea[25] and Esau de' Buondelmonti in 1396.[20] Esau
2 CONC  was the Despot of Ioannina. Another unnamed daughter was married to Gjon Zenebishi.[26]
2 CONT
2 CONT Among his grandchildren were brothers Muriq Shpata and Jakob Bua Shpata, who claimed to have been sons of Eirene.
