1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Zenevisi '''John Zenevisi de Sarbissa'''] (Albanian: Gjon Zenebishi; Italian: Giovanni Sarbissa, died 1418) was an Albanian magnate that held the estates in [https://en.wikipedia
2 CONC .org/wiki/Epirus Epirus], such as [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gjirokast%C3%ABr Argyrokastro (Gjirokastër)] and [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vagenetia Vagenetia].
2 CONT Zenevisi can be found with different spellings in historical documents. His name in modern English is usually John Zenevisi[1][2] or John Sarbissa.[2] In Italian, his name was spelled as Giovanni Sarbissa.[3] In Albanian, his name
2 CONC  is mostly spelled as Gjin Zenebishi (less commonly as Zenebishti), his given name scarcely spelled Gjon , as well. (''vidi zlatar u Zagrebu Gjon Gjoni,op.aut.'') Castle of Gjirokastër was Gjon Zenebishi's capital
2 CONT John was born into the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zenevisi_family Zenebishi family] which was a wealthy and noble [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albanian_tribes Albanian tribe] family from the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Za
2 CONC gori Zagoria region], between [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P%C3%ABrmet Përmet] and [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gjirokast%C3%ABr Gjirokastër].[4] He was one of the most notable members of this family. After the Ottomans captur
2 CONC ed the region of Epirus, some members of the family fled to [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morea Morea], while other members held high positions within the Ottoman hierarchy.
2 CONT In 1381 and 1384, the Catholic lords of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arta,_Greece Arta] asked the Ottoman troops for protection against the invading Albanian clan of the Zenebishi; the Ottomans routed the raiders and restored or
2 CONC der in Epirus.[5] Zenebishi submitted to the Ottomans after their victory against [https://albanopedia.com/biographies/balsha-ii Balsha II] https://www.geni.com/people/Bal%C5%A1a-II-Bal%C5%A1i%C4%87/6000000064327665954 in the Batt
2 CONC le of Savra in 1385, and gave them his son as a hostage to be sent to Edirne to the court of the sultan (this son became known as Hamza, an Ottoman official).[6] Shortly after his submission, Zenebishi revolted and seized the fort
2 CONC ress of Gjirokastër, encouraged no doubt by the attack on [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ioannina Ioannina] by the Albanians of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acarnania Acarnania]. In 1386 he titled himself with the Byzantine titl
2 CONC e of sevastokrator.[7]
2 CONT Zenebishi was married Irene,(wrong;to UKNOWN name daughter ;op.aut) the daughter of Gjin Bua Shpata, Despot of Arta, and thus became the son-in-law of Shpata and the brother-in-law of the wife of [https://www.geni.com/people/Esa%C
2 CONC 3%B9-de-Buondelmonti/6000000010112989034 Esau de' Buondelmonti Despot of Epiros]. In 1399 Esau, supported by some Albanian clans, marched against his wife's brother-in-law Gjon Zenebishi of Gjirokastër. Now Esau was routed and cap
2 CONC tured, and much of his land was occupied by Zenebishi. The neighbouring magnates determined to restore the captured despotes and secured Venetian intercession in his favour. Esau returned to Ioannina in 1400, regaining the reign f
2 CONC rom Zenebishi. In 1402, Esau divorced [https://www.geni.com/people/Irene-Bua-Spata/6000000008389575863 Irene Shpata] and married https://www.geni.com/people/Eudokia-Bal%C5%A1i%C4%87/6000000010112931167, the sister of https://www.g
2 CONC eni.com/people/Konstantin-Bal%C5%A1i%C4%87/6000000035610956909, a leading Ottoman official in northern Albania.[1] After Esau's death (February 6, 1411), his wife Jevdokija tried to take control of Ioannina, but the town exiled he
2 CONC r and appointed Esau's nephew, [https://www.geni.com/people/Carlo-II-di-Tocco-conte-di-Zante/6000000010669307041 Carlo Tocco], as lord (he arrived on April 1, 1411)
