1 OCCU Prins, co-Empereur, de Byzance, доместик на схолите, was a protovestiarios and protoproedros of the Byzantine Empire, general under Romanos IV
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00139083&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andronikos_Doukas_(general_under_Romanos_IV)
2 CONT
2 CONT =Andronikos Doukas=
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos Doukas or Andronicus Ducas (Greek: Ανδρόνικος Δούκας), (d. 14 October 1077) was a protovestiarios and protoproedros of the Byzantine Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos Doukas was son of the Caesar John Doukas and Eirene Pegonitissa. His father was a brother of Emperor Constantine X Doukas. His maternal grandfather was Niketas Pegonites. Andronikos himself was a first cousin of Michael
2 CONC  VII Doukas.
2 CONT In 1071 Andronikos was the commander of a section of the Byzantine army in the campaign of Romanos IV Diogenes against the Seljuk Turks of Alp Arslan. Commanding the rearguard of the army during the Battle of Manzikert, Andronikos
2 CONC  announced that the emperor had been cut down and deserted from the battlefield. He was widely blamed for causing the crushing defeat of the Byzantine forces and the subsequent capture of Romanos IV by the enemy. In 1072, after Ro
2 CONC manos had been released by Alp Arslan, Andronikos and his brother Constantine were sent out by Michael VII and their father the Caesar John to intercept him. They defeated Romanos and hunted him down in Cilicia. It was Andronikos 
2 CONC who finally obtained Romanos' surrender and conducted him towards Constantinople. In spite of his former hatred for the deposed emperor, Andronikos is said to have opposed his blinding on June 29, 1072. In 1074, together with his 
2 CONC father, Andronikos commanded the imperial army against the rebel mercenaries led by Roussel de Bailleul. Both were captured by the rebels, who released the badly wounded Andronikos to allow him to seek proper medical treatment in 
2 CONC Constantinople. There he recovered for a few years, but in October 1077 died of an edema.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos Doukas married Maria of Bulgaria, daughter of Troian. Troian was a son of Emperor Ivan Vladislav of Bulgaria. They had at least five children:
2 CONT # Michael Doukas.
2 CONT # John Doukas.
2 CONT # Irene Doukaina, who married Emperor Alexios I Komnenos.
2 CONT # Anna Doukaina, who married George Palaiologos.
2 CONT # Theodora Doukaina, a nun.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Andronikos Doukas, Latinized as Andronicus Ducas, (Greek: Ανδρόνικος Δούκας; died 14 October 1077) was a protovestiarios and protoproedros of the Byzantine Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos Doukas was son of the Caesar John Doukas and Eirene Pegonitissa. His father was a brother of Emperor Constantine X Doukas. His maternal grandfather was Niketas Pegonites. Andronikos himself was a first cousin of Michael
2 CONC  VII Doukas.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1071 Andronikos was the commander of a section of the Byzantine army in the campaign of Romanos IV Diogenes against the Seljuk Turks of Alp Arslan. Commanding the rearguard of the army during the Battle of Manzikert, Andronikos
2 CONC  announced that the emperor had been cut down and deserted from the battlefield. He was widely blamed for causing the crushing defeat of the Byzantine forces and the subsequent capture of Romanos IV by the enemy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1072, after Romanos had been released by Alp Arslan, Andronikos and his brother Constantine were sent out by Michael VII and their father the Caesar John to intercept him. They defeated Romanos and hunted him down in Cilicia. I
2 CONC t was Andronikos who finally obtained Romanos' surrender and conducted him towards Constantinople. In spite of his former hatred for the deposed emperor, Andronikos is said to have opposed his blinding on 29 June 1072.
2 CONT
2 CONT In an act of 1073, he is recorded with his titles as protoproedros, protovestiarios and megas domestikos, which Michael Attaleiates clarifies as being the post of domestikos ton scholon of the East, which he had been given when se
2 CONC nt against Diogenes.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1074, together with his father, Andronikos commanded the imperial army against the rebel mercenaries led by Roussel de Bailleul. Both were captured by the rebels, who released the badly wounded Andronikos to allow him to seek p
2 CONC roper medical treatment in Constantinople. There he recovered for a few years, but in October 1077 died of an edema.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos Doukas married Maria of Bulgaria, daughter of Troian, a son of Emperor Ivan Vladislav of Bulgaria. They had at least five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael Doukas, protostrator.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Doukas, megas doux.
2 CONT
2 CONT Irene Doukaina, who married Emperor Alexios I Komnenos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Doukaina, who married George Palaiologos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodora Doukaina, a nun.
