2 CONT From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on Heinrich II Jasomirgott von Osterreich (Source / Forrás):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p11405.htm#i114044
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich II Jasomirgott Herzog von Österreich ▼1
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #114044, b. 1114, d. 1177
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=16 Jul 2005
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich II Jasomirgott Herzog von Österreich was born in 1114. ▼1 He was the son of Leopold III 'the Saint' Markgraf von Österreich and Agnes Salian. ▼1 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, firstly, Gertrude von Sachsen, daughter of Lothair II von Sachsen, Holy Roman Emperor and Richenza von Sachsen, on 1 May 1142. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, secondly, Theodora Comnene, daughter of Andronikos Comnenos, in 1149. ▼1 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1177. ▼1
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich II Jasomirgott Herzog von Österreich succeeded to the title of Markgraf von Österreich in 1141. ▼1 He succeeded to the title of Herzog von Bayern in 1143. ▼2 He was deposed as Duke of Bavaria in 1156. ▼2 He was created He
2 CONC rzog von Österreich in 1156. ▼1
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Heinrich II Jasomirgott Herzog von Österreich and Theodora Comnene
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. Agnes Babenberg ▼1 b. 1154, d. 1182
2 CONT
2 CONT -2. Leopold V Herzog von Österreich+ ▼1 b. 1157, d. 1194
2 CONT
2 CONT -3. Heinrich Babenberg, Duke of Mödling+ ▼1 b. 1158, d.  1233
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. [S16] Jirí Louda and Michael MacLagan, Lines of Succession: Heraldry of the Royal Families of Europe, 2nd edition (London, U.K.: Little, Brown and Company, 1999), table 77. Hereinafter cited as Lines of Succession.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. [S38] John Morby, Dynasties of the World: a chronological and genealogical handbook (Oxford, Oxfordshire, U.K.: Oxford University Press, 1989), page 127. Hereinafter cited as Dynasties of the World.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Henry II, Duke of Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich (Henry) II, (1107 – January 13, 1177), Count Palatine of the Rhine, 1140-1141, Margrave of Austria from 1141 to 1156 and, as Heinrich (Henry) XI, also Duke of Bavaria from 1141 to 1156, Duke of Austria, 1156-1177, was a p
2 CONC rince of the Babenberg dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT As the son of Markgrave Leopold III, he first became Count Palatine of the Rhine until being appointed Duke of Bavaria and Margrave of Austria when his brother Leopold IV unexpectedly died.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the course of the dispute between the Welfen and Staufen dynasties in the Holy Roman Empire, the duchy of Bavaria had been taken away from the Welf Henry the Proud by the emperor and given to the Babenberg dynasty. The new Empe
2 CONC ror Friedrich I tried to reach a compromise with the Welfs and endowed the son of Henry the Proud, Henry the Lion, with Bavaria in 1156. A replacement had to be found for the Babenberg family, namely the Privilegium Minus, by whic
2 CONC h Austria was elevated to a duchy and gained complete independence from Bavaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other than his father, who resided in Klosterneuburg for most of the time, Henry moved his residence to Vienna in 1145. Only by this act could the modern Austrian capital surpass cities such as Krems, Melk or Klosterneuburg. Since
2 CONC  then, it has remained the capital of the country. Also in 1147, St. Stephen's Cathedral was completed, which became a visible landmark of the city, showing its prominence. In 1155, Henry founded the Schottenstift monastery in Vie
2 CONC nna, in the courtyard of which a statue of him stands to this day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Until 1143, he was married to Gertrude of Süpplingenburg, the daughter of Emperor Lothar II. In 1148 he married Theodora Comnena, a niece of the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I. Both marriages strongly show the importance of the House 
2 CONC of Babenberg in Central Europe in that period.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry's brother was the important chronicler Otto of Freising. His sister Judith was the wife of William V of Montferrat.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry's nickname, Jasomirgott, was first documented during the 13th century in the form of Jochsamergott, the meaning of which is unclear. According to one theory, it is derived from an Arab word bearing a connection to the Second
2 CONC  Crusade where Henry participated in 1146. According to a popular etymology, it is derived from the formula "Ja so mir Gott helfe" (meaning: "Yes, God willing").
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry II, Duke of Austria, House of Babenberg
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 1107 Died: 1177
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke of Bavaria 1141–1156	
2 CONT *Preceded by Leopold IV	
2 CONT *Succeeded by Henry XII
2 CONT
2 CONT Margrave, then Duke of Austria 1141–1177	
2 CONT *Preceded by Leopold IV	
2 CONT *Succeeded by Leopold V
2 CONT
2 CONT Count Palatine of the Rhine 1140–1141	
2 CONT *Preceded by William of Ballerstedt	
2 CONT *Succeeded by Herman III of Stahleck
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Norwegian text, appears unattributed:
2 CONT
2 CONT Markgreve av Østerrike 1141-1156.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hertug av Østerrike 1156-1177.
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich ble også hertug av Bayern, men måtte senere avstå dette til Henrik Löwe. Til gjengjeld fikk han avstått til seg det nåværende Øvre Østerrike og begge sine land forenet til Hertugdømmet Østerrike, utskilt fra den tidligere
2 CONC  avhengighet av Bayern. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Han forla sitt fyrstesete til Wien og påbegynte bygningen av Stefanskirken.
2 CONT
2 CONT I 1147 deltok han i det annet store korstog.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han var første gang gift 01.05.1142 med Gjertrud, datter til keiser Lothar III. Hun døde 18.04.1143.
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich døde da han falt av hesten i 1177.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tekst: Tore Nygaard
2 CONT
2 CONT Kilder:
2 CONT
2 CONT Erich Brandenburg: Die Nachkommen Karls des Grossen, Leipzig 1935. Allgemeine deutsche Biographie. Mogens Bugge: Våre forfedre, nr. 1128. Bent og Vidar Billing Hansen: Rosensverdslektens forfedre, side 23, 31. 
2 CONT --------------------
