2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: He conquered Cordoba & Seville from the Moors. He and Edward I 'Longshanks' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Plantagenet, King of England
2 CONT
2 CONT History: Ferdinand III (of Castile and León), called The Saint (1199-1252), king of Castile (1217-52) and of León (1230-52); was the son of King Alfonso IX of León and Castile. In 1217 Ferdinand's mother, Berengaria, renounced her
2 CONC  title to the Castilian throne in favor of her son. Alfonso, who had himself expected to acquire Castile, was angered at his wife's action, and, aided by a group of Castilian nobles favorable to his claim, made war upon his newly 
2 CONC crowned son. Ferdinand, however, with the wise counsel of his mother, proved more than a military match for Alfonso, who at length was forced to abandon his plan of conquering Castile. Through the good offices of Berengaria, Ferdi
2 CONC nand was able to effect the peaceful union of León and Castile upon the death of his father in 1230. Ferdinand devoted his energies to prosecuting the war against the Moors, conquering Córdoba in 1236 and Seville in 1248. He was r
2 CONC igorous in his suppression of the heretical Albigenses, a fact largely responsible for his canonization more than two centuries later. In 1242 Ferdinand reestablished at Salamanca, the university originally founded by his grandfat
2 CONC her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Microsoft® Encarta® Encyclopedia 2002. © 1993-2001 Microsoft Corporation. All rights reserved.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT  Durante su reinado se unificaron definitivamente las coronas de Castilla y León, que habían permanecido divididas desde la época de Alfonso VII el Emperador, quien a su muerte las repartió entre sus hijos, los infantes Fernando y
2 CONC  Sancho.
2 CONT
2 CONT Durante su reinado fueron conquistadas y arrebatadas a los musulmanes, en el marco de la Reconquista, entre otras plazas, las ciudades de Córdoba, Sevilla, Jaén y Murcia, obligando con ello a retroceder a los reinos musulmanes, qu
2 CONC e, al finalizar el reinado de Fernando III el Santo, únicamente poseían en la Península Ibérica las actuales provincias de Huelva, Cádiz, Málaga, Granada y Almería.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fue canonizado en 1671, siendo papa Clemente X, y reinando en España Carlos II.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Alfonso III of Leon and Galicia (August 15, 1171 – September 23 or 24, 1230), first cousin of Alfonso VIII of Castile and numbered next to him as being a junior member of the family, was the king of León from the death of his fath
2 CONC er Ferdinand II in 1188 until his own death. According to Ibn Khaldun, he is said to have been called the Baboso or Slobberer because he was subject to fits of rage during which he foamed at the mouth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso was the only son of King Ferdinand II of León and Urraca of Portugal. Though he took a part in the work of the reconquest, this king is chiefly remembered for the difficulties into which his successive marriages led him wi
2 CONC th the Pope. He was first married in 1191 to his cousin Teresa of Portugal, who bore him two daughters, and a son who died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Eleanor, Queen of Castile & Princess of England. The marriage was declared null by the Pope; however, Alfonso paid no attention until he was presumably tired of his wife. His next step was to marry his second cousin, Be
2 CONC renguela of Castile, in 1197. For this act of contumacy, the king and the kingdom were placed under interdict.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Pope was, however, compelled to modify his measures by the threat that, if the people could not obtain the services of religion, they would not support the clergy, and that heresy would spread. The king was left under interdic
2 CONC t personally, but to that he showed himself indifferent, and he had the support of his clergy. Eleanor left him after the birth of five children, and the king then returned to Teresa, to whose daughters he left his kingdom in his 
2 CONC will.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso's children by Teresa of Portugal were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando (ca. 1192-August 1214), unmarried and without issue 
2 CONT
2 CONT Blessed Sancha (ca. 1193-1270) Dulce, also called Aldonza (1194/ca. 1195-ca./aft. 1243), unmarried and without issue 
2 CONT
2 CONT His eldest daughter, Sancha, was engaged to her cousin King Henry I of Castile, but Henry died in 1217 before the marriage could be solemnized. Wanting to disinherit his eldest son, Fernando, King Alfonso invited John of Brienne t
2 CONC o marry his daughter Sancha and thus inherit the Leonese throne. However, Queen Berenguela convinced John of Brienne to marry one of her daughters instead. Though she was the nominal heiress on her father's death in 1230, Sancha w
2 CONC as easily set aside by Berenguela and Fernando. Sancha became a nun at Cozollos, where she died in 1270; she was later beatified. Her sister Dulce-Aldonza spent her life with their mother in Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso's children by Berenguela of Castile were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonor (1198/1199-October 31, 1210) 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Fernando III the Saint (1200-1252) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso, 4th Lord of Molina (1203-1272) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Berenguela of Leon (1204-1237), married John of Brienne 
2 CONT
2 CONT Constanza (May 1, 1200 or 1205-September 7, 1242), became a nun at Las Huelgas, Burgos, where she died 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso also fathered many illegitimate children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso's children by Aldonza Martínez da Silva (daughter of Martim Gomes da Silva & Urraca Rodrigues and subsequently wife with issue of Diego Froilaz, Conde de Cifuentes, had issue):
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro Alfonso of León, 1st Lord of Tenorio (ca. 1196/ca. 1200-1226), Grand Master of Santiago, married N de Villarmayor, and had issue 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso Alfonso of León, died yong 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando Alfonso of León, died young 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rodrigo Alfonso of León (ca. 1210-ca. 1267), 1st Lord of Aliger and Governor of Zamora, married ca. 1240 to Inés Rodriguez de Cabrera (ca. 1200-), and had issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Teresa Alfonso of León (ca. 1210-), wife of Nuno Gonzalez de Lara, el Bueno, señor de Lara 
2 CONT
2 CONT Aldonza Alonso of León (ca. 1212/ca. 1215-1266), wife of Diego Ramírez Froilaz, nephew of her stepfather, without issue, and of Pedro Ponce de Cabrera (ca. 1210-), and had issue, ancestors of the Ponce de León 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso's child by Inés Iñíguez de Mendoza (ca. 1180-) (daughter of Lope Iñiguez de Mendoza, 1st Lord of Mendoza (ca. 1140-1189) and wife Teresa Ximénez de los Cameros (ca. 1150-)):
2 CONT
2 CONT Urraca Alfonso of León (ca. 1190/ca. 1197-), first wife ca. 1230 of Lopo III Díaz de Haro (1192-December 15, 1236), 11th Sovereign Lord of Viscaya, and had issue 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso's child by Estefánia Pérez de Limia, daughter of Pedro Arias de Limia and wife, subsequently wife of Rodrigo Suárez, Merino mayor of Galicia, had issue):
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando Alfonso of León (ca. 1211-), died young 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso's children by Maua, of unknown origin:
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando Alfonso of León (ca. 1215/1218/1220-Salamanca, 1278/1279), Archdean of Santiago, married to Aldara de Ulloa and had issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso's children by Dona Teresa Gil de Soverosa (ca. 1170-) (daughter of Dom Gil Vasques de Soverosa & first wife Maria Aires de Fornelos):
2 CONT
2 CONT María Alfonso of León (ca. 1190/1200/1222-aft. 1252), married as his second wife Soeiro Aires de Valadares (ca. 1140-) and had issue and Álvaro Fernández de Lara (ca. 1200-) and had female issue, later mistress of her nephew Alfon
2 CONC so X of Castile 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancha Alfonso of León (1210/ca. 1210-1270), a Nun after divorcing without issue Simón Ruíz, Lord of Los Cameros.
2 CONT
2 CONT Martín Alfonso of León (ca. 1210/ca. 1225-1274/ca. 1275) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Urraca Alfonso of León (ca. 1210/1228-aft.1252, married twice, first to García Romeu of Tormos, without issue, then Pedro Núñez de Guzmán 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso's other illegitimate child, mother unknown:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mayor Alfonso de León, married Rodrigo Gómez de Trava, without issue 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso IX was the first King in Western Europe who summoned the citizens to the Parliament (León's Cortes of 1188). He also founded the University of Salamanca in 1208.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ferdinand III of Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT Born July 30 or August 5, 1199, monastery of Valparaíso (Peleas de Arriba, Zamora)
2 CONT
2 CONT Died May 30, 1252, Sevilla, Spain 
2 CONT
2 CONT Venerated in Roman Catholic Church 
2 CONT
2 CONT Canonized 1271, Rome by Pope Clement X 
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Ferdinand III (July 30 or August 5, 1199 – May 30, 1252), was the King of Castile from 1217 and King of León from 1230. Through his second marriage he was also Count of Aumale. He finished the work done by his maternal grand
2 CONC father Alfonso VIII and consolidated the Reconquista. In 1231, he permanently united Castile and León. He was canonized in 1671 and, in Spanish, he is Fernando el Santo or San Fernando.
2 CONT
2 CONT St Ferdinand was the son of Alfonso IX of León and Berenguela of Castile. He was born at the monastery of Valparaíso (Peleas de Arriba, Zamora) in 1198 or 1199. His parents were divorced by order of Pope Innocent III in 1204. Bere
2 CONC nguela took their children, including Ferdinand, to the court of her father. In 1217, her younger brother Henry I died and she succeeded him to the Castilian throne, but she immediately surrendered it to her son Ferdinand, for who
2 CONC m she initially acted as regent. When Alfonso died in 1230, Ferdinand also inherited León, though he had to fight for it with Alfonso's designated heirs, Sancha and Dulce, the daughters of his first wife. He thus became the first 
2 CONC sovereign of both kingdoms since the death of Alfonso VII in 1157.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early in his reign, Ferdinand had to deal with a rebellion of the House of Lara. He also established a permanent border with the Kingdom of Aragon by the Treaty of Almizra (1244).
2 CONT
2 CONT St Ferdinand spent much of his reign fighting the Moors. Through diplomacy and war, exploiting the internal dissensions in the Moorish kingdoms, he triumphed in expanding Castilian power over southern Iberian Peninsula. He capture
2 CONC d the towns of Úbeda in 1233, Córdoba in 1236, Jaén in 1246, and Seville in 1248, and occupied Murcia in 1243, thereby reconquering all Andalusia save Granada, whose king nevertheless did homage to Ferdinand. Ferdinand divided the
2 CONC  conquered territories between the Knights, the Church, and the nobility, whom he endowed with great latifundias. When he took Córdoba, he ordered the Liber Iudiciorum to be adopted and observed by its citizens, and caused it to b
2 CONC e rendered, albeit inaccurately, into Castilian.
2 CONT
2 CONT The capture of Córdoba was the result of an uneven and uncoordinated process whereby parts (the Ajarquía) of the city first fell to the independent almogavars of the Sierra Morena to the north, which Ferdinand had not at the time 
2 CONC subjugated. Only in 1236 did Ferdinand arrive with a royal army to take Medina, the religious and administrative centre of the city. Ferdinand set up a council of partidores to divide the conquests and between 1237 and 1244 a grea
2 CONC t deal of land was parcelled out to private individuals and members of the royal family as well as the Church. On 10 March 1241, Ferdinand established seven outposts to define the boundary of the province of Córdoba.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the domestic front, he strengthened the University of Salamanca and founded the current Cathedral of Burgos. He was a patron of the newest movement in the Church: that of the friars. Whereas the Benedictines and then the Cister
2 CONC cians and Cluniacs had taken a major part in the Reconquista up til then, Ferdinand founded Dominican, Franciscan, Trinitarian, and Mercedarian houses in Andalusia, thus determining the religious future of that region. Ferdinand h
2 CONC as also been credited with sustaining the convivencia in Andalusia.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primera Crónica General de España asserts that, on his death bed, Ferdinand commended his son "you are rich in lands and in many good vassals — more so than any other king in Christendom," probably in recognition of his expans
2 CONC ive conquests. He was buried within the Cathedral of Seville by his son Alfonso X. His tomb is inscribed with four languages: Arabic, Hebrew, Latin, and an early incarnation of Castilian. St Ferdinand was canonized by Pope Clement
2 CONC  X in 1671. Several places named San Fernando were founded across the Spanish Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1219, Ferdinand married Elisabeth of Hohenstaufen (1203–1235), daughter of the German king Philip of Swabia and Irene Angelina. Elisabeth was called Beatriz in Spain. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso X, his successor 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fadrique 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand (1225–1243/1248) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor (born 1227), died young 
2 CONT
2 CONT Berenguela (1228–1288/89), a nun at Las Huelgas 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry 
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip (1231–1274). He was promised to the Church, but was so taken by the beauty of Princess Kristina of Norway, daughter of Haakon IV of Norway, who had been intended as a bride for one of his brothers, that he abandoned his hol
2 CONC y vows and married her. She died in 1262, childless. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho, Archbishop of Toledo and Seville (1233–1261) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan Manuel, Lord of Villena 
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria, died an infant in November 1235 
2 CONT
2 CONT After he widowed, he married Jeanne of Dammartin, Countess of Ponthieu, before August 1237. They had four sons and one daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand (1239–1269), Count of Aumale 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor (c.1241–1290), married Edward I of England 
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis (1243–1269) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Simon (1244), died young and buried in a monastery in Toledo 
2 CONT
2 CONT John (1245), died young and buried at the cathedral in Córdoba 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Portrait of St. Ferdinand III in a 13th century miniature
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Ferdinand III (5 August 1199 – 30 May 1252) was the King of Castile from 1217 and León from 1230. He was the son of Alfonso IX of León and Berenguela of Castile. Through his second marriage he was also Count of Aumale. He fi
2 CONC nished the work done by his maternal grandfather Alfonso VIII and consolidated the Reconquista. In 1231, he permanently united Castile and León. He was canonized in 1671 and, in Spanish, he is Fernando el Santo, San Fernando or Sa
2 CONC n Fernando Rey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand was born at the monastery of Valparaíso (Peleas de Arriba, in what is now the province of Zamora) in 1198-99.
2 CONT
2 CONT His parents' marriage was annulled by order of Pope Innocent III in 1204, due to consanguinity. Berenguela took their children, including Ferdinand, to the court of her father. In 1217, her younger brother Henry I died and she suc
2 CONC ceeded him to the Castilian throne, but immediately surrendered it to her son, Ferdinand, for whom she initially acted as regent. When Alfonso died in 1230, Ferdinand also inherited León, though he had to fight Alfonso's heirs, Sa
2 CONC ncha and Dulce, daughters of his first wife, for it. He thus became the first sovereign of both kingdoms following the death of Alfonso VII in 1157.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early in his reign, Ferdinand had to deal with a rebellion of the House of Lara. He also established a permanent border with the Kingdom of Aragon by the Treaty of Almizra (1244).
2 CONT
2 CONT St Ferdinand spent much of his reign fighting the Moors. Through diplomacy and war, exploiting the internal dissensions in the Moorish kingdoms, he triumphed in expanding Castilian power over southern Iberian Peninsula. He capture
2 CONC d the towns of Úbeda in 1233, Córdoba in 1236, Jaén in 1246, and Seville in 1248, and occupied Murcia in 1243, thereby reconquering all Andalusia save Granada, whose king nevertheless did homage as a tributary state to Ferdinand i
2 CONC n 1238. Ferdinand divided the conquered territories between the Knights, the Church, and the nobility, whom he endowed with great latifundias. When he took Córdoba, he ordered the Liber Iudiciorum to be adopted and observed by its
2 CONC  citizens, and caused it to be rendered, albeit inaccurately, into Castilian.
2 CONT
2 CONT  United arms of Castile and León which Ferdinand first used.The capture of Córdoba was the result of a well planned and executed process whereby parts (the Ajarquía) of the city first fell to the independent almogavars of the Sier
2 CONC ra Morena to the north, which Ferdinand had not at the time subjugated.[1] Only in 1236 did Ferdinand arrive with a royal army to take Medina, the religious and administrative centre of the city.[1] Ferdinand set up a council of p
2 CONC artidores to divide the conquests and between 1237 and 1244 a great deal of land was parcelled out to private individuals and members of the royal family as well as the Church.[2] On 10 March 1241, Ferdinand established seven outp
2 CONC osts to define the boundary of the province of Córdoba.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the domestic front, he strengthened the University of Salamanca and founded the current Cathedral of Burgos. He was a patron of the newest movement in the Church: that of the friars. Whereas the Benedictines and then the Cister
2 CONC cians and Cluniacs had taken a major part in the Reconquista up til then, Ferdinand founded Dominican, Franciscan, Trinitarian, and Mercedarian houses in Andalusia, thus determining the religious future of that region. Ferdinand h
2 CONC as also been credited with sustaining the convivencia in Andalusia.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primera Crónica General de España asserts that, on his death bed, Ferdinand commended his son "you are rich in lands and in many good vassals — more so than any other king in Christendom," probably in recognition of his expans
2 CONC ive conquests.[4] He was buried within the Cathedral of Seville by his son Alfonso X. His tomb is inscribed with four languages: Arabic, Hebrew, Latin, and an early incarnation of Castilian.[5] St Ferdinand was canonized by Pope C
2 CONC lement X in 1671. Several places named San Fernando were founded across the Spanish Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT The symbol of his power as a king was his sword Lobera.
2 CONT
2 CONT First marriage  Statue of Ferdinand III by G.D. Olivieri (1753, Madrid)In 1219, Ferdinand married Elisabeth of Hohenstaufen (1203–1235), daughter of the German king Philip of Swabia and Irene Angelina. Elisabeth was called Beatriz
2 CONC  in Spain. Their children were:
