1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p112.htm#i3344
2 CONT
2 CONT Pernel (or)     Petronilla de Lacy held manors of Britford in Wiltshire; Yarkhill in Hereford. She was born at Dublin, Ireland. She married Sir Ralph VI de Tony, Lord Flamstead, son of Sir Roger IV de Tony, Seigneur de Conches, Ac
2 CONC quigny, Nogent-le-Roi, Portes, & Toéni (Tosny) and Constance de Beaumont, in November 1232 or 1234; They had 1 son (Roger) & 2 daughters (Constance, wife of Sir Fulk FitzWarin, & of David ap John; & Margaret, wife of William de St
2 CONC . Omer).2,3,4 Petronilla de Lacy married William de St. Omer, Itinerant Justice for Cambridgeshire before 15 October 1256; 2nd marriage for both.4 Petronilla de Lacy died after 9 March 1290.4
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3T-Z.htm#RaoulVITosnydied1239  
2 CONT
2 CONT RAOUL [VI] de Tosny of Castle Maud, Radnorshire (-at sea [29] Sep 1239).  The Testa de Nevill lists knights who held land from the king in Hertfordshire, dated to [1204/12]: "Radulfus de Tony" held land "in Flamstede"[178].  The R
2 CONC ed Book of the Exchequer records "Radulfus de Toany" holding property "Flamstede" in Essex, and four knights’ fees and parts "in Estham, Rudmerlega, La Bereve, Warneslega, La More, Scravelega, Sendeslega" in Worcestershire in [121
2 CONC 0/12][179].  Henry III King of England granted property "Radulfo de Thoeny", which King John had granted to "Rogero de Thoeny patri ipsius Radulfi", dated 11 Jun 1229[180].  Matthew Paris records the death "in mari...circa festum 
2 CONC sancti Michaelis” 1239 of “Radulphus de Thoni”[181].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (Sep [1232/34]) as her first husband, '''PERNEL de Lacy,''' daughter of WALTER de Lacy Lord of Meath & his wife [Margery de Briouse] (-after 25 Oct 1288).  She married secondly (before 15 Oct 1256) Guillaume de Saint-Omer.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Raoul [VI] & his wife had three children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT *a)         '''ROGER''' [VI] de Tosny (29 Sep 1235-[10 Jun 1263/14 May 1264]).  A writ dated 18 Jun "48 Hen III", after the death of "Roger de de Tony alias de Thony", lists his manors but does not name his heirs[182].  m firstly 
2 CONC ALICE de Bohun, daughter of HUMPHREY de Bohun Earl of Hereford & his wife Mathilde de Valence (-bur Llanthony Priory ...
2 CONT *b)         '''CONSTANCE''' de Tosny (-after 11 Feb 1266).  The Complete Peerage notes that the manor of Yarkhill, Herefordshire was given to Ralph de Tosny and Pernel his wife who, after her husband’s death, gave it in free marri
2 CONC age to Fulk FitzWarin [IV] and Constance[187].  m [as his second wife,] FULK [IV] FitzWarin, son of FULK [III] FitzWarin & his first wife Matilda le Vavasour (-killed in battle Lewes 14 May 1264). 
2 CONT *c)         '''ALICE''' de Tosny .  Pope Nicholas IV granted dispensation to “Walter de Bello Campo” and “Alice daughter of Ralph de Tonny to remain in the marriage they contracted in ignorance that they were related in the fourth
2 CONC  degree of kindred”, and declared “their offspring legitimate”, dated 23 Sep 1289[188].  m WALTER de Beauchamp, son of [WILLIAM [IV] de Beauchamp & his wife Isabel Mauduit. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Some sources show this daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT * c) Margaret, wife of William de St. Omer.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         GUILLAUME de Saint-Omer (-after 19 Oct 1265).  The late 13th century genealogy by Balduinus de Avennis names "Bilas…Wilelmo" as the two sons of "castellano Sancti-Audomari domino Wilelmo…quintus filius Nicolaus" and his
2 CONC  wife, adding that Guillaume died childless[953].  In Naples, later in Flanders.  m (before 15 Oct 1256) as her second husband, PERNEL de Lacy, widow of RAOUL [VI] de Tosny, daughter of WALTER de Lacy & his wife [Marguerite de Bri
2 CONC ouze] (-after 25 Nov 1288). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Spouse
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Ralph VI de Toeni [k], Lord of Flamstead, b abt 1189/90, Flamstead, Hertfordshire, England, d abt 29 Sep 1239 (at sea). He md Petronilla de Lacy Nov 1234, daughter of Sir Walter de Lacy, Lord of Meath and Weobley, and Margaret
2 CONC /Margery de Braose.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.geneajourney.com/toeni.html#notek
2 CONT
2 CONT k. In 1204, with his father and brother, he was excluded by the King of France from the terms of the pacification in Normandy. In Nov 1213, King John ordered that the manors of Saham and Ryhcot be restored to him. Presumably he su
2 CONC pported King John in the civil war, as in Apr 1216, that King granted him the lands which had been held by Richard de Montfitchet, in Essex, Bucks, Cambridge, Norfolk, Suffolk and Hunts. But soon afterwards, Ralph must have joined
2 CONC  the rebellious barons, for John ordered in Jul that Robert de Mortimer be given siezin of the land of Abberley, held by Ralph, and on 5 Sep following, the King gave the manor of Flamstead to Waleran Tyes. Upon Henry III's accessi
2 CONC on to the throne, Ralph was granted the manor of Newport, Essex Jun 1218, and in Sep 1233 he was given the custody of Maud Castle in radnor. Later that year he and John de Monmouth were appointed generals of the oitevin mercenarie
2 CONC s in the Welsh marches against the Earl of Pembroke and Llewelyn. In 1239, he took the Cross and set out for the Holy Land. He married, between Nov 1232 and 1234, Pernel, daughter of Walter de Lacy. He died at sea, and his widow m
2 CONC arried bef 15 Oct 1256, William de St. Omer. She was still living 25 Nov 1288.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_de_Lacy,_Lord_of_Meath
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter de Lacy (c. 1172 – 1241) was lord of Meath in Ireland. He was also a substantial land owner in Weobley, Herefordshire, in Ludlow, Shropshire, in Ewyas Lacy in the Welsh Marches, and several lands in Normandy.[1] He was the 
2 CONC eldest son of Hugh de Lacy, a leading Cambro-Norman baron in the Norman invasion of Ireland, and Rohese of Monmouth.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Margaret de Braose, the daughter of William de Braose, 4th Lord of Bramber and Maud de St. Valery and had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT # Pernel de Lacy (c. 1201 – after 25 November 1288), married Sir Ralph VI de Toeni, Lord of Flamstead, son of Sir Roger IV de Toeni, Lord of Flamstead and Constance de Beaumont.
2 CONT # Egidia de Lacy (also called Gille) who married Richard Mór de Burgh, Lord of Connaught and Strathearn. Together they had many notable descendants, including David II of Scotland, the Earls of Clanricarde, Jane Seymour, Margaret 
2 CONC de Clare, the Earls of Ormond, English kings Edward IV and Richard III, and many other British monarchs.
2 CONT # Gilbert de Lacy of Ewyas Harold, Herefordshire was taken hostage for his father in August 1215. He predeceased his father before 25 December 1230. Gilbert married Isabel Bigod, daughter of Sir Hugh Bigod, 3rd Earl of Norfolk (Ma
2 CONC gna Carta surety) and Maud Marshal. They had a son and two daughters ...
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT >"At the time of his death in early 1241, apparently before 28 February, Walter de Lacy was blind and feeble, bankrupt, and without male heirs. His only son, Gilbert, who married Isabella, daughter of Ralph Bigod, died in 1234, le
2 CONC aving a son, Walter, and two daughters, Matilda and Margaret. This Walter was alive in 1238, and married a daughter of Theobald Butler (d. 1205), but predeceased his grandfather without heirs. Henry III took this opportunity to di
2 CONC spense patronage by arranging the marriages of the Lacy sisters, now coheirs of the Lacy estates, to noted royal servants. Margaret married John de Verdon, and Matilda married Pierre de Genevre and afterwards Geoffrey de Geneville
2 CONC ." [18]
2 CONT
2 CONT 18.  Oxford Dictionary of National Biography.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
