2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p11408.htm#i114074
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne of Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne of Kiev (c. 1130 – c. 1193) was Queen Consort of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne was the first daughter of Grand Prince Mstislav I of Kiev and his second wife, Liubava Dmitrievna.
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT King Géza II of Hungary (1130 – 31 May 1162)
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. King Stephen III of Hungary (1147 – 4 March 1172)
2 CONT
2 CONT -2. King Béla III of Hungary (1148 – 23 April 1196)
2 CONT
2 CONT -3. Elisabeth (c. 1149 – after 1189), wife of Duke Frederick of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT -4. Duke Géza (c. 1150 – before 1210)
2 CONT
2 CONT -5. Odola (? – ?), wife of Duke Svatopluk of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT -6. Helena (c. 1158 – 25 May 1199), wife of Duke Leopold V of Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne is an ancestor of Edward III of England and hence the ancestor of all subsequent English and British monarchs. She is also part of a link connecting the line of Harold Godwinson and the modern line of British monarchs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euphrosyne_of_Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Eufrozina magyar királyné [szerkesztés]
2 CONT
2 CONT 2010. január 13.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Wikipédiából, a szabad enciklopédiából.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ugrás: navigáció, keresés
2 CONT
2 CONT Eufrozina vagy Fruzsina (Kijev, 1130 - Jeruzsálem, 1193): magyar királyné, II. Géza felesége, III. István és III. Béla anyja.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Élete [szerkesztés]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1130-ban született I. Miciszláv kijevi nagyfejedelem és második felesége, egy Liubava nevű novgorodi nemeslány leányaként. 1146-ban kérte meg kezét Belos bán a 16 éves II. Géza magyar király számára, hogy az orosz anyától származó
2 CONC  és orosz neveltetésű magyar trónkövetelőnek, Borisz hercegnek az oroszországi összeköttetéseit ezáltal gyengítse. A házasságot még ugyanebben az évben megkötötték, Eufrozina Magyarországra költözött. Nyolc gyermeke született:
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. III. István (1147 - 1172. március 4.)
2 CONT
2 CONT -2. III. Béla (1148 - 1196. április 24.)
2 CONT
2 CONT -3. Margit (1149 - 1190 után), Frigyes cseh herceg felesége
2 CONT
2 CONT -4. Géza (1151 - 1210 előtt)
2 CONT
2 CONT -5. Árpád - fiatalon meghalt
2 CONT
2 CONT -6. Odola (kb. 1156 - 1169 után), kb. 1164-től Svatopluk cseh herceg felesége
2 CONT
2 CONT -7. Ilona (1158 - 1199. május 25.), 1172/74-től V. Lipót osztrák herceg felesége
2 CONT
2 CONT -8. Margit (1162 - 1208), előbb Isaac Macrodukas, majd András somogyi főispán felesége
2 CONT
2 CONT Mint magyar királyné, igen nagy befolyást gyakorolt férje elhatározásaira. Felesége kedvéért II. Géza több ízben nyújtott katonai segítséget rokonainak, különösképpen sógorának, II. Izjaszláv kijevi nagyfejedelemnek a csehek ellen
2 CONC i háborúiban.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eufrozina 1162-ben jutott nagyobb szerephez, amikor férje korai halála után kiskorú fia, III. István nevében átvette a kormányt. A bizánci támogatással fellépő II. László elől 1162-ben Pozsonyba menekült, majd Ausztriában talált m
2 CONC enedéket. Nagy része volt abban, hogy III. István 1166-ban feleségül vette Ágnest, II. Henrik osztrák herceg leányát.
2 CONT
2 CONT II. Géza fivéreinek, az I. Mánuel bizánci császár által pártfogolt II. Lászlónak és IV. Istvánnak a bukása után III. István 1165-ben hosszas harcok után ismét visszaszerezhette trónját. Fia uralkodása alatt Eufrozina csendesen és 
2 CONC köztiszteletben élt a királyi udvarban.
2 CONT
2 CONT III. István néhány évvel később, 1172. március 4-én váratlanul meghalt, és hívei Bizáncban élő öccsének, III. Bélának ajánlották föl a trónt. Béla 1172 tavaszán hazaérkezve szembesült azzal, hogy a Bizánchoz fűződő kapcsolatai mia
2 CONC tt nem egységes a támogatottsága. Eufrozina is ellene fordult, és helyette ifjabbik fiát, Géza herceget akarta trónra juttatni. Ezért Béla félreállította testvérét: miután Géza fellázadt, börtönbe záratta, az anyakirálynét pedig e
2 CONC lfogatta, majd a Szentföldre száműzte.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eufrozinának ennélfogva el kellett hagynia az országot, szentföldi száműzetésébe legkisebb leánya, Margit hercegnő is elkísérte. Jeruzsálemben halt meg 1193-ban, holttestét később hazahozták, és az általa alapított templomban Szék
2 CONC esfehérvárott temették el.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://hu.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eufrozina_magyar_kir%C3%A1lyn%C3%A9
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne of Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne of Kiev, (c. 1130 – c. 1193), Queen Consort of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne was the first daughter of Grand Prince Mstislav I of Kiev and his second wife, Liubava Dmitrievna. In 1146, she was married to King Géza II of Hungary, who had come of age shortly before.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his husband's reign she did not intervene in the politics of the kingdom, but after his death on 31 May 1162, her influence strengthened over their son, King Stephen III. The young king had to struggle against his uncles La
2 CONC dislaus and Stephen to save his throne, and Euphrosyne took an active part in the struggles. She persuaded King Vladislaus II of Bohemia to give military assistance to her son against the invasion of the Emperor Manuel I Komnenos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne's favourite son was the youngest, Duke Géza. Therefore, when King Stephen III died on 4 March 1172, she was planning to ensure his succession against her older son, Béla who had been living in the court of the Emperor M
2 CONC anuel I Komnenos. However, Béla came back, and he was crowned on 13 January 1173 although the Archbishop Lukas of Esztergom denied his coronation. Shortly afterwards, King Béla III had his brother arrested which increased the tens
2 CONC ion between Euphrosyne and his son. Although, Duke Géza managed to escape from his captivity soon, probably with Euphrosyne's help, but in 1177 he was again arrested.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1186, Euphrosyne tried to release his younger son again, but she failed. King Béla III ordered to arrest his mother and kept her confined in the fortress of Barancs (Serbian: Braničevo). Shortly afterwards, Euphrosyne was set f
2 CONC ree but she was obliged to leave the kingdom for Constantinople. From Constantinople she moved to Jerusalem where he lived as a nun in the convent of the Hospitallers and then in the Basilian monastery of Saint Sabbas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT # 1146: King Géza II of Hungary (1130 – 31 May 1162)
2 CONT
2 CONT King Stephen III of Hungary (1147 – 4 March 1172)
2 CONT
2 CONT King Béla III of Hungary (1148 – 23 April 1196)
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth (c. 1149 – after 1189), wife of duke Frederick of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Géza (c. 1150 – before 1210)
2 CONT
2 CONT Odola (? – ?), wife of duke Sviatopluk of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena (c. 1158 – 25 May 1199), wife of duke Leopold V of Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Soltész, István: Árpád-házi királynék (Gabo, 1999)
2 CONT
2 CONT Kristó, Gyula - Makk, Ferenc: Az Árpád-ház uralkodói (IPC Könyvek, 1996)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne of Kiev (c. 1130 – c. 1193) was Queen Consort of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne was the first daughter of Grand Prince Mstislav I of Kiev and his second wife, Liubava Dmitrievna. In 1146, Euphrosyne married King Géza II of Hungary, who had come of age shortly before.
2 CONT
2 CONT During her husband's reign Euphrosyne did not intervene in the politics of the kingdom, but after his death on 31 May 1162, her influence strengthened over their son, King Stephen III. The young king had to struggle against his un
2 CONC cles Ladislaus and Stephen to save his throne, and Euphrosyne took an active part in the struggles. She persuaded King Vladislaus II of Bohemia to give military assistance to her son against the invasion of the Emperor Manuel I Ko
2 CONC mnenos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne's favourite son was the youngest, Duke Géza of Hungary. When King Stephen III died on 4 March 1172, she was planning to ensure his succession against her older son, Béla, who had been living in the court of the Emperor 
2 CONC Manuel I Komnenos. However, Béla came back, and he was crowned on 13 January 1173, although the Archbishop Lukas of Esztergom denied his coronation. Shortly after, King Béla III arrested his brother, which increased the tension be
2 CONC tween Euphrosyne and her son. Duke Géza soon managed to escape, probably with Euphrosyne's help, but in 1177 he was again arrested.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1186, Euphrosyne tried to release her younger son again, but she failed. King Béla III ordered the arrest of Euphrosyne and kept her confined in the fortress of Barancs (Serbian: Braničevo). Shortly after, Euphrosyne was set fr
2 CONC ee, but she was obliged to leave the kingdom for Constantinople. From Constantinople she moved to Jerusalem where she lived as a nun in the convent of the Hospitallers, and then in the Basilian monastery of Saint Sabbas.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT King Géza II of Hungary (1130 – 31 May 1162)
2 CONT
2 CONT King Stephen III of Hungary (1147 – 4 March 1172)
2 CONT
2 CONT King Béla III of Hungary (1148 – 23 April 1196)
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth (c. 1149 – after 1189), wife of Duke Frederick of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Géza (c. 1150 – before 1210)
2 CONT
2 CONT Odola (? – ?), wife of Duke Svatopluk of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena (c. 1158 – 25 May 1199), wife of Duke Leopold V of Austria
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne is an ancestor of Edward III of England and hence the ancestor of all subsequent English and British monarchs. She is also part of a link connecting the line of Harold Godwinson and the modern line of British monarchs.
2 CONT
