2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Géza II (Hungarian : II. Géza, Slovak : Gejza II, Croatian : Gejza II), (1130, Tolna  – 31 May 1162), King of Hungary (1141–1162). He ascended the throne as a child and during his minority the kingdom was governed by his mother. H
2 CONC e was one of the most powerful monarchs of Hungary, who could intervene successfully in the internal affairs of the neighbouring countries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT Géza was the eldest son of King Béla II of Hungary  and his wife, Helena of Raška . He was only a baby when his mother introduced him and his brother Ladislaus  to the barons assembled in Arad  in order to persuade them to massacr
2 CONC e her husband's opponents.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was crowned three days after his father's death on 13 February 1141. As he was still a minor, his mother served as regent of the kingdom helped by her brother, Beloš. She faced challenges from Boris , the son of King Coloman's 
2 CONC adulterous queen, who disputed Géza's claim to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1146, Boris managed to occupy the fortress of Pozsony. Although the Hungarian troops would reoccupy the fortress, but Henry II, Duke of Austria , intervened in the struggles on behalf of the pretender. Géza personally led
2 CONC  his armies against the Austrian troops and defeated them on 11 September.
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT As an adult, Géza had a reputation as a well-respected king, whose nobles did not dare to scheme against him. The power and valor of his army was also commented upon, and Géza did not hesitate to involve himself in politics.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1146, Géza married Euphrosyne , sister of Grand Prince Iziaslav II of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT In June 1147, the Crusader  Army of King Conrad III of Germany  passed through Hungary without major conflicts, then King Louis VII of France  arrived in the country, followed by the pretender Boris, who had secretly joined the Fr
2 CONC ench Crusaders. Although King Louis VII refused to extradite the pretender to Géza, he did promise to take him abroad under close custody.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1148, Géza sent troops to his brother-in-law Iziaslav II against Prince Vladimir of Chernihiv . In 1149, he assisted his maternal uncle, Duke Uroš II of Raška  against the Byzantine Empire. In 1150, Géza sent new troops to Izia
2 CONC slav, who had been struggling against Prince Yuri I of Suzdal , but his brother-in-law was not able to maintain his rule in Kiev. In the same year, the Serbian and Hungarian armies were defeated by the Byzantine troops, therefore 
2 CONC Duke Uroš II had to accept the Byzantine rule over Raška .
2 CONT
2 CONT In the autumn of 1150, Géza lead his armies against Prince Vladimirko of Halicz (son-in-law of the late King Coloman), but the prince managed to persuade Géza's advisors to convince their king to give up the campaign. It can be fo
2 CONC und in a Ruthenian chronicle Hypatian Codex , where at the date of 1150 one can read: The Hungarian King Géza II crossed the mountains and seized the stronghold of Sanok  with its governor as well as many villages in Przemysl  are
2 CONC a. In 1152, Géza and Iziaslav II went together against Halych, and they defeated Volodymyrko's armies at the San  River. Géza had to return to his kingdom because, during his campaign, Boris attacked the southern territories of Hu
2 CONC ngary supported by Byzantine troops. However, Géza would defeat the pretender and made a truce with the Byzantine Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1154, he supported the rebellion of Andronikos Komnenos  against Emperor Manuel I and laid siege to Barancs , but the emperor had overcome his cousin's conspiracy and liberated the fortress.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1157, his younger brother, Stephen conspired against him supported by their uncle, Beloš. Although Géza would overcome their conspiracy, Stephen fled to the court of Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor . Géza sent his envoys to the
2 CONC  emperor and promised to assist him with troops against Milan . Therefore Frederick I refused any support from Stephen who then fled to Constantinople . Stephen was followed, in 1159, by their brother, Ladislaus, who also had cons
2 CONC pired against Géza.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1161, inspired by the new Archbishop of Esztergom, Lukács , Géza not only acknowledged the legitimacy of Pope Alexander III  instead of Antipope Victor IV , who had been supported by Emperor Frederick I, but he also renounced t
2 CONC he right of investiture 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried in Székesfehérvár.      Wikipedia
2 CONT
