2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude of Merania (1185 – 24 September 1213) was the first wife of Andrew II, king of Hungary. She was the daughter of Berthold IV, Duke of Merania and his wife Agnes of Rochlitz. Through her son Bela, Gertrude was an ancestress
2 CONC  of Philippa of Hainault, Queen consort of Edward III of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Family
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Murder
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 In Media
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 References
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude and her Meranian family Gertrude's sister was Agnes of Merania, a famous beauty, who married King Philippe Auguste of France. Her other sister was St. Hedwig of Andechs, wife of Henry I the Bearded. Their brother was Otto
2 CONC  I, Duke of Merania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude's mother, Agnes was a great great granddaughter of Frederick I, Duke of Swabia and his wife Agnes of Germany. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes of Germany was a daughter of Henry IV, Holy Roman Emperor and his first wife, Bertha of Savoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertude's paternal grandparents were Berthold V, Count of Andechs and his wife, Hedwig of Wittelsbach, a descendant of Bela I of Hungary, through his daughter, Sophia.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Her parents wanted their daughters to all make important political marriages, which would create alliances for Berthold IV. Gertrude married Andrew II of Hungary before 1203. The couple had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Maria of Hungary (c. 1204 – 1237), wife of Tzar Ivan Asen II of Bulgaria
2 CONT
2 CONT King Béla IV of Hungary (1206 – 3 May 1270)
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Elisabeth of Hungary (1207 – 10 November 1231), wife of Landgraf Louis IV of Thuringia
2 CONT
2 CONT King Coloman of Halych (1208 – after 11 April 1241)
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Andrew II of Halych (c. 1210 – 1234) [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude had much political influence over her husband. It was probably Gertrude who persuaded Andrew to conspire against his brother again, but when King Emeric, who had realised that Andrew's troops outnumbered his armies, went 
2 CONC unarmed, wearing only the crown and the sceptre, to Andrew's camp near Varasd, Andrew surrendered voluntarily on the spur of the scene. The king had his brother arrested, but Andrew managed to escape shortly afterwards. During thi
2 CONC s time, Gertrude escaped back to her father.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Murder
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude was killed in 1213, by the Hungarian noblemen, who were jealous over the advancement of her relatives at court. While the king was in battle, Gertrude gave out Hungarian land as "gifts" to her German relatives. (One third
2 CONC  of the country was given away but the Hungarians got it back after the queen's death). Thus, Hungary did not prosper and the queen acted as a dictator.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hungarian nobles had enough and in 1213 while hunting, she was killed. Due to the current political situation most of her murderers remained unpunished during the rule of Andrew II. Only Gertrude's son Béla IV took revenge aft
2 CONC er his assession to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude's tomb was of a Gothic style. Her tomb was excavated between 1967 and 1980. [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Through her son Bela, Gertrude was an ancestress of Philippa of Hainault, Queen consort of Edward III of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude's husband went on to remarry twice again. His second wife was Yolanda de Courtenay, who bore him one daughter, Violant, who married James I of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Yolanda's death, Andrew married Beatrice d'Este, they had a son called Stephen.
2 CONT
2 CONT See:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gertrude_of_Merania
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude of Merania (1185 – September 24, 1213) was the first wife of Andrew II, king of Hungary. She was the daughter of Berthold IV, Duke of Merania. Her sister was Agnes of Merania, a famous beauty, who married King Philippe Au
2 CONC guste of France. Yet another sister was St. Hedwig of Andechs.
2 CONT
2 CONT She and Andrew married before 1203, and she was the mother of his successor Bela IV of Hungary, Saint Elizabeth of Hungary, wife of Louis, landgrave of Thuringia, and Anna Maria, wife of tsar Ivan Asen II of Bulgaria. Gertrude was
2 CONC  killed in 1213, by the Hungarian noblemen, who were jealous over the advancement of her relatives at court. While the king was in battle, Gertrude gave out Hungarian land as "gifts" to her German relatives. 1/3 of the country wer
2 CONC e gifts but the Hungarian got it back after the queen's death. Thus, Hungary did not prosper and the queen acted as a dictator. The Hungarian nobles had enough and in 1213 while hunting, she was killed. Due to the current politica
2 CONC l situation most of her murderers remained unpunished during the rule of Andrew II. Only Gertrude's son Béla IV took revenge after elevated to the throne.
2 CONT
