2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcellus Donald R. von Redlich, Pedigrees of Some of the Emperor Charlemagne's Descendants, volume I (1941; reprint, Baltimore, Maryland, U.S.A.: Genealogical Publishing Company, 2002), page 56. Hereinafter cited as Pedigrees of 
2 CONC Emperor Charlemagne, I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcellus Donald R. von Redlich, Pedigrees of Emperor Charlemagne, I, page 57.
2 CONT
2 CONT source: http://thepeerage.com/p372.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide of Burgundy (c. 1233 – 23 October 1273) was a daughter of Hugh IV, Duke of Burgundy, by his first wife Yolande of Dreux. She was a member of the House of Burgundy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Family
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 See also
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide's brother was Robert II, Duke of Burgundy, he succeeded their father upon his death in 1270. Adelaide's eldest brother was Odo, Count of Nevers, but he died in 1266, therefore he never inherited the duchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Odo III, Duke of Burgundy, and Alice of Vergy. Her maternal grandparents were Robert III of Dreux and Aénor of Saint-Valéry.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1251, Adelaide married Henry III, Duke of Brabant (c. 1230 – 28 February 1261, Leuven), he was the son of Henry II, Duke of Brabant, and Marie of Hohenstaufen. The couple had four children, all of whom lived to adulthood,
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Henry IV, Duke of Brabant (c. 1251 – after 1272)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.John I, Duke of Brabant (1253–1294)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.Godfrey of Brabant, Lord of Aarschot (died 11 July 1302, Kortrijk), killed at the Battle of the Golden Spurs, married in 1277 Jeanne Isabeau de Vierzon (died after 1296)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.Maria of Brabant (1256, Leuven – 12 January 1321, Murel), married at Vincennes on 27 August 1274 to King Philip III of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide's husband died in 1261, and Adelaide died in 1273, aged forty years.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] See also
2 CONT
2 CONT Dukes of Brabant family tree
2 CONT
2 CONT Dukes of Burgundy family tree
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestors of Alice of Burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT Alix of Burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide of Burgundy (c. 1233 – 23 October 1273) was a daughter of Hugh IV, Duke of Burgundy by his first wife Yolande of Dreux. She was a member of the House of Burgundy. Her granddaughter, Marguerite of France was the second Que
2 CONC en consort of King Edward I of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Family
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 See also
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide's brother was Robert II, Duke of Burgundy, he succeeded their father upon his death in 1270. Adelaide's eldest brother was Eudes of Burgundy, but he died in 1266, therefore he never inherited the duchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Eudes III, Duke of Burgundy and Alice of Vergy. Her maternal grandparents were Robert III of Dreux and Aénor of Saint-Valéry.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1251, Adelaide married Henry III, Duke of Brabant (c. 1230 – 28 February 1261, Leuven), he was the son of Henry II, Duke of Brabant and Marie of Hohenstaufen. The couple had four children, all of whom lived to adulthood,
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Henry IV, Duke of Brabant (c. 1251 – after 1272)
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. John I, Duke of Brabant (1253–1294)
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Godfrey of Brabant, Lord of Aarschot (died 11 July 1302, Kortrijk), killed at the Battle of the Golden Spurs, married in 1277 Jeanne Isabeau de Vierzon (died after 1296)
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Maria of Brabant (1256, Leuven – 12 January 1321, Murel), married at Vincennes on 27 August 1274 to King Philip III of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Isabella of Aragon, Queen of France, her widower Philip III of France wished to remarry; he chose to marry Adelaide's daughter, Maria. The marriage produced three children, all of whom survived to adulthood:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Louis d'Évreux, (3 May 1276–19 May 1319), married Marguerite d'Artois by whom he had five children.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Blanche, Duchess of Austria (1282–1 March 1305), married Rudolph I of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Margaret, Queen of England (1279–14 February 1318), married on 8 September 1299 as his second wife, King Edward I of England by whom she had three children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide's husband died in 1261, and Adelaide died in 1273, aged forty years.
2 CONT
