2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas,_Count_of_Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Thomas Ι''' (Tommaso I; 1178 – 1 March 1233) was Count of Savoy from 1189 to 1233. He is sometimes numbered "Thomas I" to distinguish him from his son of the same name, who governed Savoy but was not count.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was born in Aiguebelle, the son of Humbert III of Savoy and Beatrice of Viennois. His birth was seen as miraculous; his monkish father had despaired of having a male heir after three wives. Count Humbert sought counsel from
2 CONC  St. Anthelm, who blessed Humbert three times, and it was seen as a prophecy come true when Thomas was born shortly before Anthelm himself died on 26 June 1178. He was named in honour of Saint Thomas Becket.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was still a minor when his father died on 4 March 1189, and a council of regency was established, composed of his mother Beatrice, his father's cousin Boniface I of Montferrat, and the Bishop of Saint-Jean-de-Maurienne. He 
2 CONC had reached his majority by August 1191. Thomas possessed the martial abilities, energy, and brilliance that his father lacked, and Savoy enjoyed a golden age under his leadership. Despite his youth he began the push northwest int
2 CONC o new territories. In the same year he granted Aosta Valley the "Charte des Franchises", recognising the right to administrative and political autonomy. This right was maintained until the eve of the French Revolution. Later he co
2 CONC nquered Vaud, Bugey, and Carignano. He supported the Hohenstaufens, and was known as "Thomas the Ghibelline" because of his career as Imperial Vicar of Lombardy.
2 CONT
2 CONT #	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas,_Count_of_Savoy#Career Career]
2 CONT #	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas,_Count_of_Savoy#Family_and_children Family and children] /!\ 
2 CONT --------
2 CONT
2 CONT *http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SAVOY.htm#ThomasIdied1233B
2 CONT
2 CONT THOMAS de Maurienne, son of HUMBERT III Comte de Maurienne et de Savoie & his fourth wife Béatrix de Vienne [Bourgogne-Comté] (Château de Carbonara 1178 after 26 Jun-Moncalieri 1 Mar 1233, bur Saint-Michel de la Cluse).  An undate
2 CONC d charter records a donation to Saint-Maurice by "felicis memorie Humbertus…Savoie comes" and the confirmation by "Thomas filius eiusdem comitis"[258].  He succeeded his father in 1189 as THOMAS I Comte de Maurienne et de Savoie, 
2 CONC under the regency of Guglielmo V Marchese di Monferrato who brokered a favourable settlement to Comte Humbert III's dispute with the empire[259].  As Marchese Guglielmo was absent in Palestine at the time of the death of Comte Hum
2 CONC bert III, the regency was presumably held and the negotiations carried out by his son Bonifazio, who succeeded as Marchese di Monferrato in 1192.  This hypothesis appears confirmed by the following charter: "Thomas…Mauriannensis c
2 CONC omes et marchio Italiæ" confirmed the donations made by "pater meus…[et] domini comitis Humberti…abavi mei" to the canons of Saint-Jean de Maurienne, with the advice of "B. matris mee et…tutore meo Bonifacio marchione Montisferrat
2 CONC i", by charter dated 12 Jun 1189[260].  "Thomæ comitis et marchionis…et…Nichola filia comitis Gebennarum" granted privileges to the citizen of Susa by charter dated 25 Feb 1198[261].  He used the title Comte de Savoie: "Thomas com
2 CONC es Sabaud. et Amedeus eius filius" granted rights to the abbey of San Marco by charter dated 5 Mar 1200[262].  Comte Thomas supported the imperial party over the Guelfs, and was appointed Imperial Vicar in Italy.  Philipp King of 
2 CONC Germany granted him Moudon in Vaud, and Chieri and Testona in Piemonte in 1207.   He also acquired Carignano, Pinerolo, Moncalieri, Vigone, Albenga and Savona in Piemonte.  "Thomas Maurianensis comes et marchio" donated property t
2 CONC o the abbey of Saint-Maurice, with the consent of "filiis suis Amedeo et Humberto", by charter dated 8 Nov 1217[263].  A charter dated 1224 records an agreement between "Thomæ com. Sabaud" and the bishop of Sion, witnessed by "ips
2 CONC e Thomas comes, Amedeus primogenitus illius, Comitissa uxor Thomæ, eorum quatuor filii clerici…Willelmus, Thomas, Petrus et Bonifacius"[264].  He bought the town of Chambéry in 1232.  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines re
2 CONC cords the death in 1232 of "comitis Thome de Sabaudia"[265].  An indication of the precarious financial position of the counts of Savoy is provided by a third testament, dated 2 Nov 1240, made by Thomas´s son "Amadeus com Sab. et 
2 CONC marchio in Italia" who repeated the nomination of "Thomæ, Flandriæ comiti, fratri suo" as his heir to "totius comitatus sui Sabaudiæ marchionatus Italiæ et ducatus Chablasii" if he died without male children, on condition that he 
2 CONC satisfied all the debts of "Thomæ comitis patris et Humberti fratris ipsorum"[266]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' ( [1196] )  MARGUERITE [Beatrix] de Genève, daughter of GUILLAUME [I] Comte de Genève & his second wife Béatrix de Faucigny (-8 Apr 1257, bur Abbaye de Hautecombe).  .. ... ..... ......
2 CONT
