2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=4c400bd2-f068-461e-aae7-24f169f3f678&tid=10145763&pid=-335552714
1 NOTE Thomas I or Tommaso I (c. 1176 - March 1, 1233) was Count of Savoy from 1189-1233. He was the son of Humbert III of Savoy and Beatrice of Viennois. His birth was seen as miraculous; his monkish father had despaired of having a mal
2 CONC e heir after three wives. Count Humbert sought counsel from St. Anthelm, who blessed Humbert three times, and it was seen as a prophecy come true when Thomas was born shortly before Anthelm himself died on June 26, 1178. He was na
2 CONC med in honour of Saint Thomas Becket.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was still a minor when his father died on 4 March 1189, and a council of regency was established, comprising of his mother Beatrice, his father's cousin Boniface I of Montferrat, and the Bishop of Saint-Jean-de-Maurienne. H
2 CONC e had reached his majority by August 1191. Thomas possessed the martial abilities, energy, and brilliance that his father lacked, and Savoy enjoyed a golden age under his leadership. Despite his youth he began the push northwest i
2 CONC nto new territories. In the same year he granted Aosta Valley the "Carta delle Franchigie", recognising the right to administrative and political autonomy. This right was maintained up until the eve of the French Revolution. Later
2 CONC  he conquered Vaud, Bugey, and Carignano. He supported the Hohenstaufens, and was known as "Thomas the Ghibelline" because of his career as Imperial Vicar of Lombardy.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and children
2 CONT In 1195 he ambushed the party of Count William I of Geneva, which was escorting the count's daughter, Marguerite of Geneva, to France for her intended wedding to King Philip II of France. Thomas carried off Marguerite and married 
2 CONC her himself, producing some eight sons and six daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amedeo, his immediate successor 
2 CONT Umberto, d. between March and November 1223 
2 CONT Tommaso, lord and then count in Piedmont and founder of a line that became the Savoy-Achaea 
2 CONT Aimone, d. August 30, 1237, Lord of Chablais 
2 CONT Guglielmo (William of Savoy), Bishop of Valence and Dean of Vienne 
2 CONT Amadeo of Savoy, Bishop of Maurienne 
2 CONT Pietro, who resided much in England, became Earl of Richmond, and ultimately in 1263 became the disputed count of Savoy 
2 CONT Filippo, archbishop of Lyon, who resigned, through marriage became Count Palatine of Burgundy and ultimately in 1268 became the disputed count of Savoy 
2 CONT Bonifacio who became archbishop of Canterbury 
2 CONT Beatrice of Savoy, d. 1265 or 1266, married in December 1219 to Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence (1209-1245) and was mother of four Queens-consort 
2 CONT Alasia of Savoy, abbess of the monastery of St Pierre in Lyon (d.1250) 
2 CONT Ágatha of Savoy, abbess of the monastery of St Pierre in Lyon (d.1245) 
2 CONT Margherita of Savoy, d. 1273, married in 1218 to Hartmann I of Kyburg 
2 CONT Avita of Savoy (1215-92) who married Baldwin de Redvers, 7th Earl of Devon and Robert Aguillon (d.1286). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Further reading
2 CONT Francesco Cognasso, Il Piemonte nell'Età Sveva (Turin, 1968) 
