1 NOTE About the time his son was age 7, Enguerrand VI was killed in the war against the English at about the time of the Battle of Crecy in 1346. Whether he died in the battle or some other is uncertain.This one battle of the Hundred Ye
2 CONC ars War was between the English army of Edward III and the rest of Europe on August 26, in which Europe's greatest army of horse soldiers was massacred near Abbeville.  The French army of Philip VI had 12,000 men-at-arms, 6000 mer
2 CONC cenary Genoese crossbowmen, 20,000 milice des communes and assorted feudal vassals. The French attacked the English lines 16 times during the day until midnight, and were practically annihilated.  The English heralds counted 1,542
2 CONC  French knights on the battlefield - the English lost little more than 50 - defended by their quick shooting English archers and spearmen who waged battle against the heavily armored kngihts, many who lost their horses in battle, 
2 CONC and could not remount, and were slaughtered on the ground.
