1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_III_of_Imereti
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander succeeded upon the death of his father, George III of Imereti, in 1639. Most of his reign was spent in the struggle against the powerful prince of Mingrelia, Levan II Dadiani, who refused to acknowledge the king of Imere
2 CONC ti as his overlord, and aspired to displace him from his throne. In one of the battles, Dadiani captured and blinded Alexander’s energetic brother Mamuka, bringing the king to the edge of despair. Alexander’s father-in-law, Teimur
2 CONC az I of Kakheti, who had fled the Saffavid Persian invasion of his country to Imereti, attempted to mediate the conflict, but nothing came of this.
2 CONT
2 CONT Both Mingelian and Imeretian rulers sought Russian support in their cause. Envoys from Moscow visited Mingrelia in 1639/40, though without achieving any positive results. In response to an appeal from Alexander, another embassy ar
2 CONC rived in the Imeretian capital of Kutaisi in 1651, and, on October 9, Alexander took an oath of fealty to Tsar Alexis Mikhailovich. As the Russian were still too far from South Caucasus, this move had virtually no effect on the co
2 CONC urse of events, though. It was not until Levan’s death in 1657 that Alexander was able to avenge his earlier setbacks. He immediately marched into Mingrelia, subdued its nobles and installed his own nominee, Vameq, as prince-Dadia
2 CONC ni. In 1659, he also interfered in the Principality of Guria, and replaced the refractory prince Kaikhosro I with his protégé Demetre Gurieli. Thus, for a short time, the authority of the crown of Imereti was reestablished through
2 CONC out western Georgia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Alexander was married twice. His first wife was Tamar, daughter of Mamia II Gurieli, whom Alexander married in 1618 and divorced in 1620. He married secondly, in 1629, Nestan-Darejan, daughter of Teimuraz I of Kakheti. All 
2 CONC of his children were born of the first marriage:
2 CONT
2 CONT Bagrat V (1620–1681), King of Imereti (1660–1681). Prince Klimenti (fl. 1651). Princess Tinatin (fl. 1661 – 1680), who was married to the nobleman Lasha-Giorgi Goshadze and then to Levan III Dadiani.
