2 CONT gróf Zrínyi IV. Miklós (1518?-1566. szept. 7. Szigetvár) és második felesége Eva von Rosenberg bárónő, (*1537. április. 12.-†1591. augusztus). Esküvő: 1564. 09. 3*. Monyorókerék. Gyermekeik: 1. N. N. leány; 2. III. Zrínyi János (1
2 CONC 565-1612. febr. 24. Hohenfurt) császári pohárnok. (Megjegyzés: A metszet Dominicus Custos 1601-ben készült rézkarcának és az Eva von Rosenberg bárónőt ábrázoló festmény (lásd: másik képen) nem túl szerencsés párosítása. TJ.) A nag
2 CONC yított posztamens látható a csáktornyai Zrínyi várban a Muraközi Múzeum állandó kiállításán. Foto: Temesvári Jenő 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Since 1347 the family using "ZRINYI" instead "SUBICH" after their estate name "ZRIN" castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendants of Nicola ZRINSKI:
2 CONT * -every FIRST born son received Csáktornya residence
2 CONT * -every SECOND born son received Ozalj residence
2 CONT
2 CONT -Miklós Zrinyi was Ban of Croatia,Slavonia,Dalmatia!-
2 CONT
2 CONT Nikola Šubić Zrinski or Miklós Zrínyi (Zrin,  circa1518 – Szigetvár, September 7, 1566), was a Croatian nobleman and general in service of Habsburg Monarchy, ban of Croatia from 1542 to 1556, and member of the Zrinski noble family
2 CONC . He was known across Europe for his involvement with the Battle of Szigetvár and is today seen as a hero by both Hungarians and Croatians.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nikola was the son of Nikola III Zrinski and Jelena Karlović (sister of future Croatian ban Ivan Karlović). He distinguished himself at the siege of Vienna in 1529, and in 1542 saved the imperial army from defeat before Pest by in
2 CONC tervening with 400 Croats, for which service he was appointed ban of Croatia. In 1542 he routed an Ottoman force at the Battle of Somlyo.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1543 he married Catherine (Katarina) Frankopan, a sister of earl Stjepan Frankopan Ozaljski ("Steven Frankopan of Ozalj" in English), who placed the whole of her vast estates at his disposal. She bore him many children, among w
2 CONC hich was his successor Juraj IV Zrinski. The king Ferdinand I gave him large possessions in Hungary and Croatia, and henceforth the Zrinskis–Zrínyis became as much Magyar as Croatian magnates.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1556 Zrinski won a series of victories over the Ottomans, culminating in the battle of Babócsa. The Croats, however, overwhelmed their ban with reproaches for neglecting them to fight for the Magyars, and the emperor simultaneo
2 CONC usly deprived him of the captaincy of Upper Croatia and sent 10,000 men to aid the Croats, while the Magyars were left without any help, whereupon Zrinski resigned the banship (1561). In 1563, on the coronation of the Emperor Maxi
2 CONC milian as king of Hungary, Zrinski attended the ceremony at the head of 3000 Croatian and Magyar mounted noblemen, in the vain hope of obtaining the dignity of palatine, vacant by the death of Thomas Nadasdy. Shortly after marryin
2 CONC g (in 1564) his second wife, Eva of Rožmberk (Rosenberg), a great Bohemian heiress, he hastened southwards to defend the frontier, and defeated the Ottomans at Szeged.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1566, from August 5 to September 7, his small force (2,300 soldiers) heroically defended the little fortress of Szigetvár against the whole Ottoman host (102,000 soldiers), led by Suleiman the Magnificent in person. The Battle 
2 CONC of Szigetvár ended with Zrinski perishing with every member of the garrison in a last desperate sortie.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT A szigetvári hős,és horvát bán.-Szent Ilonán temetve.-Subich genus.
