1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sitti%C5%9Fah_Hatun
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sitti Mükrime Hatun''', (c. 1435 – April 1467), was the second wife of Ottoman Sultan Mehmed II. She was one of the daughters of Süleyman Bey, the sixth ruler of Dulkadir State. Her marriage served as an alliance between the Ot
2 CONC tomans and this buffer state.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sitti Mükrime hatun was born into the Dulkadir State as a Dulkadir princess, one of the daughters of Süleyman Bey, the sixth ruler of Dulkadir State. Her aunt Emine Hatun, daughter of Nasıreddin Muhammed Bey, the fifth ruler of Du
2 CONC lkadirids was married to Sultan Murad's father Mehmed I. While her other aunt was married to the aged Mamluk Sultan at Cairo. Later Ayşe hatun, Süleyman Bey's grand daughter, was married to the future Sultan Bayezid II and became 
2 CONC the mother of Selim I, known as the grim. According to some other sources Sitt-î Mû’karîma Khātûn was the real biological mother of Ottoman Sultan Bayezid II.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her son Bayezid II. Süleyman Bey, who is described as a man of unshapely corpulence and pathological sensuality but also as a skillful horsemen and the owner of magnificent stables, possessed a considerable army of brave, devoted 
2 CONC Turk men and was fabulously wealthy, two circumstances which in themselves sufficed to incline Murad toward the union of his son and heir with this respected noble families which centuries later, though dispossessed of its lands, 
2 CONC was still revered as a family of royal blood. The Byzantine chronicler Ducas was convinced, not without reason, that one of the Sultan's chief motives in seeking this marriage was to obtain an ally against the arrogant Karamanids 
2 CONC and Jahan Shah, the chief of the Turk men Black Sheep Tribe (Kara Koyunlu).
2 CONT
2 CONT When Mehmed turned seventeen, his father decided that he should to married to a woman of inferior station for political purposes. The Sultan's choice fell on the wealthy and beautiful daughters of Süleyman Bey, the sixth ruler of 
2 CONC Dulkadir State. It must have been in the winter of 1448-1449 that Murad summoned Çandarlı Halil Pasha, his trusted Grand Vizier, and informed him of the marriage plans. The Sultan declared that he wished the prince to marry and th
2 CONC is time as he, Murad, saw fit. Halil Pasha approved wholeheartedly of his master's plan, whereupon they decided to choose one of Süleyman's daughters. The wife if Hizir Pasha, Governor of Amasya, was sent to Elbistan to select the
2 CONC  bride in accordance with ancient custom. Her choice fell on Sitti hatun, the most beautiful of the daughters; the intermediary kissed her eyes and put the engagement ring on the finger. Later the same matron, this time accompanie
2 CONC d by Saruca Pasha, the Sultan's favoured adviser in family matters, returned to the court of Elbistan to bring the chosen bride home to Rumelia. The most distinguished nobles of the land escorted the young girl across the mountain
2 CONC s to the former Ottoman capital of Bursa, where the judges, the ülema and the sheikhs of the religious orders came to meet her in solemn procession and then onward across the Dardanelles. At the news the cortege was approaching, M
2 CONC urad sent out the grandees of the realm from Edirne to meet his future daughter-in-law, who continued on the Sultan's residence with her imposing retinue. The wedding took place on 15 December 1449 at Edirne[3] and was celebrated 
2 CONC with a great pomp for three months. Popular festivities of all sorts and poetry contests contributed to the rejoicings. The bridegroom, who had not been consulted on the choice of his bride, returned with her to Manisa immediately
2 CONC  after the celebration. The '''marriage is seem to have childless and not very happy'''. Apparently, the whole arrangement was not to Mehmed's liking.
2 CONT
2 CONT Long After Mehmed had removed his court to Istanbul, Sitti hatun remained behind Edirne, where she lived, lonely and forsaken, until the end of April 1647. It is seemed likely that her niece Ayşe, in devotion to her memory, comple
2 CONC ted the mosque which bears her name and beside which she rests under the open sky in a grave now completely uncared for her. The two cracked tombstones have been removed from the shrubbery and taken to the city museum. The mosque 
2 CONC is today used by a hay barn. According to other sources her tomb is located at the Muradiye Complex, Bursa.
