1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the husband of https://www.geni.com/people/Eudoxia-Paleologus-Fictional/6000000218937161909
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodore_Paleologus
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Theodore Paleologus''' (Italian: Teodoro Paleologo; c. 1560 – 21 January 1636) was a 16th and 17th-century Greek nobleman, soldier and assassin. According to the genealogy presented on Theodore's tombstone, he was a direct male
2 CONC -line descendant of the Palaiologos dynasty, which had ruled the Byzantine Empire from 1259 to its fall in 1453. Though most of the figures in the genealogy can be verified to have been real historical figures, the veracity of his
2 CONC  imperial descent is uncertain.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Pesaro in the north central east coast of Italy around 1560,[1] Theodore Paleologus was the son of Camilio Paleologus, about whom very little is known.[2] The name of his mother is not known.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paleologus_of_Pesaro Theodore's family] might have been late-surviving descendants of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palaiologos_dynasty Palaiologos dynasty],[4] which ruled the Byzantine Empire 
2 CONC from 1259 to 1453.[5] They claimed descent from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Palaiologos Thomas Palaiologos] (Camilio being Thomas's supposed great-great-grandson), a brother of the final emperor [https://en.wikipedia.org
2 CONC /wiki/Constantine_XI_Palaiologos Constantine XI Palaiologos], through a son called '''John''', ''whose existence can not be confirmed through contemporary sources''. All other purported ancestors (descendants of this John) of the 
2 CONC later Paleologus family can be verified through contemporary records.[6] On account of the absence of evidence for John's existence, English Byzantininst Donald Nicol wrote in 1974 that "Theodore’s claim to be a descendant of Thom
2 CONC as Palaiologos [...] must be held unproven".[7] John Hall, author of a 2015 biography on Theodore, believes that it would be wrong to "dismiss Theodore's claim out of hand" on account of a single missing link.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family and children
2 CONT
2 CONT 1906 monument erected near the grave of Theodore's son Ferdinand Paleologus on Barbados
2 CONT With his wife Mary, Theodore had six, possibly seven, children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Theodore Paleologus (June – 1 September 1600) – Theodore and Mary's first child, died in infancy.[22]
2 CONT # Dorothy Paleologus (August 1606 – 1681) – Remained in Landulph after Theodore's death. Dorothy married William Arundel, son[48] or grandson[49] of Alexander Arundel, who Nicholas Lower had purchased Clifton Hall from.[48] The en
2 CONC tries recording the marriage in the marriage registers at Landulph and at William's home parish of St Mellion grandly describe Dorothy as of "imperial stock" (Dorothea Paleologus de stirpe imperatorum).[23][48] Since the registers
2 CONC  at St. Dominic were accidentally destroyed, it is impossible to determine whether Dorothy and William had children,[49] but it is unlikely since Dorothy was fifty years old by the time of the marriage.[23] Dorothy was buried in L
2 CONC andulph in 1681.[50]
2 CONT # Mary Paleologus (? – 1674) – Remained in Landulph after Theodore's death. Very little is known of Mary and she is the only one of the children whose birth year is unknown. She was probably never married and was buried in Landulp
2 CONC h on 15 May 1674.[48]
2 CONT # Theodore Paleologus (April 1609 – April/May 1644) – The oldest son to reach adulthood, Theodore Junior fought for the Parliamentarians, or Roundheads, in the English Civil War (1642–1651). He died during the war in 1644, probabl
2 CONC y of camp fever during the early stages of the siege of Oxford, and was buried in Westminster Abbey.[23][51]
2 CONT # John Theodore Paleologus (June/July 1611 – ?) – The most enigmatic of the children, John Theodore is thought to have fought for the Royalists, or Cavaliers, in the English Civil War, but left England before its conclusion, being
2 CONC  attested in Barbados with his younger brother Ferdinand in 1644. Nothing is known of John Theodore after 1644 and his ultimate fate is unknown.[23][52]
2 CONT # (?) Elizabeth Paleologus (July/August 1614 – ?) – Known only from a partial baptismal record from Tattershall, Elizabeth is likely to have been another of Theodore and Mary's daughters. As she is never referenced again after thi
2 CONC s baptismal record, it is probable that she died in infancy.[24]
2 CONT # Ferdinand Paleologus (June 1619 – 2 October 1670) – The youngest son, Ferdinand travelled with John Theodore to Barbados,[52] where he stayed for the rest of his life, becoming one of the elite on the island.[53] He had a son, n
2 CONC amed Theodore, and was known on Barbados as the "Greek prince from Cornwall".[23] Ferdinand constructed a great house on the island, named Clifton Hall after the house the family had stayed in while in Cornwall.[54]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Fraudulent genealogy 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to some genealogies, Theodore was married to another woman before Mary. This previous marriage would have taken place on 6 July 1593 on the island Chios, his bride being [https://www.geni.com/people/Eudoxia-Paleologus-Fi
2 CONC ctional/6000000218937161909 "Eudoxia Comnena",] a daughter of the nobleman Alexius Comnenus and his wife Helen Cantacuzene (both parents possessing surnames of Byzantine imperial dynasties). Eudoxia was to have died on 6 July 1596
2 CONC , three years after the wedding, in childbirth, and the couple's only child was said to have been a girl named [https://www.geni.com/people/Theodora-Rhodocanakis-Fictional/6000000218937381823?through=6000000218937161909 "Theodora 
2 CONC Paleologus"], married in 1614 in Naples to [https://www.geni.com/people/Dimitrios-Rhodocanakis-Fictional/6000000218937236869?through=6000000218937381823 "Prince Demetrius Rhodocanakis"]. Though this genealogy has been accepted by 
2 CONC some historians in the past, and notably convinced the papacy and the British Foreign Office, it originates from forgeries created in the 1860s by the London-based Greek merchant [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demetrius_Rhodocanak
2 CONC is Demetrius Rhodocanakis], who claimed that one of Theodora's descendants was Dr. Constantine Rhodocanakis (a real historical figure), who Demetrius in turn claimed was his ancestor.[55] Demetrius's forgeries were revealed when h
2 CONC e published a biography on Constantine Rhodocanakis in 1872, wherein a portrait of Constantine was exposed to actually be a portrait of the author himself, dressed in a costume. His genealogy had been thoroughly debunked by the ea
2 CONC rly 20th century.[56]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT (Qui giace il corpo di '''Teodoro Paleologi''' [padre] di Pesaro in Italia; discendente dalla linea imperiale degli ultimi imperatori cristiani della Grecia. È il figlio di Camillo, figlio di Prosper, figlio di Teodoro, figlio di 
2 CONC [https://www.geni.com/people/Ioannis-Palaiologos/6000000000129362295 Giovanni], figlio di Tommaso secondo fratello di Costantino Paleologi 8° di questo nome e l'ultimo che ha regnato a Costantinopoli fino alla conquista da parte d
2 CONC ei turchi; ha sposato Mary, figlia di William Balls di Hadley in Souffolke Gent e aveva 5 figli: Teodoro [seppellito in Westminster Abbey], Giovanni, Ferdinando [seppellito nell'isola di Barbados] Maria e Dorothea e finisce questa
2 CONC  vita a Clyton il 21 gennaio [sic!] 1636.)
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paleologi#cite_note-9
2 CONT
2 CONT
