2 CONT ------
2 CONT -http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrico_Dandolo
2 CONT
2 CONT Enrico Dandolo (anglicised as Henry Dandolo and Latinized as Henricus Dandulus; c. 1107 – May 1205) was the 41st Doge of Venice from 1192 until his death. He is remembered for his avowed piety, longevity, and shrewdness, and is kn
2 CONC own for his role in the Fourth Crusade and the Sack of Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT  Early life and political involvement
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Venice c. 1107,[1] Enrico Dandolo was a member of the socially and politically prominent Dandolo family. He was the son of the powerful jurist and member of the ducal court, Vitale Dandolo,[2] and had two brothers: Andrea 
2 CONC and Giovanni.[3] His uncle, also named Enrico Dandolo, was patriarch of Grado.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Not much information exists on the younger Enrico before his father's death in 1174.[5] This is because Vitale lived into his nineties and his sons were not emancipated until he died.[6] Though Enrico was himself an elderly man at
2 CONC  around 67, he was still under filial subjection. This was a type of partial emancipation in which he could conduct business, but because he worked for the family, most, if not all, documents used Vitale's name rather than Enrico'
2 CONC s.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dandolo's first important political roles took place during the crisis years of 1171 and 1172, which were a tumultuous period between the Byzantine and Venetian empires. After Byzantine emperor Manuel Comnenus restored Pisans and 
2 CONC Genoans, both enemies of the Venetians, to their quarters in Constantinople as part of his plan to reclaim Italy, an angry Venetian mob attacked the recently reinstated Genoese quarter.[7] This attack caused Comnenus in March 1171
2 CONC  to order the seizure of goods and imprisonment of thousands of Venetians living in the empire.[8] Popular Venetian anger with the attack forced Doge Vitale II Michiel to gather a retaliatory expedition, which included Dandolo.[9]
2 CONC  This expedition fell apart when its participants were struck by plague in 1172, and upon his return Michiel was killed by a mob of Venetians, angry with his defeat.[10][11]
2 CONT
2 CONT The succeeding doge, Sebastiano Ziani, sought to form alliances with enemies of the Byzantine empire so that it would feel pressured into coming to terms with Venice.[12] He sent out multiple expeditions to Constantinople and King
2 CONC  William II's court in Sicily, several of which Dandolo was a part, although he never met with William.[13] Despite Dandolo's failure to meet with William II, his constant participation in these envoys shows his value and importan
2 CONC ce within the ducal court, qualities which no doubt contributed to his election as doge in 1192.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dandolo also made trips to Constantinople in 1183 and 1184. The first voyage, on which he embarked with his brother, Giovanni, was the first in which he acted as a ducal legate.[14] On this trip, he most likely engaged in negotiat
2 CONC ions for reparations of the city's Venetian quarter with the new Byzantine emperor Andronicus I.[14] He also invested and restored land to Venetian monasteries, a deed which earned him the position of legal advocate for the monast
2 CONC ery of San Cipriano di Murano.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1184, Dandolo, serving again as a ducal legate along with Pietro Ziani and Domenico Sanudo, returned to Constantinople to negotiate the restoration of the Venetian quarter with Andronicus. In this meeting, the emperor at last a
2 CONC greed to release the imprisoned Venetians, restore their quarter, and pay for reparations.[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Dogeship
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 1, 1192, after Orio Mastropietro abdicated the throne, Dandolo became the forty-first Doge of Venice.[17] He was the second doge to be chosen by a council of forty electors.[17] Already aged and blind, but deeply ambitious
2 CONC , he displayed tremendous energy and mental capacity.[18][19] His remarkable deeds over the next eleven years have led some to hypothesize that he actually may have been in his mid seventies when he became Venice's leader. None of
2 CONC  the earlier chronicles and contemporary witnesses give his exact age, only mentioning that he was very old. The commonly given birth year of c. 1107 is based on the account of Marino Sanuto the Younger (1466 – 1536) three centuri
2 CONC es later, who stated that Dandolo was eighty-five when he assumed the throne.[18] Though not the first doge to take the promissione ducale, Dandolo's is the earliest that is available to historians.[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT One of Dandolo's first decrees as doge was to evict all foreigners who had lived in Venice for fewer than two years on August 16, 1192.[21] Landlords were obligated to evict any of these foreigners from their premises. Citizens wh
2 CONC o violated the decree had to pay fifty lire, and foreigners’ goods were confiscated. Additionally, Venetians were not allowed to lend money to foreigners—excepting those from the areas of Umana or Ragusa—for a period that exceeded
2 CONC  fifteen days.[22] The reason why this decree was implemented is unknown, but it seems to correlate to a recent increase of foreigners into Venice, since it did not affect foreigners who had been living in the city for more than t
2 CONC wo years.[23]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1193, Dandolo commanded an attack on the nearby city of Zara, which for years had troubled Venice and threatened its control over the Dalmatian Coast. Until 1180, Zara had been under Venetian control, until they staged a succes
2 CONC sful rebellion in which they became the sole city on this coast that was against Venetian interests.[24] Dandolo seemed to have always supported Venice's reinstating power over the city, since he had contributed money to doge Orio
2 CONC  Mastropiero's 1187 military attempt to regain control there.[25] Dandolo's 1193 attack on Zara was only somewhat successful. He managed to regain control over the islands of Pago, Ossero, and Arbe, which had been lost in an 1190 
2 CONC attempt led by Mastropiero, though not Zara.[25]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1194, Dandolo enacted important reforms to the Venetian currency system. Before these reforms, Venice's principal coin was the silver penny, which weighed less than a gram and was about one-quarter fine.[26] Due to the debaseme
2 CONC nt of the silver penny in 1180 and the constant fluctuation in value of Jerusalem and Byzantine coins,[27] Dandolo instated three denominations of this silver penny, the bianco (half-penny), the quartarolo (quarter-penny), and the
2 CONC  silver grosso.[28] The bianco had a silver content of about five percent, and was decorated with a cross on one side and St. Mark on the other.[29] The quartarolo had almost no precious metal content, which made it the first Euro
2 CONC pean token coin since ancient Rome.[29] The grosso was the first nearly pure silver–and high denomination–coin minted in western Europe in over five centuries.[29][30] It was decorated with an image of Dandolo and St. Mark on one 
2 CONC side, and of Jesus Christ enthroned on the other side, which imitated a design typically seen on Byzantine aspron trachy coins.[29] The grosso eventually became the dominant coin of Mediterranean commerce.[29]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Fourth Crusade
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1202, six French envoys of the Fourth Crusade arrived in Venice in hopes of acquiring a fleet and supplies.[31] Dandolo arranged meetings for them with the ducal court, in which the council calculated the expenses necessary for
2 CONC  this voyage, which would be the largest project in Venetian history.[32] The terms were laid out as such: for up to a year, Venice would provide transportation and most provisions for the army. Four silver marks would be paid for
2 CONC  each knight and horse, and two would be paid for each other member. Finally, many Venetians would also join the Crusade and promised to supply fifty fully armed galleys as long as the French promised to split the spoils with them
2 CONC .[33] With the enthusiastic support of the population, Venice's participation in the Crusade was confirmed. Dandolo himself swore on holy relics to uphold every part of the agreement.[34]
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Venice soon faced a financial problem. The six original Crusaders had borrowed money as a down payment for the fleet, but failed to pay it back.[35] When more Crusaders began to arrive that June, the urgency for this mone
2 CONC y increased as many Venetians, whose business relied on this reimbursement, were being driven closer to financial ruin.[36] When the due date for payment arrived, Dandolo ordered the Crusaders, who were staying on the nearby Lido,
2 CONC  to collectively pay 85,000 marks. Even when everyone, including many poor Crusaders, contributed all they could afford, they still owed 34,000 marks.[36] Instead of ejecting them, Dandolo decided to lend this amount from the Vene
2 CONC tian state, provided that it was paid back in the form of the spoils of the Crusade. In addition, Dandolo proposed that the Crusaders agree to spend the winter in Zara. This was due to the threat of Zaran pirates to Venetian comme
2 CONC rce as well as the Venetian's interest in regaining control over the area. Additionally, staking an interest in Zara helped convince the Great Council to consent to Dandolo's plan.[37]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Crusade fleet left Venice during the first week of October 1202, following an emotional and rousing ceremony in San Marco di Venezia where Dandolo "took the cross" –committed himself to crusading–and promised to "go live or di
2 CONC e" with the Crusaders in exchange for his people's support, and his sons' taking his place during his absence.[38] The Crusaders arrived in Zara in November, the sheer size of their fleet intimidating the Zarans into near surrende
2 CONC r.[39] Dandolo gave the Zarans an ultimatum: either they leave the city right away or they would be killed.[39] Confusion ensued, as Pope Innocent forbade the Crusade from settling this dispute unrelated to their original religiou
2 CONC s agenda, especially since the land was controlled by King Emeric of Hungary, who had himself participated in a crusade.[40] Finally, Innocent threatened excommunication to anyone who antagonized the Zarans.[39] The Crusaders atta
2 CONC cked the city anyway, and it at last fell on November 24, 1202.[41] All of the Venetian members of the Crusade were thus excommunicated (the French Crusaders had sent an envoy to the pope to ask for forgiveness), but Dandolo kept 
2 CONC this a secret from them since he knew they would abandon the Crusade if they found out.[42]
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly afterwards, Alexius Angelus, son of the deposed Byzantine emperor Isaac II, arrived in Zara, looking for help to overthrow his uncle, Alexius III, after he violently seized the throne from Isaac.[43] Dandolo agreed to the 
2 CONC Crusade leaders' plan to place Alexius Angelus on the throne of the Byzantine Empire in return for his support and funds to help the Crusade.[44] The Crusaders thus took another detour to Constantinople, where the conquest and sac
2 CONC k of Constantinople took place on April 12, 1204. During the looting, Dandolo had many items of value sent back to Venice, including the four Horses of St. Mark that decorate the Venetian cathedral to this day.[45] When Constantin
2 CONC ople fell, Dandolo understood that he needed to quickly restore stability to the empire to avoid disorder that could threaten Venice. One necessary task was to find an emperor for the new Latin empire. Dandolo was offered the posi
2 CONC tion, but he refused, and Baldwin of Flanders instead took the throne.[46]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Partitio Romaniae also resulted from this conquest, and it awarded Venice three-eighths of the Byzantine Empire in accordance with an agreement drafted by the Crusaders before the fall of the empire.[47] This included a part o
2 CONC f Constantinople near the harbor, a portion of the shoreline of the Sea of Marmara, and the city of Adrianople, among other former Byzantine possessions.[48] Dandolo was also awarded the title "lord of three-eighths of the Roman E
2 CONC mpire", although these acquisitions only lasted until the collapse of the Latin empire in 1261.[49]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nineteenth-century grave marker in the Hagia Sophia's East Gallery Dandolo died in 1205 and was buried in June in the Hagia Sophia in Constantinople.[50] He was the only person to be buried there.[49] In the 19th century an Italia
2 CONC n restoration team placed a cenotaph marker near the probable location, which is still visible today. The marker is frequently mistaken by tourists as being a medieval marker of the actual tomb of the doge. The real tomb was destr
2 CONC oyed by the Ottomans after the conquest of Constantinople in 1453 and subsequent conversion of Hagia Sophia into a mosque.[50][51]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Blindness
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not known for certain when and how Dandolo became blind. According to the Chronicle of Novgorod he had been blinded by the Byzantines during the 1171 expedition to Byzantium.[citation needed] Supposedly, Emperor Manuel Comne
2 CONC nus "ordered his eyes to be blinded with glass; and his eyes were uninjured, but he saw nothing".[52] According to Thomas F. Madden's study, Dandolo suffered from cortical blindness as a result of a severe blow to the back of the 
2 CONC head received sometime between 1174 and 1176.[53] Documents show Dandolo's signature being fully legible in 1174 but sprawling across the paper in 1176, suggesting that his sight deteriorated over time.[53]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dandolo's blindness appears to have been total. Geoffrey de Villehardouin, whom Dandolo accompanied on the Fourth Crusade, wrote that "although his eyes appeared normal, he could not see a hand in front of his face, having lost hi
2 CONC s sight after a head wound".[54] This piece of primary evidence seems to support Madden's theory that Dandolo's blindness was cortical, since his eyes appeared to be unharmed.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Dandolo's son, Ranieri, served as vice-doge during Dandolo's absence and was later killed in the war against Genoa for the control of Crete.[55][56] It is unclear if he had other children besides Ranieri as the existence of none c
2 CONC an be confirmed by contemporary evidence.[57]
2 CONT
2 CONT During his dogeship, Dandolo was married to a woman named Contessa Minotto, to whom he delegated authority over his commercial and private affairs along with his brother, Andrea, and assumed friend Filippo Falier of the San Tomà p
2 CONC arish, before he departed on a political trip to Constantinople with his brother, Giovanni, in 1183.[58] Contessa's identity is debated, and it is often thought that she may have been a member of the Minotto clan though the veraci
2 CONC ty of this claim is inconclusive.[59]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the nineteenth-century, the Regia Marina (Italian Navy) launched an ironclad battleship named Enrico Dandolo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enrico Dandolo and his role in the conquest of Constantinople were referenced by Lord Byron in his poem Childe Harold's Pilgrimage:[60]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Oh, for one hour of blind old Dandolo!
2 CONT
2 CONT  Th' octogenarian chief, Byzantium's conquering foe.
