2 CONT After Zoe and Michael died she came out of retirement to Rule as Empress.
2 CONT The Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium: Oxford Univ Press 1991.
2 CONT Michael Psellus, Chronographia, New Haven: Yale University Press, 1953 Vol 1,2,3,4,5,6,7
2 CONT http://www.fordam.edu/halsall/basis/psellus-chrono-02.html
1 NOTE Theodora (Greek: Te?d??a, Theodora, 984 - early September, 1056) was a Byzantine Empress. She was co-empress from 1042 and from January 11, 1055 to after August 31, 1056 actively ruled the Eastern Roman Empire or, the Roman Empire
2 CONC  of the Middle Ages that often is described as the Byzantine Empire. She was born into the Macedonian dynasty that ruled the empire for almost two hundred years, and would become its last monarch.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of Constantine VIII and Helena, daughter of Alypius. Possessed of a strong and austere character, Theodora refused the hand of the heir-presumptive, Romanos Argyros, who then was married to her sister, Zoe, in
2 CONC stead in 1028.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although living in retirement Theodora excited Zoe's jealousy and, on a pretext of a conspiracy with Presian of Bulgaria, she was confined in a monastery.
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 19, 1042, the popular movement which caused the dethronement of Michael V also led to Theodora's installment as the active co-empress with her sister. After two months of active participation in government alongside her s
2 CONC ister, she allowed herself, virtually, to be superseded by Zoe's new husband, Constantine IX Monomachos, on June 11, 1042. Nevertheless, Theodora was considered as an empress alongside her sister and brother-in-law throughout thei
2 CONC r reign. Her sister predeceased her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the death of Constantine IX on January 11, 1055 and in spite of her seventy years of age, Theodora vigorously reasserted her dormant rights to rule and frustrated an attempt to supersede her on behalf of the general, Nikephor
2 CONC os Proteuon, governor of Bulgaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT By her firm administration she controlled the unruly nobles and checked numerous abuses; but she marred her reputation by excessive severity toward private enemies and the undue employment of menials for advisers, including her in
2 CONC fluential minister Leo Paraspondylos. Leo's faction was interested in maintaining its control of government through the aging empress, while the patriarch Michael Keroularios advocated that Theodora advance a subject to the throne
2 CONC  through marriage to her, something which would have assured the succession. This was not accomplished.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodora became gravely ill on August 31, 1056 and died a few days later, in early September. Having no children and being the last member of her dynasty, she had chosen one of her favorites, the former military minister, Michael 
2 CONC VI Bringas, as her successor on the recommendation of Leo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael VI was not related to the great Macedonian dynasty that had ruled the Byzantine Empire for a period of 189 years, however, and did not receive universal support. This lack of support resulted in a series of conflicts for t
2 CONC he throne among various noble families, that lasted from 1056 until 1081.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT The Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium, Oxford University Press, 1991. 
1 NOTE She ruled with her sister Zoe in 1042 and again from 1055 to 1056 alone.
