1 NOTE {geni:about_me} - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Guiscard (her father) 
2 CONT *(GREEK) Olympia (Eleni), was married to Constantine Doukas, son of Michael the Emperor of the Romans. The engagement was canceled in 1078 by Nikephoros III Botaneiates and with this pretext, Rovert invaded Romania.
2 CONT *(ITA) Olimpia (ante 1060 - ?), sposò nel 1076 (promessa già dal 1074) Costantino Ducas, figlio dell'imperatore di Bisanzio Michele VII Ducas e di Maria di Alania; a Costantinopoli fu nominata Elena
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT - http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SICILY.htm#EriaM1078UgoEste
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert "Guiscard" & his second wife had eleven children
2 CONT
2 CONT 10.      [OLYMatthew ParisIAS] of Apulia (-after 1090).  The Annals of Romoald record the betrothal of an unnamed daughter of Robert "Guiscard" and "imperatorem Constantinopolem" in 1076[354].  The Alexeiad records that Emperor Mi
2 CONC khael Dukas "promised his own son Konstantinos in marriage to the daughter of this barbarian Robert", in a later passage stating that "the lady's name was Helena"[355].  Skylitzes records the betrothal of "Robertus…filiam…Helenam"
2 CONC  and "suo filio Constantino" (referring the son of Emperor Mikhael VII), dated to [1073/75][356].  Amatus also records this betrothal[357].  She is named Olympias by Houts who does not cite the primary source on which this is base
2 CONC d[358].  She lived in Constantinople after her betrothal and was baptised into the Greek Orthodox church as HELENA.  Orderic Vitalis says that two of the daughters of Robert "Guiscard" were living in Constantinople, and that they 
2 CONC remained there after the accession of Emperor of Alexios I (in 1081), performing light service at court before being sent back to Sicily[359].  After the betrothal was ended, she was placed in a convent.  She eventually returned t
2 CONC o Italy after the death of her parents, and settled at her uncle's court at Palermo.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Betrothed''' (Aug 1074, contract broken 1078) to KONSTANTINOS Dukas co-Emperor, Porphyrogenetos son of Emperor MIKHAEL VII & his wife Maria of Georgia (-[1092/97]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ------
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Psellos' monody, Andronikos was married to an unnamed woman, who died soon after his own death. He had no descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constantine_Doukas
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine Doukas
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine Doukas or Ducas (Greek: Κωνσταντίνος Δούκας, Kōnstantinos Doukas), (c. 1074–c. 1095) was Byzantine co-emperor from c. 1075 to 1078 and from 1081 to 1087. He was the son of Emperor Michael VII Doukas and his Georgian wi
2 CONC fe Maria of Alania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine was porphyrogennētos, i.e., Born in the purple, and was associated on the throne by his father soon after his birth. He was quickly involved in imperial diplomacy, as the infant was betrothed to '''Olympias of Apulia, 
2 CONC renamed Helena''', the daughter of the Norman leader Robert Guiscard and Sikelgaita. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://wc.rootsweb.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=randyj2222&id=I1084&style=TEXT
2 CONT
2 CONT >“In 1074 Michael VII of Byzantium had suggested his own son Constantine a s the prospective bridegroom for the most beautiful -- he had been careful to specify -- of Robert [Guiscard]'s daughters. The Guiscard had not hesitated: 
2 CONC the opportunity of becoming father-in-law to the Emperor of Byzantium was too good to miss. He had accepted the proposal, and shortly afterwards had bundled his daughter Helena [i.e., Olympias] off to Constantinople, there to purs
2 CONC ue her studies in the imperial gynaeceum un til her infant fiance should be of marriageable age. "The overthrown of Michael VII in 1078 put paid to all Helena's chances of attaining the imperial throne. The hapless princess found 
2 CONC herself immured in a convent, with which she was doubtless far from pleased." Norwich then goes on to relate that Olympias/Helena's imprisonment gave her father the perfect pretext to invade Byzantium. He doesn't mention the girl'
2 CONC s ultimate fate, if she were ever returned to her father or if she remained in Byzantium.” [Norwich, John Julius. The Normans in the South 1016-1130. Longmans: London, 1967.] [https://books.google.com/books?id=poRoAAAAMAAJ&focus=s
2 CONC earchwithinvolume&q=Helena limited view]
