2 CONC  Formbacensium, "Heinricus genuit Gebehardum et Dietricum"[264].  “Adelheid comitissa” donated “mansum ad Winzingin et Crufilingin...” to Göttweig, for the soul of “viri sui Heinrici”, and after their mother’s death “filii eius Ge
2 CONC behart et Dietrich” donated “vineam ad Mirchingin”, dated to [1090] and [1096][265].  "...Ditrich Formbach, Gebehardus comes de Piugin et frater eius Ernist..." witnessed a charter dated [1121/22][266].  Graf von Viechtenstein.  T
2 CONC he Codex Traditionum of Formbach monastery records a donation by "Domnus Dietricus comes de Viehtensteine" for the soul of "uxoris sue domne Adelheide"[267].  The necrology of Seeon records the death "III Kal Dec" of "Dietricus co
2 CONC m"[268].  The necrology of Scheftlarn records the death "III Kal Dec" of "Dietricus com"[269].  The necrology of Melk records the death "II Kal Dec" of "Dietricus com"[270].  m [https://www.geni.com/people/Adelheid-von-%C3%96sterr
2 CONC eich/6000000006727873713?through=6000000004533221528 ADELHEID], daughter of -- (-14 Jun -).  The Codex Traditionum of Formbach monastery records a donation by "Domnus Dietricus comes de Viehtensteine" for the soul of "uxoris sue d
2 CONC omne Adelheide"[271].  Wegener suggests that she was the daughter of Luitpold III Markgraf of Austria, on the basis of her husband's death being recorded in the necrology of Scheftlarn, a foundation of Markgraf Luitpold's son Otto
2 CONC  Bishop of Freising with which the Grafen von Formbach had no other known connection[272].  However, this hypothesis is difficult to sustain from a chronological point of view as Markgraf Luitpold III second marriage took place in
2 CONC  1105 while Adelheid was already married to Dietrich in 1120.  The necrology of Melk records the death "XVIII Kal Jul" of "Adalheid com"[273].  Graf Dietrich & his wife had [one] child: 
