1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humbert_II,_Count_of_Savoy (23 languages)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Humbert II''' (Italian: Umberto II), nicknamed '''the Gat''' (c. 1065 – 19 October 1103[1]), was Count of Savoy from 1080 until his death in 1103. He was the son of Amadeus II of Savoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Gisela of Burgundy,[1] daughter of William I, Count of Burgundy, and had seven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Amadeus III of Savoy (1095-1148)[1]
2 CONT *William, Bishop of Liège[1]
2 CONT *Adelaide, (d. 1154), married to Louis VI of France[1]
2 CONT *Agnes, nicknamed '''the Horn''' (d. 1127), married to Archimbald VI, lord of Bourbon[1]
2 CONT *Umberto[1]
2 CONT *Reginald, nicknamed '''the Tower''' [1]
2 CONT *Guy, nicknamed '''the Huge''', abbey of Namur
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT *http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SAVOY.htm#HumbertIIdied1103B
2 CONT
2 CONT '''HUMBERT de Savoie''', son of AMEDEE II Comte de Savoie & his wife Jeanne [de Genève] ([1072]-Moûtiers 19 Oct 1103, bur Moûtiers).  "Umbertus comes, filius quondam Amedei" donated property to the monastery at Pinerolo by charter
2 CONC  dated 29 Nov 1098[143].  He succeeded his father in 1080 as HUMBERT II "le Renforcé" Comte de Maurienne et de Savoie, under the regency of his paternal grandmother.  On her death in 1091, he lost much of her territories, retainin
2 CONC g only the valley of Susa[144].  He was the first of his family to strengthen relations with France, diverting his attention from Italy after the loss of most of the family's Italian possessions.  "Ubertus filii qda Amedo" donated
2 CONC  property to Santa Maria d´Ivrea by charter dated 14 Sep 1094[145].  "Nantelmus" confirmed the foundation of the priory of Bellevaux en Bauges ["Bellævalles…supra villam Boggarum"], with the support of "Humberto comite", by charte
2 CONC r dated to the end-11th century[146].  "Humbertus comes et marchio" donated property to the abbey of Aulps ["in pago Gebennensi in valle…Alpis"], with the consent of "Girardo Alingiensi et Gilione de Rovorea quorum feudem est", by
2 CONC  charter dated to [1094], witnessed by "Girardus Alingiensis, Rodulphus de Fulciniaco, Uldricus comes, Anselmus, Willelmus, Amedeus filius eiusdem Girardi"[147].  "Humbertus comes et Girardus de Alingio et Gislo miles a quibus est
2 CONC  ille locus" consented to the agreement between the abbey of Molesme and the abbey of Aulps, recorded in a charter dated 1097[148].  "Umbertus comes filius quondam Amedei" donated property to Pinerolo by charter dated 29 Nov 1098[
2 CONC 149].  The necrology of Saint-Jean-de-Maurienne records the death "XIV Kal Nov" of "Humbertus comes Maur."[150].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([1090]) as her first husband, GISELE de Bourgogne, daughter of GUILLAUME I Comte Palatin de Bourgogne, Comte de Vienne et de Mâcon & his wife Etiennette --- ([1075]-after 1133).  Her parentage is deduced from Suger stating that
2 CONC  her daughter Adelaide, wife of Louis VI "le Gros" King of France, was the niece of Pope Calixtus II[151].  This is corroborated by "Guido Viennensis archiepiscopus" (later Pope Calixtus II) addressing a letter to "nepoti suo Amed
2 CONC eo comiti" (Amedée III Comte de Savoie, son of Gisèle by her first marriage) dated [1115][152].  Her date of birth is estimated from her having given birth to five children by her second husband whom she married in [1105], and ass
2 CONC uming that she was no more than 17 years old when she gave birth to her first child by her first husband.  "Amedeus comes" donated property to Saint-Jean de Maurienne, for the soul of "patris sui Uberti comtis", with the consent o
2 CONC f "Gisla matre et fratribus eius Guillelmo atque Umberto", by charter dated 21 Oct 1104[153].  She married secondly ([1105]) Ranieri Marchese di Monferrato.  Her second marriage is confirmed by Orderic Vitalis who records the marr
2 CONC iage of Guillaume de Normandie and the daughter of Raniero III Marchese di Monferrato, naming both the bride's parents and specifying that the marriage was arranged by the bride's uterine half-sister, Adélaïde de Maurienne Queen o
2 CONC f France[154].  
2 CONT
2 CONT   Comte Humbert II & his wife had [seven] children
