1 OCCU Count of Burgundy (1057-1087) and Mâcon (1078-1085.), Count of Amous
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''He is a Direct Descendant of Charlemagne.'''
2 CONT This is his lineage:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Charlemagne
2 CONT #Louis I, The Pious
2 CONT #Giséle of Cysoing
2 CONT #Berengar I, emperor of the Romans
2 CONT #Gisela of Friuli
2 CONT #Berengar II of Ivrea, king of Italy
2 CONT #Adalbert II, king of Italy
2 CONT #Otto Guillaume I, comte de Bourgogne et de Mâcon
2 CONT #Reginald I of Ivrea, count palatine of Burgundy
2 CONT #William I "the Great" count of Burgundy
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume I was Count Palatin of Burgundy (Comte Palatin de Bourgogne). See http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liste_des_comtes_palatins_de_Bourgogne.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William I, Count of Burgundy''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.friesian.com/lorraine.htm#franchecomte
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00026527&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BURGUNDY%20Kingdom.htm#GuillaumeIdied1087B
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_I,_Count_of_Burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT Adapted from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT William I (1020 – 12 November 1087), called the Great (le Grand or Tête Hardie, "the Rash") was Count of Burgundy and Mâcon from 1057 to 1087. He was a son of Renaud I and Adelaide, daughter of Richard II, Duke of Normandy. Willia
2 CONC m was the father of several notable children, including Pope Callistus II.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1057, he succeeded his father and reigned over a territory larger than that of the Franche-Comté itself. In 1087, he died in Besançon and was buried there in the cathedral of St John.
2 CONT
2 CONT William married a woman named Stephanie.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT They had many children (not in order):
2 CONT
2 CONT #     * Renaud II, William's successor, died on First Crusade
2 CONT #     * Stephen I (Étienne), successor to Renaud II, Stephen died on the Crusade of 1101
2 CONT #     * Raymond, married (1090) Urraca, the reigning queen of Castile; successor to Étienne I, also was King of Leon and Galicia
2 CONT #     * Guy of Vienne, elected pope, in 1119 at the Abbey of Cluny. as Calixtus II
2 CONT #     * Sybilla (or Maud), married (1080) Eudes I of Burgundy
2 CONT #     * Gisela of Burgundy, married (1090) Humbert II of Savoy and then Renier I of Montferrat
2 CONT #     * Adelaide
2 CONT #     * Eudes
2 CONT #     * Hugh III, Archbishop of Besançon
2 CONT #     * Clementia married Robert II, Count of Flanders and was Regent, during his absence
2 CONT #     * Stephanie married Lambert, Prince de Royans (d.1119)
2 CONT #     * Ermentrude, married (1065) Theodoric I
2 CONT #     * (perhaps) Bertha wife of Alphonso VI of Castile
2 CONT #     * Guillaume
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by Renaud I 	Count of Burgundy 1057 – 1087 	Succeeded by Renaud II
2 CONT
2 CONT Note
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. ^ She was identified as the daughter of Adalbert, Duke of Lorraine in an article by Szabolcs de Vajay in Annales de Bourgogne, XXXII:247-267 (Oct-Dec 1960), but the author subsequently made an unqualified retraction of this cla
2 CONC im in "Parlons encore d'Etiennette" in Prosopographica et Genealogica, vol. 3: Onomastique et Parenté dans l'Occident medieval, K. S. B. Keats-Rohan and C. Settipani, eds. (2000), pp. 2-6.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT * Portail sur Histoire Bourgogne et Histoire Franche-Comté, Gilles Maillet.
2 CONT * FMG on William I, Comté de Burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guillaume_Ier_de_Bourgogne
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume Ier de Bourgogne dit Guillaume le Grand ou Tête Hardie (1020-1087) comte de Bourgogne, comte de Mâcon et père du Pape Calixte II
2 CONT
2 CONT Biographie
2 CONT
2 CONT Né le 1020, fils du comte Renaud Ier de Bourgogne et d'Adélaïde de Normandie (fille du duc Richard II de Normandie).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1057 il succède le 3 septembre 1057 à son père qui décède. Guillaume Ier de Bourgogne et ses fils aînés Renaud II de Bourgogne puis Étienne Ier de Bourgogne, sont des comtes de Bourgogne très puissants, régnant sur des terres dépa
2 CONC ssant largement les limites du puissant et vaste comté de Bourgogne. Ils sont vassaux contre leur gré de l'empire germanique suite au testament du roi Rodolphe III de Bourgogne mort en 1032 et à la guerre de succession de Bourgogn
2 CONC e (1032-1034).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1039 l'archevêque de Besançon, Hugues Ier de Salins, devient l'homme de confiance favori du nouvel empereur germanique, Henri III du Saint-Empire (neveu du précédent). L'empereur accorde alors une certaine autonomie franche et le 
2 CONC droit de s'auto-administrer par son propre gouvernement au comté de Bourgogne dont il est nommé chancelier et récompensé très largement pour sa totale et très dévouée collaboration et pour ses services de vassal à son suzerain.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1043 l'empereur germanique Henri III vient à Besançon, pour se fiancer avec Agnès d'Aquitaine, nièce du comte Renaud Ier de Bourgogne, et fille du duc Guillaume V d’Aquitaine. Pour cette occasion, l’archevêque de Besançon, Hugues 
2 CONC Ier de Salins, obtient des droits régaliens sur la ville de Besançon (droits juridiques, politiques, fiscaux et économiques ... ) Il est nommé prince de l’empire germanique (rang maximum avant empereur) et règne en souverain sur l
2 CONC a cité lui et ses futurs successeurs avec l'empereur et le pape Grégoire VII pour seuls supérieurs. Il échappe au pouvoir des comtes de Bourgogne
2 CONT
2 CONT 1076 l'empereur germanique Henri IV du Saint-Empire s'oppose aux pouvoirs absolus du pape Grégoire VII et se voit excommunié par le Vatican ce qui le discrédite profondément dans l'Europe profondément chrétienne où le pape a un gr
2 CONC and pouvoir sur les têtes couronnées d'alors. C'est le début de la lutte de pouvoir entre l'empereur germanique et le Vatican (querelle des Investitures).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1078 le comte Guy II de Mâcon se fait moine à l'Abbaye de Cluny et cède son titre et ses terres à son cousin Guillaume Ier de Bourgogne.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1085 Guillaume Ier de Bourgogne s’affirme comme le personnage le plus important du comté de Bourgogne et met la main sur le pouvoir ecclésiastique après le décès des puissants archevêques de Besançon, Hugues Ier de Salins et Hugue
2 CONC s II en y faisant ordonner ses fils Hugues III de Bourgogne archevêque et Guy de Bourgogne (futur Pape sous le nom de Calixte II) administrateur du diocèse de son frère.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1087 il décède à Besançon à l'age de 67 ans et est inhumé à la cathédrale Saint-Étienne, remplacée au XVIIIe siècle par la Cathédrale Saint-Jean, où furent transférées les sépultures des comtes de Bourgogne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ses fils Renaud II de Bourgogne et Étienne Ier de Bourgogne lui succèdent et meurent en croisade en Terre Sainte, suivis en cela par leur frère Raymond de Bourgogne, roi de Leon et de Galice, ce qui affaiblira grandement le pouvoi
2 CONC r de leur famille.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mariages et enfants
2 CONT
2 CONT Il se marie en secondes noces entre 1049 et 1057 avec Étiennette. Il a eu les enfants suivants1 :
2 CONT * Eudes. Son père fait une donation à la cathédrale de Besançon en 1087 pour le repos de son âme.
2 CONT * Renaud II († 1097 en croisade), comte de Bourgogne.
2 CONT * Guillaume2.
2 CONT * Ermentrude mariée en 1065 à Thierry Ier, comte de Montbéliard ,d'Altkirch et de Ferrette.
2 CONT * Guy administrateur de l'Archevêché de Besançon puis élu 160 ème pape en 1119 sous le nom de Calixte II.
2 CONT * Étienne Ier (†1102 à Ascalon) comte de Bourgogne.
2 CONT * Sybille épouse en 1080 Eudes Ier, duc de Bourgogne
2 CONT * Raymond de Bourgogne († 1107 en Espagne) marié en 1090 à Urraque Ire, reine de Castille et de Léon.
2 CONT * Hugues († 1103).
2 CONT * Gisèle mariée en 1090 à Humbert II, comte de Savoie
2 CONT * Clémence (1078 † 1129) mariée en 1092 à Robert II, comte de Flandre, puis vers 1125 à Godefroid Ier, duc de Brabant.
2 CONT * Étiennette, épouse le Lambert François, de Valence seigneur de Royans.
2 CONT * Berthe († 1097) épouse en 1093 Alphonse VI (1040 † 1109), roi de Castille et de Léon.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Savoie (Savoy) is a region of southeastern France that extends from Lake Geneva to the Isère River and borders on the Italian frontier.  Its command of the western Alpine passes into Italy enhances its strategic importance. Savoy 
2 CONC was the original domain of the Savoy dynasty, which ruled Italy from 1861 to 1946.
2 CONT
2 CONT Savoy's early Celtic inhabitants were conquered by the Romans in 121 BC. During the 5th century AD the Burgundians gained control of the region, which passed in 534 to the Frankish kingdom of Burgundy. Savoy came under the suzerai
2 CONC nty of the Holy Roman emperor in 1033. Count Humbert I aux Blanches Mains (the Whitehanded), founder of the House of Savoy and a vassal of the German emperor, then controlled much of the region. He settled in Chambéry and began ex
2 CONC tracting exorbitant tolls from neighboring kings who wanted to march through the pass. By the 14th century, this powerful kingdom included Nice, the Jura, Piedmonte, and Geneva.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Savoy dukes increasingly favored their Italian lands, particularly since generations of French monarchs had expressed military designs on Savoy, which was largely French-speaking. The dukes transferred their capital to Piedmon
2 CONC te in 1563. France annexed Savoy in 1792, but it was restored to the House of Savoy in 1815. In 1860, however, after a plebiscite, the region was returned to France, and the French acquiesced to the rule of the House of Savoy over
2 CONC  a kingdom in north central Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivrea is a town and commune of the province of Turin in the Piedmont region of northwestern Italy. Situated on the road leading to the Aosta Valley (part of the medieval Via Francigena), it straddles the Dora Baltea and is regarde
2 CONC d as the centre of the Canavese area. Ivrea lies in a basin that, in prehistoric times, formed a great lake.  The town first appears in history as a cavalry station of the army of the Roman Empire, founded in 100 BC and set to gua
2 CONC rd one of the traditional invasion routes into northern Italy over the Alps. The Latin name of the town was Eporedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Cathedral of Ivrea.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the fall of the Western Roman Empire, Ivrea became seat of a duchy under the Lombards (6th-8th centuries). Alessandro Manzoni in his Adelchi, names one duke Guinigi of Ivrea, chosen by king Desiderius as defender of Pavia. U
2 CONC nder the Franks (9th century), Ivrea was a county capital. In the year 1001, after a period of disputes with bishop Warmund, ruler of the city, Arduin conquered March of Ivrea. Later he became King of Italy and set a dynasty that 
2 CONC lasted until the 11th century, when the city switched again to the bishops' suzerainty.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following century Ivrea became a free commune, but succumbed in the first decades of the 13th century. In 1238 Emperor Frederick II put it under his domains. Later Ivrea was disputed between the bishops, the marquis of Monferr
2 CONC ato and the House of Savoy. In 1356 Ivrea was acquired by Amadeus VI of Savoy. With the exception of the brief French conquest at the end of the 16th century, Ivrea remained under Savoy until 1800: on May 26 of that year Napoleon 
2 CONC Bonaparte entered the city along with his victorious troops, establishing control that ended in 1814 after his fall.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burgundy (French: Bourgogne; German: Burgund) is a region historically situated in modern-day France and Switzerland.
2 CONT
2 CONT History
2 CONT
2 CONT Burgundy was inhabited in turn by Celts, Romans (Gallo-Romans), and in the 4th century assigned by Romans to the Burgundians, a Germanic people, who settled there and established their own kingdom. This Burgundian kingdom was conq
2 CONC uered in the 6th century by the Franks who continued the kingdom of Burgundy under their own rule.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, the region was divided between the Duchy of Burgundy (west of Burgundy) and the County of Burgundy (east of Burgundy). The Duchy of Burgundy is the more famous of the two, and the one which reached historical fame. Later, t
2 CONC he Duchy of Burgundy became the French province of Burgundy, while the County of Burgundy became the French province of Franche-Comté, literally meaning free county.
2 CONT
2 CONT The modern-day administrative région of Bourgogne comprises most of the former Duchy of Burgundy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Burgundians were one of the Germanic peoples who filled the power vacuum left by the collapse of the western half of the Roman Empire. In A.D. 411, they crossed the Rhine and established a kingdom at Worms. Amidst repeated cla
2 CONC shes between the Romans and Huns, the Burgundian kingdom eventually occupied what is today the borderlands between Switzerland, France, and Italy. In 534, the Franks defeated Godomar, the last Burgundian king, and absorbed the ter
2 CONC ritory into their growing empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burgundy's modern existence is rooted in the dissolution of the Frankish Empire. When the dynastic succession was settled in the 880s, there were four Burgundies:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. the Kingdom of Upper (Transjurane) Burgundy around Lake Geneva,
2 CONT    2. the Kingdom of Lower Burgundy in Provence, and
2 CONT    3. the Duchy of Burgundy west of the Saône
2 CONT    4. the County of Burgundy east of the Saône
2 CONT
2 CONT The two kingdoms of Upper and Lower Burgundy were reunited in 937 and absorbed into the Holy Roman Empire under Conrad II in 1032, as the Kingdom of Arles. The Duchy of Burgundy was annexed by the French throne in 1477. The County
2 CONC  of Burgundy remained loosely associated with the Holy Roman Empire (intermittently independent, whence the name "Franche-Comté"), and finally incorporated into France in 1678, with the Treaties of Nijmegen.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Middle Ages, Burgundy was the seat of some of the most important Western churches and monasteries, among them Cluny, Citeaux, and Vézelay. During the Hundred Years' War, King John II of France gave the duchy to his youn
2 CONC ger son, rather than leaving it to his successor on the throne. The duchy soon became a major rival to the French throne, because the Dukes of Burgundy succeeded in assembling an empire stretching from Switzerland to the North Sea
2 CONC , mostly by marriage. The Burgundian territories consisted of a number of fiefdoms on both sides of the (then largely symbolic) border between the Kingdom of France and the Holy Roman Empire. Its economic heartland was in the Low 
2 CONC Countries, particularly Flanders and Brabant. The court in Dijon outshone the French court by far, both economically and culturally. In Belgium and in the south of the Netherlands, a 'Burgundian lifestyle' still means 'enjoyment o
2 CONC f life, good food, and extravagant spectacle'.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the late 15th and early 16th centuries, Burgundy provided a power base for the rise of the Habsburgs, after Maximilian of Austria had married into the ducal family. In 1477 at the battle of Nancy during the Burgundian Wars the 
2 CONC last duke Charles the Bold was killed in battle and Burgundy itself taken back by France. After the death of his daughter Mary her husband Maximilian moved the court first to Mechelen and later to the palace at Coudenberg, Brussel
2 CONC s, and from there ruled the remnants of the empire, the Low Countries (Burgundian Netherlands) and Franche-Comté, then still an imperial fief. The latter territory was ceded to France in the Treaty of Nijmegen of 1678.
2 CONT --------------------
