1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Medlands does not show her named "Anne".'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ====FMG: Burgundy [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BURGUNDY.htm#MargueriteBourgognedied1242 Dukes]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Duke Hugues III & his second wife had [three] children:'''...
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. '''MARGUERITE de Bourgogne''' ([1192]–[1228/30] or [1242]). Valbonnais cites the testament of "''la duchesse Beatrix" dated 1228 which names "son héritier le Dauphin André son fils…Matilde sa fille aînée femme de Jean Comte de 
2 CONC Chalon…Marguerite son autre fille épouse d´Amedée fils du comte de Savoye''"[389]. The testament of "''Guigo Dalphinus, Vienn. et Albonis comitis''", dated 27 Jun 1267, confirmed donations made by "…''matertera Margarita vel Domin
2 CONC a Ducissa avia mea…meæ amitæ quondam comitissæ Sabaudiæ''"[390]. It is not clear from this document whether "''matertera Margarita''" and "''meæ amitæ quondam comitissæ Sabaudiæ''" refer to the same person as they are named in the
2 CONC  context of two separate donations in different parts of the testament. The use of "''matertera''" and "''amitæ''" as two different terms to express the relationship "aunt" suggests that they may have been different individuals. V
2 CONC albonnais assumes that they were the same person, "''Marguerite sœur du dauphin André''" who married "''Amé fils de Thomas Comte de Savoie''", and adds that "''la duchesse Beatrix''" named "''son héritier le Dauphin André son fils
2 CONC …Matilde sa fille aînée femme de Jean Comte de Chalon…Marguerite son autre fille épouse d´Amedée fils du comte de Savoye''" in her testament dated 1228[391]. If the suggested second marriage of Comte Amedée is correctly shown in t
2 CONC he document SAVOY, Marguerite de Bourgogne must have died in [1228/30]. If it is incorrect, her date of death is estimated to [1242]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (1222) as his first wife, [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SAVOY.htm#AmedeeIVdied1253 '''AMEDEE de Savoie'''], son of THOMAS I Comte de Savoie & his wife Marguerite {Béatrix} de Genève (Montmélian, Savoie 1197–Montmélian 2
2 CONC 4 Jun or 13 Jul 1253, bur Hautecombe, abbaye royale de Sainte-Marie). He succeeded his father in 1233 as AMEDEE IV Comte de Savoie.
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Marguerite of Burgundy, Countess of Savoy
2 CONT ([https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marguerite_of_Burgundy,_Countess_of_Savoy Wikipedia])
2 CONT
2 CONT * Born	1192
2 CONT * Died	1243
2 CONT * Noble family	House of Burgundy
2 CONT * Spouse(s)	Amadeus IV, Count of Savoy
2 CONT * Father	Hugh III, Duke of Burgundy
2 CONT * Mother	Béatrice of Albon
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite of Burgundy (referred to as Anne by some sources; 1192–1243), was, by marriage, Countess of Savoy from 1233 until her death. She was the youngest daughter of Hugh III, Duke of Burgundy, and his second wife, Béatrice of 
2 CONC Albon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1217, she married Amadeus IV, Count of Savoy. As dowry, she brought Miribel castle, Ornacieux castle, and other properties in Viennois territory. The marriage was agreed to as part of a treaty between the families, includin
2 CONC g terms not to acquire further territories within each other's dominions. She and her new husband were also designated as heirs for her brother Guigues VI of Viennois after any children of his.[1] However, this peace between the f
2 CONC amilies did not last long, with conflict resuming by 1228.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT She also personally inherited some money upon the death of her mother. The 1267 will of her nephew Guigues VII of Viennois confirmed donations of property which she had made.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite and Amadeus had two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice of Savoy (d. 1258), married firstly in 1233 to Manfred III of Saluzzo (d. 1244), married secondly on April 21, 1247 to Manfred of Sicily
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret of Savoy (d. 1254), married firstly on December 9, 1235 to Boniface II of Montferrat, married secondly to Aymar III, Count of Valentinois
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that some scholars are unclear on Marguerite's given name. A book about the house of Savoy at this time mentions the debate regarding her name,[1] but finds a source[3] which Cox finds settles the debate.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT #  Cox 1974, p. 24.
2 CONT #  Cox 1974, p. 29.
2 CONT #  Chevalier, U. ed. (1913-1926). Regeste dauphinois, Valence, Vienne. no. 6973
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References===
2 CONT
2 CONT Cox, Eugene L. (1974). The Eagles of Savoy. Princeton: Princeton University Press. ISBN 0691052166.
2 CONT =---------------------------=
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00049931&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne de Bourgogne (1192 † 1243), fille d'Hugues III, duc de Bourgogne et d'Agnès de Lorraine. Elle épousa en 1222 Amédée IV de Savoie. Ils eurent :
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Béatrice de Savoie (1223-1259), mariée en 1233 à Manfred III, marquis de Saluces (1210-1244), puis en 1247 à Manfred (1232-1266), roi de Sicile.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Marguerite († 1254), mariée en 1235 à Boniface II († 1253), marquis de Montferrat, puis Aymar III de Poitiers († 1277), comte de Valentinois.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh III, Duke of Burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT By his second marriage, he produced:
2 CONT
2 CONT André Guigues VI (1184 - 1237), Dauphin of Viennois
2 CONT
2 CONT Mahaut (1190 - 1242), married in 1214 John I, Count of Châlon and Auxonne (1190 - 1267)
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne (1192 - 1243), married in 1222 Amadeus IV (1197 - 1253), Count of Savoy
2 CONT
