2 CONT a)         PIETRO Ziani (-Santa Giustina 13 Mar 1230, bur Venice San Giorgio Maggiore).  The Historia Ducum Veneticorum names "patri sui [=Pietro Ziani] Sebastiani Ziani", recording that they were both buried "in cenobio Sancti Ge
2 CONC orgii maioris"[220].  He was elected Doge of Venice in 1205.  Andrea Dandulo´s Chronicon Venetum records the election of "Petrus Ziano dux…ex patre Sebastiano duce genitus" 5 Aug 1205[221].  Andrea Navagiero´s Storia della Repubbl
2 CONC ica Veneziana records that "Pietro Ziani" was elected Doge in 1205[222].  He abdicated in 1229.  Andrea Navagiero´s Storia della Repubblica Veneziana records that "Pietro Ziani" retired "alle sue case a Santa Giustina", died there
2 CONC  one month later, and was buried "a San Giorgio Maggiore"[223].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' firstly MARIA, daughter of ---.  The Historia Ducum Veneticorum names "Maria dukissa, de domo Basiliorum" as first wife of Pietro Ziani[224].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' secondly (1213) CONSTANCE of Sicily, daughter of TANCRED di Lecce King of Sicily & his wife Sibilla di Medania.  The Historia Ducum Veneticorum names "Constanciam…Tancredi regis Sycilie filiam" as second wife of Pietro Zia
2 CONC ni[225]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
2 CONT - https://de.zxc.wiki/wiki/Pietro_Ziani_(Doge)_
2 CONT
2 CONT Pietro Ziani (* approx. 1153/1155 ; † March 14, 1229 in Venice ), the son of Sebastiano Ziani , was the 42nd Doge of Venice from 1205 to 1229 . 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Pietro Ziani came from a Venetian family that quickly produced two doges, Pietro himself and his father Sebastiano . After a gradual social and economic rise from the second half of the 11th century, the family only gained politic
2 CONC al importance in the 12th and 13th centuries. She lived in the parish of S. Giustina with great, downright incredible wealth. The last important representative was Marco, the son of Doge Pietro, who died young. After his death in 
2 CONC 1254 there were only insignificant branch lines in the family. The Venetian opera composers of the late 17th century Pietro Andrea Ziani (1616–1684) and Marcantonio Ziani (1653–1715) have nothing to do with the noble Doge family. 
2 CONC Not to be confused with the Ziani family are the Venetian aristocratic families Zeno or Geno and Zane.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Pietro was probably the older son of Sebastiano Ziani and his wife Froyza; his siblings were Giacomo and Mabiliota. Pietro and his brother have been running his father's business since 1173. In 1177 they dissolved their economic c
2 CONC ommunity, and from then on each ran alone. Pietro Ziani married Maria (probably from the Basilio / Baseggio family) who died around 1221 or shortly before that. The marriage remained childless. In his second marriage he married Ko
2 CONC nstanze, daughter of King Tancred of Sicily, around 1221 . From the marriage came the son Marco and the daughters Marchesina, married Badoer, and Maria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pietro Ziani was considered the richest Venetian of his time. He had inherited a large part of his fortune from his father Sebastiano, who had acquired it mainly through long-distance trading (see Venice's economic history ). Piet
2 CONC ro was also active in long-distance trade, but also had rich property in the center of Venice (tenement houses), on the islands of the Venetian lagoon (salt pans, vineyards) and the mainland (farms, mills).
2 CONT
2 CONT In September 1228 he made his will, in which he considered nearly a hundred Venetian churches, monasteries and hospitals, but also numerous relatives and friends. The main heir was his underage son Marco. In February 1229 the doge
2 CONC  is said to have abdicated. He died on March 14, 1229. He was buried in the church of the monastery of San Giorgio Maggiore in his father's sarcophagus. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Political career'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Pietro Ziani gained his first political and diplomatic experience in the run-up to the Peace of Venice, which in 1177 between Pope Alexander III. and Emperor Friedrich I (Barbarossa) in Venice: he received the Pope together with h
2 CONC is brother Giacomo in the monastery of San Nicolò on the Lido and escorted him to the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT A large number of political offices and diplomatic missions followed: in 1184 he was envoy of Doge Orio Mastropiero in Constantinople, 1184 to 1197 Vogt of the monastery of San Giorgio Maggiore. He was iudex , high official of the
2 CONC  city and political advisor to the Doge in various years between 1186 and 1203, since 1190 Count of [https://www.geni.com/projects/Rab/41398 Rab] (Italian Arbe), the island off the Dalmatian coast under Venetian rule. In 1192 he b
2 CONC elonged to the electoral college that Enrico Dandolo elected Doge, in 1201 he became Podestà of the neighboring city of Padua and in 1205 a member of the Small Council .
2 CONT
2 CONT On August 5, 1205, Pietro Ziani was elected Doge, succeeding Enrico Dandolo, under whose significant participation the crusaders conquered the cities of Zara and Constantinople during the Fourth Crusade. Pietro Ziani allegedly mad
2 CONC e the daring suggestion to move Venice to Constantinople, as its location was strategically much more favorable than that of the lagoon city, which is equally threatened by land and sea . The Venice Grand Council allegedly rejecte
2 CONC d the proposal by a majority of only one vote.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his tenure, he largely successfully overcame the problems that the victorious outcome of the Fourth Crusade brought with it for Venice: the founding and expansion of the Venetian colonial empire in the Levant, battles again
2 CONC st rivals Genoa and Pisa and against the Byzantine Empire of Nikaia for control of trade in the Aegean and Black Seas.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1224, the first Venetian water authority was established under Ziani, which after 1500 became the collegio solenne delle acque . It was their job to keep the canals free of mud and to make them navigable. In 1226 he brought the
2 CONC  Franciscans to Venice and he was the founder of the San Lazaro Hospice for lepers . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ester Pastorello (Ed.): Andrea Dandolo, Chronica per extensum descripta aa. 460-1280 dC , (= Rerum Italicarum Scriptores XII, 1), Nicola Zanichelli, Bologna 1938, pp. 281-291. ( [https://archive.org/details/rerumitalicarums121card
2 CONC /page/280/mode/2up Digitized, p. 280 f.] )
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Literature, articles, sources''':
2 CONT * Silvano Borsari: Una famiglia veneziana del medioevo: gli Ziani , in: Archivio Veneto 145 (1978) 27-72.
2 CONT * Irmgard Fees: Wealth and Power in Medieval Venice. The Ziani family , De Gruyter, 1988.
2 CONT * [https://books.google.hr/books?id=3AsoDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA53&lpg=PA53&dq=pietro+ziani+rab&source=bl&ots=Sz_vFuNS2n&sig=ACfU3U2-qyl3PR6Rp1_CDN4m_Pnk9nY-IQ&hl=hr&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjE2rvvmIDwAhVyh_0HHU8ZD6oQ6AEwD3oECBMQAw#v=onepage&q=pietr
2 CONC o%20ziani%20rab&f=false Die Münzen und Medaillen Venedigs], author Manfred Miller, p. 53
2 CONT * https://memim.com/pietro-ziani.html
2 CONT * [https://hrcak.srce.hr/index.php?show=clanak&id_clanak_jezik=329150 The Cases of Rovinj and Rab], pdf (eng), authors: Dušan Mlacović, Marija Mogorović Crljenko, Danijela Doblanović Šuran, p. 171
2 CONT * https://morganirich.tripod.com/id2.html
2 CONT * [https://www.conoscerevenezia.it/?p=22927 Famiglia Ziani]
2 CONT
