1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Berenguela I de Castilla De Wikipedia, la enciclopedia libre http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berenguela_I_de_Castilla#
2 CONT
2 CONT Berenguela I de Castilla (* Segovia, 1 de junio de 1180 - † Monasterio de las Huelgas, Burgos, 8 de noviembre de 1246). Fue Reina de Castilla[2] en 1217 y reina consorte de León entre 1197 y 1204.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hija primogénita del rey castellano Alfonso VIII y de su esposa, Leonor Plantagenet, era bisnieta de otra Berenguela, la esposa de Alfonso VII de Castilla y hermana de Ramón Berenguer IV de Barcelona, quien introdujo ese nombre ca
2 CONC talán en la familia real castellana. Por línea materna era nieta de Enrique II de Inglaterra y de otra importante mujer de la época, Leonor de Aquitania.
2 CONT
2 CONT En el momento de su nacimiento, Berenguela era la única hija de los reyes, ya que los infantes nacidos con anterioridad no habían sobrevivido, por lo que era la heredera nominal al trono castellano y la convertía en un partido muy
2 CONC  deseado en toda Europa.
2 CONT
2 CONT El primer compromiso matrimonial de Berenguela se acuerda en 1187 cuando pide su mano Conrado, duque de Rothenburg y quinto hijo del emperador germánico Federico I Barbarroja. Al año siguiente, en Seeligenstadt, se firma el contra
2 CONC to matrimonial, tras lo cual Conrado marchó a Castilla, donde en la ciudad de Carrión se celebraron los esponsales y el joven conde fue armado caballero.
2 CONT
2 CONT El matrimonio no llegó a consumarse, en un primer momento por la edad de Berenguela y después porque los reyes tuvieron en 1189 un hijo varón, Fernando, que pasó a ser designado heredero al trono, lo que provocó que el emperador F
2 CONC ederico, al ver frustradas sus aspiraciones hacia Castilla y a pesar de la dote de 42.000 aurii de la infanta, perdiera todo interés en mantener la boda de su hijo, el cual no volvería a encontrarse jamás con Berenguela. Esta soli
2 CONC citó al Papa la anulación del compromiso, seguramente influenciada por agentes externos, como su abuela Leonor de Aquitania, a quien no interesaba tener a un Hohenstaufen como vecino de sus feudos franceses. Pero estos temores se 
2 CONC verían posteriormente neutralizados cuando el duque fue asesinado en 1196.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dos años más tarde (1197), Berenguela se casó en la ciudad de Valladolid con el Rey de León Alfonso IX, pariente suyo en tercer grado (era su tío segundo). De este matrimonio nacerán cinco hijos:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Berenguela (1204 - 1235), casada con Juan de Brienne, Rey-regente de Jerusalén;
2 CONT *Constanza (1200 - 1242), monja en el monasterio de las Huelgas;
2 CONT *Fernando III el Santo (1201 - 1252);
2 CONT *Leonor (1198 - 1202);
2 CONT *Alfonso (1203 - 1272), Señor de Molina y Mesa por su primer matrimonio. Se casó sucesivamente con Mafalda de Lara, heredera de Molina y Mesa, con Teresa Nuñez y con Mayor Téllez de Meneses, Señora de Montealegre y Tiedra –de este
2 CONC  último enlace nacería la célebre María de Molina, esposa de Sancho IV de León y Castilla–. Pero en 1204, el Papa Inocencio III anuló el matrimonio alegando el parentesco de los cónyuges, a pesar de que Celestino III lo había perm
2 CONC itido en su momento. Ésta era la segunda anulación tanto para Berenguela como para Alfonso, que solicitaron vehementemente una dispensa para permanecer juntos. Pero este Papa fue uno de los más duros en cuestiones matrimoniales, a
2 CONC sí que se les denegó, aunque consiguieron que su descendencia fuese considerada como legítima.
2 CONT
2 CONT Disuelto el lazo matrimonial, Berenguela regresa a Castilla al lado de sus padres, donde se dedicó al cuidado de sus hijos.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Berengaria (Castilian: Berengaria; 1180 – 8 November 1246), was briefly queen of Castile and León. The eldest daughter of Alfonso VIII of Castile and Eleanor of England, she was briefly engaged to Conrad II, Duke of Swabia, but he
2 CONC  was murdered in 1196 before they could be married.
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengaria married King Alfonso IX of León in 1198, but this was annulled in 1204 by Pope Innocent III because they were second cousins. Berengaria and Alfonso had five children, including one who died in infancy, and when she ret
2 CONC urned to her father's court in Castile, she brought her children with her to Otella.
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengaria often found herself politically at odds with her former husband. Alfonso had two daughters, Sancha and Dulce, by his first wife, Theresa of Portugal, and wished to disinherit Berengaria's children in favor of these daug
2 CONC hters. To this end, he invited John of Brienne to marry his eldest daughter, Sancha, and thus inherit his kingdom. Berengaria sabotaged this plan by convincing John of Brienne to marry her own daughter, Berengaria of Leon, instead
2 CONC . Later, on 24 September 1230 when Alfonso died, Berengaria and Ferdinand acted to set aside the rights of Sancha and Dulce by offering them a lifetime appanage, which they accepted. This was done so that, with Berengaria's aid, h
2 CONC e could assume the Leonese throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Her children with Alfonso IX included:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Eleanor (1198/1199-31 October 1210)
2 CONT     * King Ferdinand III of Castile (1200-1252)
2 CONT     * Alfonso, 4th Lord of Molina (1203-1272)
2 CONT     * Berengaria of Leon (1204-1237), married John of Brienne
2 CONT     * Constance (1 May 1200 or 1205-7 September 1242), became a nun at Las Huelgas, Burgos, where she died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Shadis, Miriam. Berenguela of Castile's Political Motherhood, 1996
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Berenguela (or Berengaria) (1180 – November 8, 1246), was briefly queen of Castile and León. The eldest daughter of Alfonso VIII of Castile and Leonora of England, she was briefly engaged to Conrad II, Duke of Swabia, but he was m
2 CONC urdered in 1196 before they could be married.
2 CONT
2 CONT Berenguela married Alfonso IX of Leon of León in 1198, but this was annulled in 1204 by Pope Innocent III because they were second cousins. Berenguela and Alfonso had five children, including one who died in infancy, and when she 
2 CONC returned to her father's court in Castile, she brought her children with her to Otella.
2 CONT
2 CONT Berenguela often found herself politically at odds with her former husband. Alfonso had two daughters, Sancha and Dulce, by his first wife, Teresa of Portugal, and wished to disinherit Berenguela's children in favor of these daugh
2 CONC ters. To this end, he invited John of Brienne to marry his eldest daughter, Sancha, and thus inherit John's kingdom. Berenguela sabotaged this plan by convincing John of Brienne to marry her own daughter, also named Berenguela, in
2 CONC stead. Later, on September 24, 1230 when Alfonso died, Berenguela and Fernando acted to set aside the rights of Sancha and Dulce by offering them a lifetime appanage, which they accepted. This was done so that, with Berengueala's 
2 CONC aid, he could assume the Leonese throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT When her brother Henry died by accident in 1217, Berenguela renounced her rights to the throne, in favor of her son Fernando. Thereafter she served as the king's motherly advisor; according to the Cronica Latina, her "total intent
2 CONC  and desire being to procure honor for her son in every way possible". Berenguela helped quell the rebellious nobles, and then arranged for Fernando to marry a high-born wife, Beatriz (Beatrix) of Swabia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Berenguela maintained strong connections with her sister Blanca, who was Queen of France. It was Blanche who suggested sending Jeanne of Ponthieu as a bride for Fernando after his first wife's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Berengaria (Castilian: Berenguela; 1 January/June 1180 – 8 November 1246), was briefly queen of Castile and León. The eldest daughter of Alfonso VIII of Castile and Eleanor of England, she was briefly engaged to Conrad II, Duke of
2 CONC  Swabia, but he was murdered in 1196 before they could be married.
2 CONT
