2 CONT Jeanne of Dammartin or Joan of Dammartin (b. 1229 – d. Abbeville, March 16, 1279) Queen consort of Castile and León (1252), Countess of Ponthieu (1237-1279) and Montreuil.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was daughter of Simon de Dammartin, Count of Ponthieu (1251-1276) and his wife Marie of Ponthieu, Countess of Montreuil (1221-1251). In 1237 she married in Burgos with King Ferdinand III of Castile and Leon (1198/1199-1252).
2 CONT
2 CONT They had four sons and one daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand (1239–1269), Count of Aumale 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor, married Edward I of England 
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis (1243–1269) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Simon (1244), died young and buried in a monastery in Toledo 
2 CONT
2 CONT John (1245), died young and buried at the cathedral in Córdoba 
2 CONT
2 CONT aka Joana, Joanna. Alternate birthdate 1208. Alternate deathplace Abbeville, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Question as to whether she was married to anyone apart from Fernando III or Jean (see below)
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/NORTHERN%20FRANCE.htm
2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT The image depicts a portrait of Jeanne de Dammartin (c. 1220 – March 16, 1279), also known as Joan, Countess of Ponthieu, and Queen consort of Castile and León. The partial inscription "IOHA REGI VXO E" in the image likely transla
2 CONC tes from Latin as "IOHANNA REGINA UXOR E" (Joanna, Queen, wife of), confirming her identity as a queen. 
2 CONT Here's more about Jeanne de Dammartin:
2 CONT Queen Consort and Countess: She was Queen of Castile and León by marriage to Ferdinand III of Castile and also ruled in her own right as Countess of Ponthieu (from 1251) and Aumale (from 1237).
2 CONT Family: She was the eldest daughter of Simon of Dammartin and Marie de Ponthieu. Her most notable child was Eleanor of Castile, who later became Queen consort of England through her marriage to King Edward I.
2 CONT Life Events: After Ferdinand III's death in 1252, she had a strained relationship with her stepson Alfonso X and eventually returned to Ponthieu. She later married Jean de Nesle and managed her inherited counties, notably dealing 
2 CONC with significant debts upon her death.
2 CONT Significance: Her life and marriages played a role in the complex political landscape of medieval France, Castile, and England, particularly concerning control over her inherited territories like Ponthieu which bordered Normandy. 
2 CONT
