2 NOTE The image displays the Coat of Arms of Castile, a significant heraldic emblem historically associated with the Kingdom of Castile and its monarchs. 
3 CONT Here's more information about this coat of arms:
3 CONT Description (Blazon): The arms feature a golden (Or) triple-towered castle, with black (Sable) masonry and blue (Azure) windows and port, set against a red (Gules) field.
3 CONT Origin and Significance: This symbol represents the Kingdom of Castile, a major medieval kingdom on the Iberian Peninsula that played a crucial role in the formation of Spain. The castle emblem is specifically attributed to Alfons
3 CONC o VIII of Castile, who reigned in the late 12th and early 13th centuries.
3 CONT Union with León: In 1230, Ferdinand III of Castile united the Kingdom of Castile with the Kingdom of León, creating the Crown of Castile. To symbolize this union, the Castilian arms were often quartered with the arms of León (a pu
3 CONC rple lion).
3 CONT Modern Usage: The original elements of the Castilian arms continue to be incorporated into modern heraldry. They are prominently featured in the coat of arms of the Spanish autonomous community of Castile and León, and are also a 
3 CONC part of the national coat of arms of Spain. Its symbolism has also been used in the heraldry of various cities and territories historically linked to the Crown of Castile, including some in the Americas. 
