1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1250-1315 Sovereign Countess Marguerite   de Neuilly   of Passava and Akova (Greece)
2 CONT
2 CONT The county was known as Passe-avant at the time, and she succeeded her father, Jean II.  She first married Guibert de Cors, then Guglielmo da Verona, Lord   of that part of Eubœa around Oreos and thirdly to  Jean de Saint-Omer of 
2 CONC Thebes. She was sent as a hostage to Constantinople in 1262 to secure the release of Guillaume de Villehardouin Prince of Achaia, whose niece she was. In Akova she was the heiress of her uncle Gautier II de Rozières, baron of Akov
2 CONC a (Matagrifon) but was unable to claim her inheritance within 2 years and 2 days due to her absence and Prince Guillaume therefore declared her inheritance forfeit. Her third husband demanded the return of her inheritance, which w
2 CONC as refused by a specially summoned parliament at Glarentza.  Prince Guillaume granted 1/3 of the barony to Marguerite as a concession, granting the other two-thirds to his own daughter Marguerite. She was daughter of Jean de Neuil
2 CONC ly, Baron of Passava
2 CONT
