2 CONT King Pedro III & his wife had six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         Infante don ALFONSO de Aragón (Valencia 4 Nov 1265-Barcelona 18 Jun 1291, bur Barcelona Franciscan Monastery).  The Crónica de San Juan de la Peña names "el primero Alfonso et el otro Jayme et el otro Frederico" as the 
2 CONC sons of King Pedro and his wife Constanza[541].  The Historia Sicula of Bartolomeo di Neocastro names "Alfonsus, Elisabeth regina Portugalli…Rex Iacobus, Dominus Fridericus, domina Violanta et dominus Petrus" as the children of "P
2 CONC etro regi Aragonum" and his wife[542].  The Chronicle of Ramon Muntaner names "Alfonse, Jacques, Frédéric et Pierre" as the four sons of "le seigneur infant Pierre" and "la fille de Mainfroi roi de Sicile…Constance"[543].  He succ
2 CONC eeded his father in 1285 as ALFONSO III "el Liberal" King of Aragon and Valencia, Count of Barcelona.  After he succeeded, he continued his father’s campaign against the Balearic Islands to punish his uncle Jaime II King of Mallor
2 CONC ca, for the latter’s support of the French during the dispute over Sicily.  He recaptured  Mallorca city and Ibiza 1286, and captured Minorca from the Moors Jan 1287.  However, diplomatic tensions with France and the Papacy worsen
2 CONC ed, and eventually Alfonso was forced to withdraw his support for his brother at Tarascon Feb 1291 as the price for lifting sanctions.  He was forced to grant further privileges to the Aragonese Union 1287 (“el privilegio de la un
2 CONC ión”), after the latter invaded Valencia and placed an embargo on royal revenues.  The Crónica de San Juan de la Peña records the death in 1292 of King Alfonso aged 27 and his burial "en el monasterio de los frayres menores de Bar
2 CONC chinona"[544].  The Chronicle of Ramon Muntaner records the death of King Alfonso, dated to 1291, his burial "à l´ordre des frères mineurs à Barcelona", and adds that "il était parfaitement vierge, n´ayant jamais approché de femme
2 CONC "[545].  m (Betrothed [8 Oct 1272], by proxy Westminster Abbey 15 Aug 1290, not consummated) as her first husband, ELEANOR of England, daughter of EDWARD I King of England & his first wife Infanta doña Leonor de Castilla (Windsor 
2 CONC Castle before 17 Jun 1269-Ghent 12 Oct 1297, bur Westminster Abbey).  The marriage contract between “Edwardus...rex Angliæ...filiam suam majorem” and “infans Petrus...regis Aragonum primogenitus” is dated 8 Oct 1272[546].  Despite
2 CONC  the error of name, it is likely that this betrothal relates to the king´s known eldest son Alfonso, whom Eleanor later married by proxy, rather than an otherwise unrecorded older son named Pedro: no case has been found in the fam
2 CONC ily of the kings of Aragon where the oldest son of the king was named after his father.  A charter dated 19 Jun 1281 confirmed the marriage contract between “rex...filiæ nostræ” and “rege Aragoniæ...primogeniti sui”[547].  The Chr
2 CONC onicle of Ramon Muntaner records that Edward I King of England sent "Jean d´Agrilli" to Barcelona to negotiate the marriage of his daughter to Alfonso III King of Aragon, dated to 1286, and records the betrothal later the same yea
2 CONC r[548].  She married secondly (20 Sep 1393) Henri III Comte de Bar.  The Continuator of Florence of Worcester records the marriage "apud Bristoll vigilia S Matthaæi Apostoli" 20 Sep [1293] of "Alienora regis Angliæ flia primogenit
2 CONC a" and "domino Henrico comitis de Baroduc"[549].  Mistress: DULCE, daughter of BERNAT de Caldes, of Barcelona, & his wife ---.  Cingolani records her parentage and relationship with King Alfonso, citing a codicil dated 13 Jun 1291
2 CONC  under which the king requested that his heir “beneficiat Dulce” and take care of any child if she were pregnant, commenting that he found no record of any child being born[550]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         Infante don JAIME de Aragón (Valencia 10 Aug 1267-Barcelona 2 Nov 1327, bur Barcelona church of San Francisco, transferred to Monastery of Santa Cruz, prov Tarragona).  The Crónica de San Juan de la Peña names "el prime
2 CONC ro Alfonso et el otro Jayme et el otro Frederico" as the sons of King Pedro and his wife Constanza[551].  The Chronicle of Ramon Muntaner names "Alfonse, Jacques, Frédéric et Pierre" as the four sons of "le seigneur infant Pierre"
2 CONC  and "la fille de Mainfroi roi de Sicile…Constance"[552].  He succeeded his father in 1285 as GIACOPO King of Sicily.  He succeeded his brother in 1291 as JAIME II “el Justo” King of Aragon and Valencia, Conde de Barcelona. 
