1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_of_Portugal
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in 1271, Queen Isabel was married to King Diniz (or Dinis). King Diniz was ruling over Portugal when the Templars escaping from France came to him in search of sanctuary; later King Diniz would establish the Order of Christ w
2 CONC ith the same posessions and knights as the Templars, with the permission of Rome. His wife Isabel was extremely devoted to the Pentecost and the celebration of the Holy Ghost (where a child is put on the throne as Emperor of the W
2 CONC orld, accomplishing the Fifth Age or Empire as related in the dream of Nebuchadnezzar, as explained by the Prophet Daniel). Like her great-aunt Saint Elizabeth of Hungary, for whom she was named, Saint Isabel of Portugal dedicated
2 CONC  her life to the poor. She established orphanages and provided shelter for the homeless. She also founded a convent in Coimbra. There are many versions of the story of Queen Isabel’s miracle of turning bread into roses, but they a
2 CONC re all fundamentally the same. She is said to have been forbidden by her unfaithful husband to give to the poor. Having hidden bread to give away in her apron, she encountered King Diniz, who asked her what she was carrying. Not w
2 CONC anting to let on that the contents of her apron were meant for the poor, she responded that they were roses. The bread was transformed into roses, and King Dinis, who could not understand how she could have possession of fresh ros
2 CONC es in January, did not punish his wife. A similar legend is told about her great-aunt Elizabeth of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Known for settling disputes, Queen Isabel was called the Peacemaker. When her son Affonso (or Afonso) declared war on his father, jealous of the attention being paid by Diniz to his illegitimate sons, she rode between the armies, 
2 CONC reconciling the two sides. On another occasion, she rode to Estremoz, despite being ill, to keep the army of Affonso, by then Affonso IV, from fighting that of Castile. Affonso, angry at the mistreatment his daughter Maria was suf
2 CONC fering at the hands of her husband, the king of Castile, had ordered an attack. Isabel stopped the fighting, but the exertion proved to be too much for her and she fell ill, dying shortly thereafter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel was buried in Coimbra. She was canonized in 1625 by  Pope Urban VIII, and '''her feast day is July 4th'''. Many Portuguese and Portuguese-American organizations bear her name.
2 CONT
