1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Voir [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yolande_de_Bourgogne '''Wikipedia...''']
2 CONT * Assasine son beau-fils pour donner l'héritage à ses propres enfants. Est tuée à son tour par son mari furieux. Veuve sans descendance de son premier mari Jean "Tristan" de France
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BURGUNDY.htm#YolandeBourgognedied1280
2 CONT
2 CONT a)      '''   YOLANDE de Bourgogne''' ([1248/49]-2 Jun 1280, bur Nevers, église Saint-François).  The marriage contract between “Oedes fiuz le Duc de Bourgongne, cuens de Nevers et sires de Bourbon...Yolent nostre ainznée fille” a
2 CONC nd “Loys...roi de France...monseigneur Iehan son fil” is dated 8 Jun 1258[443].  Baronne de Donzy, Dame de Saint-Aignan.  The marriage contract between “Louis roi de France...Jean de France son fils” and “Yoland fille de Eudes com
2 CONC te de Nevers” is dated Jan 1265[444].  On the death of her mother in 1262, she claimed to succeed to the counties of Nevers, Auxerre and Tonnerre as the eldest daughter, but they were split between the three sisters by arrêt du pa
2 CONC rlement 1 Nov 1273, under which Yolande became Ctss de Nevers.  Hugues IV Duke of Burgundy, in light of dispute between “Ioannem filium...Ludovici Franc. regis et...neptem nostram Yolendim eius uxorem, filiam primogeniti filii nos
2 CONC tri Odonis”, and himself, ordered that Yolande be returned to her father until her husband was 21 years old by charter dated May 1266[445].  She claimed to succeed her grandfather as Dss of Burgundy in 1272, as the most senior mem
2 CONC ber of the family, but Philippe III King of France, who was appointed arbitrator, found in favour of her uncle Duke Robert II.  The Genealogia Comitum Flandriæ names "Yolendem filiam Odonis comitis Nivernensis et viduam Iohannis f
2 CONC ilii Ludovici regis Francie" as second wife of "Robertus primogenitus Guidonis", specifying that her husband obtained the county of Nevers by this marriage[446].  The Iohannis de Thilrode Chronicon names "Yolandem filiam Odonis co
2 CONC mites Nivernensis et viduam Iohannis filii Ludovici…regis Francie" as [second] wife of "Robertus primogenitus Guidonis et Mathilde"[447].  An arrêt of the Parliament dated 1 Nov 1273 addressed “dominus Ioannes de Cabilone miles...
2 CONC de parte Aalesin uxorem suam...Yolandim comitissam Niverrnensem [...Robertum de Flandria eius maritum] et Margaretam reginam Siciliæ sorores suas” in respect of the succession of “Mathildis quondam comitissæ Nivernensis matris suæ
2 CONC ”, ordered the partition of “Nivernensi, Altissiodorensi et Tornodorensi comitatib.”, under which Nevers was granted to Yolande, Tonnerre to Marguerite, and Auxerre to Alix[448].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' firstly (contract Vincennes 8 Jun 1258, Jun 1265) JEAN “Tristan” de France, son of LOUIS IX King of France & his wife Marguerite de Provence (Damiette, Egypt 8 Apr 1250-Tunis 2 or 3 Aug 1270, bur Saint-Denis).  Comte de Ne
2 CONC vers in 1265, by right of his wife.  He claimed to inherit the counties of Auxerre and Tonnerre on the death of his father-in-law.  Comte de Valois et de Crépy Mar 1268.  He died of dysentery on crusade in Tunis.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Betrothed'' (before 25 Aug 1271, contract terminated before Mar 1272) to Infante don JAIME de Aragón, son of JAIME I "el Conquistador" King of Aragon & his second wife Iolanda of Hungary (Barcelona [or Montpellier?] 1243-Palma d
2 CONC e Mallorca 29 May 1311).  This betrothal is confirmed by the following document: “infans Petrus” confirmed the property donations “in regno Mayorice et in comitatibus Rossilonis, Ceritanie et Confluentis et in villa Montispessulan
2 CONC i” made by “infans Jacobus, frater noster...racione donacionis propter nupcias seu doarii domine Yolendi, comitisse de Nevers” by charter dated 25 Aug 1271[449].  He succeeded his father in 1276 as JAIME II King of Mallorca.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' secondly (contract Auxerre Mar 1272) as his second wife, ROBERT de Flandre Seigneur de Béthune et de Dendermonde [Termonde], son of Guy de Dampierre Count of Flanders & his first wife Mathilde de Béthune Dame de Béthune ([
2 CONC 1249]-Ypres 17 Sep 1322, bur Ypres St Martin, transferred to Ypres Cathedral).  Comte de Nevers, in right of his wife.  He succeeded his father in 1305 as ROBERT III Count of Flanders.  
