0 @N6000000002244391424@ NOTE He was appointed king of Sicily by his brother James II of Aragon to prevent
1 CONT the island's return to the Angevin Charles II of Naples. Frederick called
1 CONT himself the III to revive the Ghibelline tradition of the Holy Roman Emperors
1 CONT Frederick I and II. A war with Naples and the papacy followed. By a peace
1 CONT agreement, it was agreed that Frederick should be allowed to retain the title
1 CONT of "king of Trinacria" until his death, when the island would revert back to
1 CONT the Angevins, but hostilities resumed & Frederick named his son successor.
0 @N6000000002188002426@ NOTE Abaqa Khan
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Abaqa Khan (1234–1282), the son of Hulagu and Oroqina Khatun, a Mongol Christian. As the second Il-Khan emperor in Persia, he reigned from 1265–1282.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his reign, Abaqa, a devout Buddhist, attempted to convert the Muslims and harassing them mercilessly by promoting Nestorian and Buddhist interests ahead of the Muslims, by sending embassies to Pope Gregory X and Edward I of
1 CONC  England. During his harsh reign, many Muslims had attempted to assassinate Abaqa. In 1265, upon his succession, he received the hand of Maria Despina Palaiologina, the illegitimate daughter of Emperor Michael VIII Palaeologus, in
1 CONC  marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the time of Hulagu's death from illness in 1265, Abaqa, on his father's behalf, continued to fight the Golden Horde forces of Berke Khan until 1267, the time of Berke's death. However, Barrak Khan tried to annex Iran in 1270 an
1 CONC d thus starting war against Abaqa in the city of Herat. Abaqa successfully defended Iran, leaving his people victorious. Abaqa was succeeded by his Muslim brother Tekuder.
1 CONT
1 CONT Abaqa was later assassinated. The assassin viewed Maria as part of his rightful inheritance, but she fled back to Constantinople where her father, apparently wishing to spare his capital the fate that befell Baghdad, tried to marr
1 CONC y her off again to another Mongol khan. Maria could not accept the offer, became a nun and founded The Church of Panagia Mouchliotissa around 1285.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT Khan genealogy 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Hulagu Ilkhanid Dynasty
1 CONT 1265–1282 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Tekuder 
0 @N6000000002187845125@ NOTE Catherine I of Courtenay
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Catherine I of Courtenay (1274–1307/8) was Titular Empress of Constantinople from 1283 to her death in 1307/8. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT She was the daughter of the Titular Emperor of Constantinople Philip I of Courtenay by Beatrice of Anjou. Her maternal grandparents were King Charles I of Sicily and Countess Beatrice of Provence. The senior Beatrice was the fourt
1 CONC h daughter of Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence and Beatrice of Savoy.
1 CONT
1 CONT On her father's death in 1283, Catherine inherited his claims to the throne of Constaninople and was recognized as empress by the Latin states in Greece. On February 8, 1301, she married Count Charles of Valois, son of King Philip
1 CONC  III of France and Isabella of Aragon. Charles became titular emperor with Catherine until her death in January 1307 or 1308.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT By Charles of Valois, Catherine I had three children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Catherine, who died young. 
1 CONT John, count of Chartres, who died young. 
1 CONT Catherine II of Valois, Princess of Achaea and titular empress of Constantinople. 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Philip I Titular Latin Empress
1 CONT 1283–1308
1 CONT with Charles of Valois 1301–1308 Succeeded by
1 CONT Catherine II 
