0 @N6000000000437110704@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Byzantine Emperor
0 @N6000000000945061270@ NOTE Keiser av Bysants 1081 - 1118. 
1 CONT Alexios var en fortreffelig feltherre som ble utropt til keiser av hæren 24.01.1081.
1 CONT Han erobret Konstantinopel, hvoretter hans motstander Nikeforos gikk i kloster.
1 CONT I 1081 ble han overvunnet ved Durazzo av Robert Guiscard, men da denne ble kalt
1 CONT hjem til Apulia, ble hans etterlatte hær nesten tilintetgjort av Alexios. Robert angrep på ny i
1 CONT 1084, men døde i 1085.
1 CONT Det første korstog til Jerusalem fant sted fra 1096 til 1099. Bakgrunnen var at den
1 CONT hellige stad hadde falt i tyrkernes hender. Så lenge araberne var herrer i Jerusalem, hadde de
1 CONT tatt godt imot pilegrimene, de så jo hvor mange penger det var å tjene på valfartene. Men
1 CONT tyrkerne var så fanatiske muhammedanere at de ikke tålte å se kristne blandt seg. Da nå
1 CONT pilegrimsskarene kom hjem fra Jerusalem og fortalte om hvordan tyrkerne hadde overfalt og
1 CONT plyndret dem, om hvordan de hadde drevet gjøn med deres andaktsøvelser og til og med drept
1 CONT flere pilegrimer, da steg det et skrik av forbitrelse mot de vantro fra hele Vesten.
1 CONT Gotfred av Bouillon var den som først sto ferdig til oppbrudd for å dra til Bysants,
1 CONT korsfarernes nærmeste felles mål. Snart var ikke mindre enn syv korstogshærer på vei dit.
1 CONT Alexios likte seg ikke riktig da han fikk høre hva som var på ferde. Aldri hadde han tenkt seg at
1 CONT han skulle bli så overveldende bønnhørt da han ba Den hellige fader om hjelp. Hva kunne
1 CONT ikke disse, i hans øyne, halvt barbariske vesterlendingene under sine tøylesløse
1 CONT krigerhøvdinger, komme til å finne på når de fikk se keiserbyens rike skatter! Det var ingen lett
1 CONT oppgave å holde dem i godt humør og gjøre deres sverd til lydige redskaper for den bysantiske
1 CONT politikk. Fremfor alt gjaldt det nå å hindre ethvert samarbeid mellom korsfarerhøvdingene før
1 CONT han hadde fått dem vel over til Lilleasia. Alexios skjønte at han måtte underhandle med dem en
1 CONT av gangen og lokke eller true dem til å avlegge lensed til keiseren for de erobringene de
1 CONT eventuelt kom til å gjøre i Lilleasia og Syria, og deretter i tur og orden få dem over Bosporus så
1 CONT snart som mulig.
1 CONT Først gjaldt det altså Gotfred av Bouillon. Han nektet hårdnakket å anerkjenne keiseren
1 CONT som sin lensherre, å gjøre seg til ?keiserens slave?, som han kalte det. Det begynte med at
1 CONT han sa nei takk til keiserens innbydelse til å komme og hilse på ham. Gotfred aktet tydeligvis
1 CONT først å avvente sine krigsfellers ankomst. Men det måtte for alt i verden ikke få skje. Alexios
1 CONT grep derfor til det middel som etter hans mening var best egnet til å få korsfarerhøvdingen myk,
1 CONT han avskar alle provianttilførsler. Hertugen svarte med å skaffe seg proviant selv med makt, og
1 CONT keiseren måtte åpne for tilførslene igjen. Slik svinget begivenhetene fram og tilbake helt til
1 CONT Gotfred av Bouillon innså at han i lengden måtte komme til å trekke det korteste strå i en
1 CONT kraftprøve med keiseren. Nå fant han seg i å besøke Alexios i hans palass, bøye kne for
1 CONT keiseren som satt på sin trone, og avlegge troskapsed til ham. Gotfred anerkjente dermed
1 CONT Alexios som lensherre over alle de landområder han kom til å erobre i Østen. Orientalerens list
1 CONT hadde seiret over vesterlendingens stolthet.
1 CONT Deretter ble Gotfreds tropper transportert over Bosporus i god tid før Robert Guiscards
1 CONT sønn, Bohemund av Tarent, ankom. Slik gjorde Alexios opp med den ene korsfarerhøvdingen
1 CONT etter den andre og forvandlet dem så godt som alle sammen til lydige redskap for sine egne
1 CONT planer før han satte dem over til den asiatiske siden av sundet. Med stor psykologisk
1 CONT skarpsindighet behandlet han hver enkelt etter sin egenart. Ja, en av høvdingene skrev
1 CONT begeistret hjem til sin hustru: ?Keiseren er som en far for meg; han elsker meg mer enn alle de
1 CONT andre fyrstene. Og for en rikdom og en makt han  har!? Med uovertreffelig mesterskap hadde
1 CONT keiser Alexios tillempet den gamle romerske regelen: ?Divide et impera!?
1 CONT Alexios styrte klokt og kraftig, og skaffet riket igjen herredømme over store deler av
1 CONT Lilleasien. Alexios brakte orden i rikets indre forhold og beskyttet kirken. Hans liv er skildret i
1 CONT ?Alexiaden?.
0 @N6000000002187821082@ NOTE Alexius I Comnenus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Alexius I (Greek: ??????? ?' ??µ????? or Alexios I Komnenos) (1048 – August 15, 1118), Byzantine emperor (1081–1118), was the third son of John Comnenus, the nephew of Isaac I Comnenus (emperor 1057–1059).
1 CONT
1 CONT His father declined the throne on the abdication of Isaac, who was accordingly succeeded by four emperors of other families between 1059 and 1081. Under one of these emperors, Romanus IV Diogenes (1067–1071), he served with distin
1 CONC ction against the Seljuk Turks. Under Michael VII Parapinaces (1071–1078) and Nicephorus III Botaniates (1078–1081) he was also employed, along with his elder brother Isaac, against rebels in Asia Minor, Thrace and in Epirus in 10
1 CONC 71.
1 CONT
1 CONT The success of the Comneni roused the jealousy of Botaniates and his ministers, and the Comneni were almost compelled to take up arms in self-defence. Botaniates was forced to abdicate and retire to a monastery, and Isaac declined
1 CONC  the crown in favour of his younger brother Alexius, who then became emperor at the age of 33.
1 CONT
1 CONT By that time Alexius was the lover of the Empress Maria Bagrationi, a daughter of king Bagrat IV of Georgia who was successively married to Michael VII Ducas and his successor Botaniates, and was renowned for her beauty. Alexius a
1 CONC nd Maria lived almost openly together at the Palace of Mangana, and Alexius had Michael VII and Maria's young son, the prince Constantine Ducas, adopted and proclaimed heir to the throne. The affair conferred to Alexius a degree o
1 CONC f dynastic legitimacy, but soon his mother Anna Dalassena consolidated the Ducas family connection by arranging the Emperor's wedding with Irene Ducaena or Doukaina, granddaughter of the caesar John Ducas, head of a powerful feuda
1 CONC l family and the "kingmaker" behind Michael VII.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alexius' involvement with Maria continued and shortly after his daughter Anna Comnena was born, she was betrothed to Constantine Ducas and moved to live at the Mangana Palace with him and Maria. The situation however changed drast
1 CONC ically when John II Comnenus was born: Anna's engagement to Constantine was dissolved, she was moved to the main Palace to live with her mother and grandmother, Constantine's status as heir was terminated and Alexius became estran
1 CONC ged with Maria, now stripped of her imperial title. Shortly afterwards, the teenager Constantine died and Maria was confined to a convent.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT This coin was struck by Alexius during his war against Robert Guiscard.Alexius' long reign of nearly 37 years was full of struggle. At the very outset he had to meet the formidable attack of the Normans (Robert Guiscard and his so
1 CONC n Bohemund), who took Dyrrhachium and Corfu, and laid siege to Larissa in Thessaly (see Battle of Dyrrhachium). The Norman danger ended for the time with Robert Guiscard's death in 1085, and the conquests were reversed.
1 CONT
1 CONT He had next to repel the invasions of Pechenegs and Cumans in Thrace, with whom the Manichaean sect of the Bogomils made common cause; and thirdly, he had to cope with the fast-growing power of the Seljuk Turks in Asia Minor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Above all he had to meet the difficulties caused by the arrival of the knights of the First Crusade, which had been, to a great degree, initiated as the result of the representations of his own ambassadors, whom he had sent to Pop
1 CONC e Urban II at the Council of Piacenza in 1095. The help which he wanted from the West was simply mercenary forces and not the immense hosts which arrived, to his consternation and embarrassment. The first group, under Peter the He
1 CONC rmit, he dealt with by sending them on to Asia Minor, where they were massacred by the Turks in 1096.
1 CONT
1 CONT The second and much more serious host of knights, led by Godfrey of Bouillon, he also led into Asia, promising to supply them with provisions in return for an oath of homage, and by their victories recovered for the Byzantine Empi
1 CONC re a number of important cities and islands—Nicaea, Chios, Rhodes, Smyrna, Ephesus, Philadelphia, Sardis, and in fact most of Asia Minor (1097–1099). This is ascribed by his daughter Anna as a credit to his policy and diplomacy, b
1 CONC ut by the Latin historians of the crusade as a sign of his treachery and falseness. The crusaders believed their oaths were made invalid when Alexius did not help them during the siege of Antioch; Bohemund, who had set himself up 
1 CONC as Prince of Antioch, briefly went to war with Alexius, but agreed to become Alexius' vassal under the Treaty of Devol in 1108.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the last twenty years of his life he lost much of his popularity. The years were marked by persecution of the followers of the Paulician and Bogomil heresies—one of his last acts was to burn Basil, a Bogomil leader, with wh
1 CONC om he had engaged in a theological controversy; by renewed struggles with the Turks (1110–1117); and by anxieties as to the succession, which his wife Irene wished to alter in favour of her daughter Anna's husband, Nicephorus Brye
1 CONC nnius, for whose benefit the special title panhypersebastos ("honored above all") was created. This intrigue disturbed even his dying hours.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alexius was for many years under the strong influence of an eminence grise, his mother Anna Dalassena, a wise and immensely able politician whom, in a uniquely irregular fashion, he had crowned as Empress Augusta instead of the ri
1 CONC ghtful claimant to the title, his wife Irene. Dalassena was the effective administrator of the Empire during Alexius' long absences in war campaigns: she was constantly at odds with her daughter-in-law and had assumed total respon
1 CONC sibility for the upbringing and education of her granddaughter Anna Comnena.
0 @N6000000003051152152@ NOTE In 1078, when Nicephorus III Botaniates became Emperor, he made Alexius, who had defeated his rival, Bryennius, Domestic.
1 CONT He was short but athletic, dark, handsome, dashing, gentle, shrewd, eloquent, talented, ambitious and strong-willed.
1 CONT He was adopted by Maria the Alan, the second wife of Nicephorus III thereby making him the champion of the legitimacy of her son Constantine.
1 CONT Warned by Maria that Nicephorus III was planning to blind them, Alexius and his brother, Issac, fled to the army of Thrace, where he was proclaimed Emperor and assaulted New Rome. Someone opened a gate, there was street fighting, 
1 CONC pillage and massacre, which he was powerless to prevent. The Navy came over to Alexius and Nicephorus III abdicated and entered a monastery in 1081, indicating that his only regret was that future absence of meat from his diet!
1 CONT He had attained the throne only with the support of many powerful factions. The treasury was empty, the army reduced, scattered and demoralized. He made peace with the Seljuks and enrolled 7000 of their warriors. When he decided t
1 CONC o drive the Normans into the sea, he left the city and the palace under the control of his brother, and entrusted the government to his mother. He took the army to Durrazo, where the battle began in Oct., 1081. It looked as though
1 CONC  he was winning, but then his Serbian and Turkish allies were bought of with Norman gold and withdrew from the field. Alexius and his courtiers seperated and fled.
1 CONT Alone and hungry, he wandered over the mountains to Ochrida to try to gather another army, but their were no soldiers to be had. His family made great personal sacrifices, but the populace refused to cooperate as did the church. H
1 CONC e invoked an ancient canon and pronounced the confiscation of the church's treasury.
1 CONT When the leader of the Normans, Guiscard, died of an epidemic in 1085, that danger ended. In Apr., 1091, he annihilated the Patzinaks, and, returning in triumph, but childless, proclaimed Constantine Ducas his successor.
1 CONT In 1095, Pope Urban II called on Christians to take up arms against Islam and free the Holy Sepulcher, thereby attaining complete atonement. While many sought only the remission of their sins, serfs also hoped to escape from bonda
1 CONC ge, adventurers to make fortunes and malefactors to evade punishment.
1 CONT The first to depart were the poor, the People's Crusade of Peter the Hermit. Five groups marched east. The first two committed such excesses along the way that the Hungarians wiped them out. The third began to butcher Jews on the 
1 CONC Rhine and was scattered by the Hungarians. But two hosts, led by Walter the Penniless and Peter the Hermit, reached New Rome in the summer of 1096.
1 CONT Alexius recieved them with expedient forbearance, gave them food and money, and urged them to await the next contingent of Crusaders outside the city walls, but they began looting the suburbs, even sacking churches, so he sent the
1 CONC m over to Civitot, a fort he had built in Turkey's shore. They continued to maraud, even torturing Christians. Soonthey began to ravage the Turkish countryside and 25,000 were killed. The imperial fleet brought the 3000 survivors 
1 CONC back to New Rome to await the next Crusaders. These were already streaming eastward in plundering groups. Among them were Godfrey of Bouillon, who sought to exterminate the Jews in the German cities through which he passed, Raymon
1 CONC d of Toulouse and Bohemund of Tarento. The advent of these armed hosts brought the danger of a sudden attack on the city. Though Alexius fed them, they too pillaged, killing all who resisted, including Orthodox priests. Alexius tr
1 CONC eated them with tact, patience and generosity, and moving through riot, arson, intrigue and insolence, obtained from most of the leaders the promise to restore to the Empire any land that had been hers before the Turkish invasion 
1 CONC that they might conquer and to acknowledge him as suzerain for any further conquests. His success was probably due to their realization that his food, fleet, army and counsel were indispensible.
1 CONT In the spring of 1097, they crossed to Anatolia. In 1098, when Antioch fell to Kerboga, many of them, including Stephen of Blois, fled and meeting Alexius, told him that it had certainly fallen, so being already overextended, he r
1 CONC etreated.
1 CONT Then Alexius was occupied with the arrival of four major grous of Crusaders from Italy, France Germany and Scandinavia who looted, murdered and even assaulted the city. He overlooked their misdeeds, exacted their recognition of hi
1 CONC m as overlord, and dispatched them against the Moslems, who made short work of them.
1 CONT In 1112, in broken health, he took the field to battle the encroaching Turks once more.
1 CONT When in 1118, he fell ill, to thwart the machinations of his wife, he gave his son John his ring, and bade him take over the realm.
