0 @N6000000004303404418@ NOTE AKA  Adilasia.
1 CONT WIKIPEDIA:
1 CONT
1 CONT Azalaïs of Montferrat
1 CONT
1 CONT Azalaïs of Montferrat (also Adelasia or Alasia) (d. 1232), was marchioness and regent of Saluzzo.
1 CONT
1 CONT Azalaïs was one of at least three daughters of William V of Montferrat and his wife Judith of Babenberg. Her brothers included William of Montferrat, Count of Jaffa and Ascalon, Conrad I of Jerusalem, and Boniface of Montferrat.
1 CONT She married Marquis Manfred II of Saluzzo before 1182, in which year she received lands in Saluzzo, Racconigi, Villa, Centallo and Quaranta, in case her marriage (like that of her sister Agnes) should need to be annulled for reaso
1 CONC ns of sterility. However, after this date, she bore at least five children:
1 CONT
1 CONT •	Agnes m. Comita III, giudice of Logudoro, in Sardinia.
1 CONT •	Boniface (the heir, who predeceased his father) m. Maria of Torres (daughter of Agnes's husband).
1 CONT •	Margaret m. Geoffrey de Salvaing.
1 CONT •	(unnamed daughter) m. Marquis William II of Ceva.
1 CONT •	Thomas.
1 CONT Like her brother Boniface, Azalaïs was a patron of troubadours. She is mentioned in Peire Vidal's song, Estat ai gran sazo:
1 CONT Dieus sal l'onrat marques
1 CONT E sa bella seror...
1 CONT (God save the honoured marquis
1 CONT And his beautiful sister)[1]
1 CONT and is the dedicatee of his Bon' aventura don Dieus als Pizas.[2]
1 CONT Around 1192, she had built the church of San Lorenzo, which she granted to the canons of San Lorenzo in Oulx; her eldest son, Boniface, named after her brother, is mentioned for the first time in the donation. However, Boniface di
1 CONC ed in 1212, and with the death of her husband in February 1215, Azalaîs became regent of Saluzzo for her grandson, Manfred III.
1 CONT In 1216, she made a treaty with Thomas I of Savoy for a marriage between his son Amadeus and her grand-daughter Agnes. However, the marriage never took place, possibly on grounds of consanguinity, since Azalaïs was a first cousin 
1 CONC of Thomas's father. Amadeus married Anne of Burgundy, and Agnes became Abbess of the Cistercian convent of Santa Maria della Stella in Rifreddo.[3] Azalaïs also made political and ecclesiastical agreements with Alba and with the B
1 CONC ishop of Asti.
1 CONT When young Manfred reached his majority in 1218, Azalaïs returned to church patronage. In 1224, she endowed the convent of Rifreddo with the income of the church of San Ilario. In 1227, she made further grants to the canons of Oul
1 CONC x. She died in 1232, and was buried in the Cistercian abbey of Santa Maria di Staffarda.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT 1.	^ Peire Vidal, Estat ai gran sazo
1 CONT 2.	^ Peire Vidal, Bon' aventura don Dieus als Pizas
1 CONT 3.	^ comune.rifreddo.cn.it: Informazioni storiche
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources & Further Reading
1 CONT •	Ernest Hoepffner, Le Troubadour Peire Vidal: sa vie et son œuvre, Paris, 1961
1 CONT •	A M Patrone, "Alasia", Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani, vol. 1, p. 583
1 CONT •	Leopoldo Usseglio, I Marchesi di Monferrato in Italia ed in Oriente durante i secoli XII e XIII, 1926.
0 @N6000000000337158346@ NOTE (Research):Philip of Swabia Philip of Swabia Pronounced As: swab , 1176?-1208, German king (1198-1208), son of Holy Roman Emperor Frederick I. After the death (1197) of his brother, German King and Holy Roman Emperor Henry VI, he 
1 CONC unsuccessfully attempted to secure the succession in Germany of his infant nephew, the later Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II; for the sake of the house of Hohenstaufen, he finally consented to his own election as German king. A sm
1 CONC all, anti-Hohenstaufen group led by the archbishop of Cologne elected (1198) Otto IV antiking. In the ensuing war Philip was supported by Philip II of France, while Otto had the support of his uncle Richard I of England. Though su
1 CONC ccessful at first, Philip's cause was weakened when Pope Innocent III declared (1201) for Otto. However, the year 1204 marked a turn in Philip's favor; with his capture (1206) of Cologne, the war was virtually ended. Negotiations 
1 CONC with the pope had resulted in a satisfactory settlement when Philip was murdered by a personal enemy. Otto IV was elected his successor as German king. Philip became involved in the Fourth Crusade (1202-4; see Crusades) partly thr
1 CONC ough his marriage to the Byzantine princess Irene, daughter of Emperor Isaac II. The extent of Philip's influence in diverting the crusade to Constantinople is still debated. Philip Encyclopædia Britannica Article    born 1178 die
1 CONC d June 21, 1208, Bamberg, Ger.   also called Philip Of Swabia,  German Philipp Von Schwaben German Hohenstaufen king whose rivalry for the crown involved him in a decade of warfare with the Welf Otto IV.   The youngest son of the 
1 CONC Holy Roman emperor Frederick I Barbarossa, Philip was destined for the church. After being provost of the cathedral at Aachen, he was, in 1190 or 1191, elected bishop of Würzburg. Shortly after the death of his brother Frederick (
1 CONC 1191), however, he abandoned his ecclesiastical career. Another brother, the Holy Roman emperor Henry VI, made him duke of Tuscany in 1195 and duke of Swabia in 1196. In May 1197 he married Irene, daughter of the Byzantine emperor
1 CONC  Isaac II Angelus.   At Henry VI's death in September 1197, his son, the future emperor Frederick II, was less than three years old, and the German princes were unwilling to accept him as king. The princes favourable to the Hohens
1 CONC taufens elected Philip German king in March 1198. The opposing party, led by Archbishop Adolf of Cologne, elected Otto, a son of Henry the Lion of Brunswick of the rival Welf dynasty, king in June of that year. Otto was crowned at
1 CONC  Aachen, the proper place for the ceremony, by Archbishop Adolf. Philip's coronation, by another prelate, did not take place until September 1198 at Mainz.   In the ensuing civil war the Hohenstaufen cause prospered at first. In 1
1 CONC 201, however, Pope Innocent III recognized Otto as king and excommunicated Philip. Philip's fortunes were only restored in 1204, by a series of defections from Otto's side, culminating in that of Adolf of Cologne himself. In June 
1 CONC 1205, Adolf crowned Philip at Aachen.   The city of Cologne, which, notwithstanding its archbishop, had sided with Otto, was captured in January 1207, and Otto's cause seemed lost. Late in 1207, however, when Philip offered to giv
1 CONC e Otto one of his daughters in marriage and to enfeoff him with either the duchy of Swabia or the kingdom of Arles, Otto, buoyed by hopes of financial, if not military, support from the kings of England and Denmark, rejected the o
1 CONC ffer. Nevertheless, a truce was arranged that lasted until June of the following year.   In 1208 Pope Innocent III recognized Philip as king and promised to crown him emperor. Philip, who had mobilized his army at Bamberg in order
1 CONC  to move against Otto, was waiting for the truce to expire when he was murdered by Otto of Wittelsbach, count Palatine of Bavaria, to whom he had refused to give one of his daughters in marriage. Eventually his daughters were marr
1 CONC ied: Beatrix the Elder to his old rival Otto, Cunigunda to King Wenceslas of Bohemia, and Beatrix the Younger to Ferdinand III of Castile.   A brave man, Philip was praised by contemporaries for his mildness and generosity. The di
1 CONC version of the Fourth Crusade to Constantinople is assumed by some authorities to have been prompted by him in the interests of his brother-in-law, the Byzantine emperor Alexius IV Angelus.
0 @N6000000000437115604@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Holy Roman Emperor
0 @N6000000002187834277@ NOTE Philip of Swabia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip of Swabia depicted in a medieval manuscript (about 1200).Philip of Swabia (1177 – June 21, 1208) was king of Germany and duke of Swabia, the rival of the emperor Otto IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Biography 
1 CONT 2 Ancestors 
1 CONT 3 References 
1 CONT 4 Philip's descendants 
1 CONT 5 See also 
1 CONT 6 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Biography
1 CONT Philip was the fifth and youngest son of the emperor Frederick I and Beatrix, daughter of Renaud III, count of Burgundy, and brother of the emperor Henry VI. He entered the clergy, was made provost of Aix-la-Chapelle, and in 1190 
1 CONC or 1191 was chosen bishop of Würzburg. Having accompanied his brother Henry to Italy in 1191, Philip forsook his ecclesiastical calling, and, travelling again to Italy, was made duke of Tuscany in 1195 and received an extensive gr
1 CONC ant of lands. In 1196 he became duke of Swabia, on the death of his brother Conrad; and in May 1197 he married Irene Angelina, daughter of the Byzantine emperor, Isaac II, and widow of Roger III, Titular King of Sicily, a lady who
1 CONC  is described by Walther von der Vogelweide as " the rose without a thorn, the dove without guile."
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip enjoyed his brother's confidence to a very great extent, and appears to have been designated as guardian of the Henry's young son Frederick, afterwards the emperor Frederick II, in case of his father's early death. In 1197 
1 CONC he had set out to fetch Frederick from Sicily for his coronation as King of the Germans when he heard of the emperor's death and returned at once to Germany. He appears to have desired to protect the interests of his nephew and to
1 CONC  quell the disorder which arose on Henry's death, but was overtaken by events. The hostility to the kingship of a child was growing, and after Philip had been chosen as defender of the empire during Frederick's minority he consent
1 CONC ed to his own election. He was elected German king at Mühlhausen on March 8, 1198, and was crowned at Mainz on the September 8 following.
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile, a number of princes hostile to Philip, under the leadership of Adolph, Archbishop of Cologne, had elected an anti-king in the person of Otto, second son of Henry the Lion, duke of Saxony. In the war that followed, Phili
1 CONC p, who drew his principal support from south Germany, met with considerable success. In 1199 he received further accessions to his party and carried the war into his opponent's territory, although unable to obtain the support of P
1 CONC ope Innocent III, and only feebly assisted by his ally Philip Augustus, king of France. The following year was less favourable to his arms; and in March 1201 Innocent took the decisive step of placing Philip and his associates und
1 CONC er the ban, and began to work energetically in favour of Otto.
1 CONT
1 CONT Also in 1201, Philip was visited by his cousin Boniface of Montferrat, the leader of the Fourth Crusade. The Crusaders were by this time under Venetian control and were besieging Zara on the Adriatic Sea. Although Boniface's exact
1 CONC  reasons for meeting with Philip are unknown, while at Philip's court he also met Alexius Angelus, Philip's brother-in-law. Alexius convinced Boniface, and later the Venetians, to divert the Crusade to Constantinople and restore I
1 CONC saac II to the throne, as he had recently been deposed by Alexius III, Alexius and Irene's uncle.
1 CONT
1 CONT The two succeeding years were still more unfavourable to Philip. Otto, aided by Ottokar I, king of Bohemia, and Hermann I, landgrave of Thuringia, drove him from north Germany, thus compelling him to seek by abject concessions, bu
1 CONC t without success, reconciliation with Innocent. The submission to Philip of Hermann of Thuringia in 1204 marks the turning-point of his fortunes, and he was soon joined by Adolph of Cologne and Henry I, Duke of Brabant.
1 CONT
1 CONT On January 6, 1205 he was crowned again with great ceremony by Adolph at Aix-la-Chapelle, though it was not till 1207 that his entry into Cologne practically brought the war to a close. A month or two later Philip was loosed from 
1 CONC the papal ban, and in March 1208 it seems probable that a treaty was concluded by which a nephew of the pope was to marry one of Philip's daughters and to receive the disputed dukedom of Tuscany. Philip was preparing to crush the 
1 CONC last flicker of the rebellion in Brunswick-Lüneburg when he was murdered at Bamberg, on June 21, 1208, by Otto of Wittelsbach, count palatine in Bavaria, to whom he had refused the hand of one of his daughters. Philip was a brave 
1 CONC and handsome man, and contemporary writers, among whom was Walther von der Vogelweide, praise his mildness and generosity.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Ancestors
1 CONT Philip's ancestors in three generations Philip of Swabia Father:
1 CONT Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor Paternal Grandfather:
1 CONT Frederick II, Duke of Swabia Paternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Frederick I, Duke of Swabia 
1 CONT Paternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Agnes of Germany 
1 CONT Paternal Grandmother:
1 CONT Judith Paternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Henry IX, Duke of Bavaria 
1 CONT Paternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Wulfhild of Saxony 
1 CONT Mother:
1 CONT Beatrice I, Countess of Burgundy Maternal Grandfather:
1 CONT Renaud III, Count of Burgundy Maternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Stephen I, Count of Burgundy 
1 CONT Maternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Beatrix of Lorraine 
1 CONT Maternal Grandmother:
1 CONT Agatha Maternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Simon I, Duke of Lorraine 
1 CONT Maternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Adelaide 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Peter Csendes, Philipp von Schwaben. Ein Staufer im Kampf um die Macht, 2003. 
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition article "Philip", a publication now in the public domain. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Philip's descendants
1 CONT Philip of Swabia married Irene Angelina, daughter of Isaac II Angelus on May 25, 1197. Their four daughters were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Beatrice of Hohenstaufen (1198-1212), married Otto IV, Holy Roman Emper
1 CONT Cunigunde of Hohenstaufen (1200-1248), married King Wenceslaus I, King of Bohemia 
1 CONT Mary of Hohenstaufen (1201-1235), married Henry II, Duke of Brabant 
1 CONT Elisabeth of Hohenstaufen (1203-1235), married King Ferdinand III of Castile 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT Dukes of Swabia family tree 
1 CONT
