0 @N6000000002187862159@ NOTE John I, Duke of Brabant
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT John I van Brabant, also called John I the Victorious (Leuven 1252/1253 – May 3, 1294 in Leuven) was Duke of Brabant (1267–1294), Lothier and Limburg (1288–1294).
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Henry III, Duke of Brabant and Aleidis of Burgundy, daughter of Hugh IV, Duke of Burgundy. He was also an older brother of Maria of Brabant, Queen consort of Philip III of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1267 his older brother Henry IV, Duke of Brabant, being mentally deficient, was deposed in his favour. His greatest military victory was the Battle of Woeringen, by which John I came to reign over the Duchy of Limburg. In 1288 
1 CONC Limburg was formally attached to Brabant.
1 CONT
1 CONT John I is said to be a perfect model of a feudal prince in the days of chivalry: brave, adventurous, excelling in every form of active exercise, fond of display, generous in temper. This made him very popular in Middle Ages poetry
1 CONC  and literature. Even today there exists an ode to him, so well-known that it was a potential candidate to be the North Brabant anthem. Jan delighted in tournaments and was always eager to take part in jousts. He was also famous f
1 CONC or his many illegitimate children.
1 CONT
1 CONT On May 3, 1294 at some marriage festivities at Bar-le-Duc (now France), John I was mortally wounded in the arm in an encounter. He was buried in the church of the Minderbroeders in Leuven, but since the Protestant iconoclasm (Beel
1 CONC denstorm) in 1566, nothing remains of his tomb.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT
1 CONT He was married twice. First, on September 5, 1270 with Marguerite of France, daughter of Louis IX of France and Marguerite Berenger of Provence. In this marriage he had a son, who born and died 1271. Second, he married 1273 to Mar
1 CONC garet of Flanders (d. July 3, 1285), daughter of Guy, Count of Flanders and had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Godfrey (1273/74 – aft. September 13, 1283).
1 CONT    2. John II of Brabant (1275–1312).
1 CONT    3. Margaret (4 October 1276–14 December 1311, Genoa), married 9 June 1292 to Henry VII, Holy Roman Emperor.
1 CONT    4. Marie (d. after 2 December 1338), married to Count Amadeus V of Savoy.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Dukes of Brabant family tree
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Henry IV Duke of Brabant and Lothier
1 CONT 1267–1294 Succeeded by
1 CONT John II
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT interregnum Duke of Limburg
0 @N6000000002187862141@ NOTE John II, Duke of Brabant
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Jan II van Brabant (September 27, 1275 – October 27, 1312, Tervuren), also called John II, the Peaceful, was Duke of Brabant, Lothier and Limburg (1294–1312). He was the son of John I of Brabant and Margaretha of Flanders, daughte
1 CONC r of Guy of Dampierre.
1 CONT
1 CONT On July 8, 1290, Jan married Margaret Plantagenet in Westminster Abbey, London. She was a daughter of King Edward I of England and his first Queen consort Eleanor of Castile. Only one child was born out of this marriage, John III,
1 CONC  Duke of Brabant.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the reign of Jan II, Brabant continues supporting a coalition to stop French expansion. He tried to conquer South Holland (district of medieval Holland) from the pro-french count John II of Holland, but was not successful. 
1 CONC John, who suffered from kidney stones and wanted his duchy to be peacefully handed over to his son upon his death, in 1312 signed the famous Charter of Kortenberg.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his death Jan II was buried in the St. Michael and Gudula Cathedral in Brussels.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also: Dukes of Brabant family tree
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT John I Duke of Brabant, Lothier, and Limburg
1 CONT 1294–1312 Succeeded by
1 CONT John III
