0 @N6000000002187757971@ NOTE Robert of Courtenay
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT House of Capet
1 CONT Cadets
1 CONT Senior House
1 CONT Valois
1 CONT Bourbon
1 CONT Burgundy
1 CONT Portugal
1 CONT Aviz
1 CONT Braganza
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert of Courtenay (died 1228), emperor of the Latin Empire, or of Constantinople, was a younger son of the emperor Peter of Courtenay, and a descendant of the French king, Louis VI, while his mother Yolanda of Flanders was a sis
1 CONC ter of Baldwin and Henry of Flanders, the first and second emperors of the Latin Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT When it became known in France that Peter of Courtenay was dead, his eldest son, Philip, Marquis of Namur, renounced the succession to the Latin empire of Constantinople in favor of his brother Robert, who set out to take possessi
1 CONC on of his distracted inheritance, which was then ruled by Conon of Béthune as regent. Crowned emperor on March 25, 1221 Robert, who was surrounded by enemies, appealed for help to the pope Honorius III and to the king of France Ph
1 CONC ilip II; but meanwhile his lands were falling into the hands of the rival Despotate of Epirus and Empire of Nicaea.
1 CONT
1 CONT Some little aid was sent from western Europe, but soon Robert was compelled to make peace with his chief foe, John Ducas Vatatzes, emperor of Nicaea, who was confirmed in all his conquests. Robert promised to marry Eudoxia, daught
1 CONC er of the late emperor of Nicaea, Theodore I Lascaris, a lady to whom he had been betrothed on a former occasion; however, he soon repudiated this engagement, and married a French lady, already the fiancée of a Burgundian gentlema
1 CONC n. Heading a conspiracy, the Burgundian drove Robert from Constantinople, and early in 1228 the emperor died in Morea.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Yolanda Latin Emperor
1 CONT 1221–1228 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Baldwin II
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
