0 @N6000000002322291892@ NOTE She was the heiress of the titular king of Jerusalem. upon the marriage to
1 CONT her, Frederick declared himself king of Jerusalem, which was the high point
1 CONT as well as the turning point of Frederick's conception of sovereignty.
1 CONT Eschatological prophecies concerning his rule were then made, and the Emperor
1 CONT considered himself to be the messiah, a new David! His entry into Jerusalem
1 CONT was compared with that of Christ on Palm Sunday, and, indeed, in a manifesto
1 CONT the Emperor compared himself to Christ!
0 @N6000000002187911269@ NOTE Yolande of Jerusalem
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Yolande of Brienne (1212 - 1228), also known as Yolanda or Isabella II, became Queen of Jerusalem as an infant in 1212.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT The death of Queen Isabella-Yolande (From MS of William of Tyre's Historia and Old French Continuation, painted in Acre, 13C. Bib. Nat. Française.)She was the only child of Maria of Montferrat, Queen of Jerusalem, and John of Brie
1 CONC nne. Maria was the daughter of Queen Isabella of Jerusalem by her second husband Conrad I, and heiress, on her mother's death, of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Because John did not have a direct claim on the throne, Yolande succeeded 
1 CONC her mother Maria, who died giving birth to her in 1212. John ruled as regent until 1225, when Yolande married Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor with the encouragement of Pope Honorius III, who hoped by this bond to attach the emper
1 CONC or firmly to the Sixth Crusade.
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick claimed the kingdom for himself; when Yolande died after giving birth to their son Conrad in 1228, Frederick ruled as regent, settling a truce with the Muslims in 1229 during the Sixth Crusade.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also: Other young rulers
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Maria and John Queen of Jerusalem
1 CONT 1212–1228
1 CONT (with Frederick, 1225-1228) Succeeded by:
1 CONT Conrad II 
