0 @N6000000003300443325@ NOTE REIGNED: Emperor of the Holy Roman Empire and King of Germany (1190 - 119 7);
1 CONT crowned king of the Romans at Aachen in 1169, and king of Italy at Mila n on
1 CONT the occasion of his marriage to Constance of Sicily (1186).
1 CONT Henry VI, 1165-97, emperor (1191-97) and German king (1190-97), was the s on
1 CONT and successor of FREDERICK I. As the husband of Constance, heiress of Sic ily,
1 CONT he gained control (1194) of that kingdom. He also took RICHARD I of Engla nd
1 CONT prisoner and forced him to swear fealty. He died while preparing to lead a
1 CONT Crusade.
1 CONT SOURCE: Concise Columbia Electronic Encyclopedia Copyright 1994, Columbia
1 CONT University Press.;Columbia Ency. (c) 1944
0 @N6000000002322298103@ NOTE Henry, the German king of the Hohenstaufen dynasty, increased his power
1 CONT and that of his dynasty by his aquisition of the kingdom of Sicily through
1 CONT his marriage to Constance I, posthumous daughter of the Sicilian king, Roger
1 CONT II. Although Henry failed in his objective of making the German crown
1 CONT hereditary, like the Sicilian crown, his son Frederick II, who became king of
1 CONT Sicily immediately after Henry VI"s death, was subsequently elected Holy
1 CONT Roman Emperor. Note: Constance was 11 years older than Henry.
0 @N6000000002187834391@ NOTE Henry VI, Holy Roman Emperor
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry VI 
1 CONT Duke of Swabia, King of Burgundy,
1 CONT King of Sicily, King of the Romans,
1 CONT King of Germany and Holy Roman Emperor 
1 CONT
1 CONT Portrait of Henry VI from the Codex Manesse (folio 6r) 
1 CONT Reign December 25, 1194-September 28, 1197 
1 CONT Coronation December 25, 1194 
1 CONT Born November, 1165 
1 CONT  Nijmegen 
1 CONT Died September 28, 1197 
1 CONT  Messina 
1 CONT Buried Palermo 
1 CONT Consort Constance of Sicily 
1 CONT Issue Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor 
1 CONT Royal House Hohenstaufen 
1 CONT Father Frederick I 
1 CONT Mother Beatrix of Burgundy 
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry VI (November 1165, Nijmegen – 28 September 1197, Messina), sometimes called the Cruel, was King of Germany 1190-1197, Holy Roman Emperor 1191-1197, and King of Sicily 1194-1197.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was the son of the emperor Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor and Beatrix of Burgundy, and was crowned King of the Romans at Bamberg in June 1169, at the age of four. After having taken the reins of the Empire from his father, 
1 CONC who had gone on the Crusade, in 1189-1190 he suppressed a revolt by Henry the Lion, former duke of Saxony and Bavaria and relative of Frederick.
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance of Sicily was betrothed to Henry in 1184, and they were married on January 27, 1186. Constance was the sole legitimate heir of William II of Sicily, and, after the latter's death in November 1189, Henry had the possibili
1 CONC ty of adding the Sicilian crown to the imperial one, as his father had died crossing the Saleph River in Cilicia, now part of Turkey June 10, 1190.
1 CONT
1 CONT In April 1191, in Rome, Henry and Constance were crowned Emperor and Empress by Pope Celestine III. The crown of Sicily, however, was harder to gain, as the barons of southern Italy had chosen a grandson of Roger II, Tancred, coun
1 CONC t of Lecce, as their king. Henry began his work besieging Naples, but he had to return to Germany (where Henry the Lion had revolted again) after his army had been heavily hit by an epidemic. Constance, who stayed behind in the pa
1 CONC lace at Salerno, was betrayed by the Salernitans, handed over to Tancred, and only released on the intervention of Celestine III, who in return recognized Tancred as King of Sicily. Henry had a stroke of fortune when the duke of A
1 CONC ustria Leopold gave him his prisoner, the King of England Richard I. Henry managed to exact from the English a ransom of 150,000 silver marks, a huge sum for that age, and with this money, he could raise a powerful army to conquer
1 CONC  southern Italy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was granted free passage in Northern Italy, signing with the Italian communes a treaty in January 1194. The following April he also reached a settlement with Henry the Lion. In February Tancred died, leaving as heir a young 
1 CONC boy, William III. Henry met little resistance and entered Palermo, capital city of the Kingdom of Sicily, on November 20, and was crowned on December 25. He is also said to have had the young William blinded and castrated, while m
1 CONC any Sicilian nobles were burned alive.
1 CONT
1 CONT At that point, Henry was the most powerful monarch in the Mediterranean and Europe, since the Kingdom of Sicily added to his personal and Imperial revenues an income without parallel in Europe. Henry felt strong enough to send hom
1 CONC e the Pisan and Genoese ships without giving their governments the promised concessions in Southern Italy, and even received tribute from the Byzantine Empire. In 1194 his son, Frederick, the future emperor and king of Sicily and 
1 CONC Jerusalem, was born. Henry secured his position in Italy, naming his friend Conrad of Urslingen as Duke of Spoleto and giving the Marche to Markward of Anweiler.
1 CONT
1 CONT His next aim was to make the imperial crown hereditary. At the Diet of Würzburg, held in April 1196, he managed to convince the majority of the princes to vote for his proposal, but in the following one at Erfurt (October 1196) he
1 CONC  did not achieve the same favourable result.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1197 the tyrannical power of the foreign King in Italy spurred a revolt, especially in southern Sicily, where Arabs were the majority of the population, which his German soldiers suppressed mercilessly. In the same year Henry p
1 CONC repared for a Crusade, but, on September 28, he died of malaria in Messina.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was fluent in Latin and, according to Alberic of Troisfontaines, was "distinguished by gifts of knowledge, wreathed in flowers of eloquence, and learned in canon and Roman law". He was a patron of poets and poetry, and he al
1 CONC most certainly composed the song "Kaiser Heinrich", now among the Weingarten Song Manuscripts.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to his rank and with Imperial Eagle, regalia, and a scroll, he is the first and foremost to be portrayed in the famous Codex Manesse, a fourteenth century manuscript showing 140 reputed poets (see Minnesänger), and at le
1 CONC ast three poems are attributed to a young and romantically minded Henry VI. In one of those he describes a romance which makes him forget all his earthly power, and neither riches nor royal dignity can outweigh his yearning for th
1 CONC at lady (ê ich mich ir verzige, ich verzige mich ê der krône – before I give her up, I’d rather give up the crown).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Alberic of Troisfontaines, Chronicon 
1 CONT David Abulafia, Frederick II 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Frederick I Barbarossa King of Germany
1 CONT 1190–1197 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Philip 
1 CONT Holy Roman Emperor
1 CONT 1191–1197 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Otto IV 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Parentage and children
1 CONT Frederick II of Swabia Judith of Bavaria Renald III of Burgundy Agatha of Lorraine 
1 CONT Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor Beatrix of Burgundy 
1 CONT Henry VI, Holy Roman Emperor 
1 CONT Spouse(s) Children 
1 CONT Constance of Sicily Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor 
