0 @N6000000000945059868@ NOTE Konge av Ungarn 1141-1162. 
1 CONT Geza seiret i kamp mot russere og greker. I hans regjeringstid utvandret tallrike
1 CONT Sachsere til Ungarn. Han lot dem i 1143 bosette seg i grevskapeet Scepuze (Szepes, Zeps)
1 CONT og flere områder i Transylvanien, hvor deres etterkommere levere ennu idag.
0 @N6000000002187757223@ NOTE Géza II of Hungary
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT (Redirected from Geza II of Hungary)
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Géza II (Hungarian: II. Géza, Slovak: Gejza) was king of Hungary from 1141 until his death in 1161.
1 CONT
1 CONT Géza succeeded his father, Béla II of Hungary, in 1141, and because he was still a minor, his maternal uncle Beloš served as regent. He faced challenges from Boris, the son of King Kálmán's adulterous queen, who disputed his claim
1 CONC  to the throne. As an adult, Géza had a reputation as a well-respected king, whose nobles did not dare to scheme against him. The power and valor of his army was also commented upon, and Géza did not hesitate to involve himself in
1 CONC  politics. He supported the Welf party against the Hohenstaufens, and defeated Henry II of Austria in battle in 1146. He also supported his brother-in-law, Iziaslav II of Kiev, militarily, and fought a war against the Byzantine Em
1 CONC peror Manuel I Comnenus from 1149 to 1155.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1146, Géza married Euphrosyne of Kiev, daughter of Grand Duke Mstislav of Kiev. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT István III (1147-1172) 
1 CONT Béla III (1148-1196) 
1 CONT Ilona of Hungary (died 1199), married Leopold V, Duke of Austria 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Engel, Pat. Realm of St. Stephen : A History of Medieval Hungary, 2001 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Béla II King of Hungary Succeeded by:
1 CONT István III 
0 @N6000000002248211179@ NOTE Geisa II (1141-1162), whom oral tradition attributes the first colonization (therefore in 1992-1993 there was celebrated the 850th anniversary of Transilvanian Saxon presence in Siebenbürgen). 
1 CONT
1 CONT Source:Dokument.../coloniz.htm/... Erstellt: 05.11.1996. Letzte Änderung: 15.12.1996 ; Autor: Klaus Popa
0 @N6000000003949871843@ NOTE He succeeded his father in 1141 as GÉZA II King of Hungary, under the regency from 1142 of his maternal uncle Beloš of Serbia during which time Hungarian ties with Serbia were strengthened[504].  Hungarian troops assisted Géza's m
1 CONC aternal uncle Uroš II Grand Župan of Serbia in his defence against Byzantium, but the latter won a decisive victory on the River Tara in 1150.  The following year, Emperor Manuel Komnenos declared war on Hungary, besieged Zemun bu
1 CONC t withdrew without occupying Hungarian territory[505].  Peace was negotiated with Manuel I Emperor of Byzantium in 1156[506].  During the reign of Géza II, large-scale German colonisation took place in Transylvania[507].  The necr
1 CONC ology of Salzburg St Rudpert records the death "II Kal Jun" of "Geutse Ungarorum rex"[508].  The Gesta Hungarorum records that King Géza reigned for twenty years and was buried at Székesfehérvár
0 @N6000000006111920006@ NOTE GIVN Geza Ii, King
1 CONT SURN Hungary
1 CONT AFN 91R7-FX
