0 @N6000000000337169788@ NOTE (Research):Ottocar I Ottocar I Pronounced As: otkär or Premysl Ottocar I przhemisl ôtôkär , d. 1230, duke (1197-98) and king (1198-1230) of Bohemia. The struggle within the Holy Roman Empire for the imperial crown enabled Ottocar 
1 CONC to obtain (1198) from Philip of Swabia the royal title. Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II confirmed the title (1212) and the election (1216) of Ottocar's son (later Wenceslaus I) as his successor. In 1221, Ottocar was forced to surr
1 CONC ender disciplinary rights over the clergy to the bishop of Prague but retained control over secular church affairs. ======================================================== Przemysl Ottokar I, King of Bohemia 1198-1230 (1155-1230)
1 CONC   Born circa 1155   Died 15 December 1230  Married circa 1198 Konstanze of Hungary  Died 4 December 1240   Initially confirmed as Duke of Bohemia in 1192 by Emperor Heinrich VI, he was deposed the following year but regained posse
1 CONC ssion of Bohemia in 1197. In 1198 he was acknowledged as King of Bohemia by Emperor Philipp and had almost total autonomy for Bohemia. His title was later  confirmed by Emperor Friedrich II in 1212 and this extinguished the contro
1 CONC l of the Empire over Bohemian affairs. Przemysl Ottokar I then established a basis for a strong Bohemian state and the hereditary rights of his family. Source: Leo van de Pas
0 @N6000000002187828312@ NOTE Ottokar I of Bohemia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Otakar I (also spelled Ottokar or Premysl I. Otakar) (c. 1155 – December 15, 1230), king of Bohemia (1198 - 1230), was a younger son of King Vladislav II (d. 1174) and a member of the Premyslid dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT His early years were passed amid the anarchy which prevailed everywhere in the country, after several struggles, in which he took part, he was recognized as ruler of Bohemia by the emperor Henry VI in 1192. He was, however, soon o
1 CONC verthrown for joining a conspiracy of German princes to bring down the Hohenstaufen monarchy. In 1197 Otakar forced his brother, duke Vladislav Jindrich, to abandon Bohemia to him and to content himself with Moravia. Taking advant
1 CONC age of the German civil war between the Hohenstaufen claimant Philip of Swabia and the Welf candidate Otto IV, Ottokar declared himself King of Bohemia. This title was supported by Philip of Swabia who needed Czech military suppor
1 CONC t against Otto (1198).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1199 he divorced his wife, Adelheid of Meissen, a member of the Wettin (dynasty) for a marriage with Constance of Hungary, the young daughter of the Hungarian King Bela III.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1200 - with Otto IV in the ascendancy, Otakar abandoned his pact with Philip and declared for the Welf faction. Both Otto and Pope Innocent III subsequently accepted Otakar as hereditary King of Bohemia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip's consequent invasion of Bohemia was successful. Otakar, having been compelled to pay a fine, again ranged himself among Philip's partisans and still later was among the supporters of the young king, Frederick II. In 1212 F
1 CONC rederick granted the Golden Bull of Sicily to Bohemia. This document recognised Otakar and his heirs as Kings of Bohemia. The King was no longer subject to appointment by the Emperor, and was only required to attend Diets close to
1 CONC  the Bohemian border. Although a subject of the Holy Roman Empire, the Bohemian King was to be the leading electoral prince of the empire and to furnish all subsequent Emperors with a bodyguard of 300 knights when they went to Rom
1 CONC e for their coronation.
1 CONT
1 CONT Otakar's reign was also notable for the start of German immigration into Bohemia and the growth of towns in what had until that point been forest lands. In 1226 Otakar went to war against Frederick II of Austria after the latter w
1 CONC recked a deal which would have seen Otakar's daughter (Saint Agnes of Bohemia) married to Emperor Frederick II's son, Henry II of Sicily. Otakar then planned for the same daughter to marry Henry III of England, but this was vetoed
1 CONC  by the Emperor who knew Henry to be an opponent of the Hohenstaufen dynasty. Then widowed Emperor himself wanted to marry Agnes, but by then she didn't want to play a role in an arranged marriage anymore. With the help of the pop
1 CONC e, Agnes entered the convent.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Ottokar was married first in 1178 to Adelheid of Meissen (after 1160 - February 2, 1211), who gave birth to the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Vratislav (before 1181 - after 1225)
1 CONT     * Dagmar (Markéta) (ca 1186 - May 24, 1213), married to Valdemar II of Denmark, King of Denmark
1 CONT     * Božislava
1 CONT     * Hedvika
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1199, he married Constance of Hungary (1181 – December 6, 1240), who gave birth to the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Vratislav (ca 1200 – ?)
1 CONT     * Judith (- June 2, 1230), married to Bernard of Carinthia, Duke of Carinthia
1 CONT     * Anne (Anna Lehnická) (1204 - June 23, 1265), married to Henry II the Pious, Duke of Wroclaw
1 CONT     * Anežka
1 CONT     * Wenceslaus I of Bohemia (ca 1205 - September 23, 1253), King of Bohemia
1 CONT     * Vladislaus (Vratislav) (1207 - February 18, 1227), Margrave of Moravia
1 CONT     * Premyslid (Premysl) (1209 - October 16, 1239), Margrave of Moravia, married to Margaret of Meran
1 CONT     * Wilhelmina of Bohemia (Vilemína Ceská, Guglielmina Boema) (1210 - October 24, 1281)
1 CONT     * Saint Agnes of Bohemia (January 20, 1211 – March 6, 1282)
1 CONT
