0 @N6000000002188005457@ NOTE Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick II 
1 CONT King of Sicily, King of Cyprus and Jerusalem,
1 CONT King of the Romans, King of Germany
1 CONT and Holy Roman Emperor 
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign December 9, 1212 – December 13, 1250 
1 CONT Coronation September 3, 1198 
1 CONT Born December 26, 1194 
1 CONT  Jesi, Marche, Italy 
1 CONT Died December 13, 1250 
1 CONT  Castel Fiorentino, Puglia, Italy 
1 CONT Buried Cathedral of Palermo 
1 CONT Consort various 
1 CONT Issue Conrad IV of Germany 
1 CONT Royal House Hohenstaufen 
1 CONT Father Henry VI 
1 CONT Mother Constance of Sicily 
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick II (December 26, 1194 – December 13, 1250), of the Hohenstaufen dynasty, was a pretender to the title of King of the Romans from 1212 and unopposed holder of that monarchy from 1215. As such, he was King of Germany, and 
1 CONC of Italy, and of Burgundy. He was Holy Roman Emperor from his papal coronation in 1220 until his death. His original title was King of Sicily, which he held as Frederick I from 1198 to death. His other royal titles, accrued for a 
1 CONC brief period of his life, were King of Cyprus and Jerusalem by virtue of marriage and his connection with the Crusades.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was raised and lived most of his life in Sicily, his mother, Constance, being the daughter of Roger II of Sicily. His empire was frequently at war with the Papal States, so it is unsurprising that he was excommunicated twice an
1 CONC d often vilified in chronicles of the time. Pope Gregory IX went so far as to call him the Antichrist. After his death the idea of his second coming where he would rule a 1000-year reich took hold, possibly in part because of this
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT He was known in his own time as Stupor mundi ("wonder of the world"). Said to speak nine languages and be literate in seven [Armstrong 2001, p. 415] (at a time when many monarchs and nobles were not literate at all), Frederick was
1 CONC  a very modern ruler for his times, being a patron of science and the arts.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was patron of the Sicilian School of poetry. His royal court in Palermo, from around 1220 to his death, saw the first use of a literary form of an Italo-Romance language, Sicilian. The poetry that emanated from the school preda
1 CONC tes the use of the Tuscan idiom as the preferred lingua franca of the Italian peninsula by at least a century. The school and its poetry were well known to Dante and his peers and had a significant influence on the literary form o
1 CONC f what was eventually to become the modern Italian.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
