1 CONT ekteskap med Alexios I. Denne allianse ville kraftig styrke hans families anseelse ved å
1 CONT forbinde de to mektigste av aristokratiets familier. Hans plan møtte sterk motstand ved hoffet,
1 CONT men ekteskapet fant likevel sted.
1 CONT Han var onkel til Michael VII.
0 @N6000000002187821230@ NOTE Caesar John Doukas1
1 CONT b. 1012, d. 1088, #5624
1 CONT Pedigree 
1 CONT      Caesar John Doukas was born in 1012. He was the son of Andronikos Doukas.2 He married Eirene Pegonitissa, daughter of Niketas Pegonites, before 1036.2 Caesar circa 1059. He was the pretender to the Imperial Throne in 1059. He
1 CONC  was again pretender to the throne in 1073. He was bribed the German mercenaries guarding the gates of the city to permit entry to his grandson-in-law Alexius and his troops on 1 April 1081 in Constantinople, Byzantium. He died in
1 CONC  1088 at age 76 years.  
1 CONT
1 CONT http://homepages.rootsweb.com/~cousin/html/p85.htm#i5624
0 @N6000000000437115039@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Caesar
0 @N6000000003051208504@ NOTE He, along with Psellus and Nicephorus Paleologus, bureaucrats all, attempted to prevent the Emperor Romanus IV Diogenes from taking the field against the Seljuk Turks, but were unsuccessful.
1 CONT The Emperors defeat led to Johns takeover of the Varangian Guard, then he and the rest forced the Empress to take the veil, declared Romanus deposed, and proclaimed the reign of his nephew, Michael II.
1 CONT John had Romanus eyes put out in a manner guaranteed to cause mortal infection. His head became unrecognizably swollen, and he was sent to the Isle of Prote whhere he died a few days later in 1072.
0 @N6000000003637348274@ NOTE John Doukas or Ducas (Greek: ??????? ????a?, Ioannes Doukas), (died c. 1088), was the son de Andronikos Doukas, a Paphlagonian nobleman who may have served as governor de the theme de Moesia and younger brother de Emperor Constant
1 CONC ine X Doukas. John Doukas was the paternal grandfather de Irene Doukaina, wife de Emperor Alexios I Komnenos.
0 @N6000000003949741960@ NOTE In 1073, he was proclaimed emperor at Amorium by Roussel de Bailleul (commander of the Norman mercenaries), who had mutinied against Emperor Mikhael VII, and marched on Constantinople.  Emperor Mikhael sought help from the Seljuks
1 CONC , promising them east Anatolia, and they surrounded Roussel's forces on Mount Sophon in Cappadocia[215].  Ioannes became a monk as IGNATIOS in 1076.  The Alexeiad records that "the Cæsar Ioannes, his paternal uncle" advised Empero
1 CONC r Mikhael Dukas to retire to a monastery after he was deposed[216].  Imperial counsellor in 1081. 
