0 @N6000000000945071159@ NOTE Konge av Ungarn 1077 - 1095. 
1 CONT Laszlo (Ladislaus) etterfulgte sin bror Geza I. I 1088 ble han av de tyske prinser tilbudt
1 CONT keisertronen, men avslo.
1 CONT Han gjorde sitt rike uavhengig av den tyske keiser, og erobret Kroatien og Dalmatien. I
1 CONT 1091 tvang han kumanerne til å anta kristendommen. Han fremmet ro og orden, ga lover og
1 CONT var like god kriger som en vis lovgiver.
1 CONT Konsiliet i Plaisance valgte ham til leder av det første korstog. Han mottok denne
1 CONT utmerkelse, men døde en tid etter, da han bega seg til Bøhmen.
1 CONT Laszlo var første gang gift med Gisela.
1 CONT I 1198 ble han kanonisert av pave Cölestin III. Hans grav i katedralen i Nagy-Varad ble
1 CONT et søkt sted for pilgrimmer.
0 @N6000000002187690199@ NOTE Ladislaus I of Hungary
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ladislaus I, Saint (Hungarian: I. László, Slovak: Ladislav I) (June 27, 1040 – July 29, 1095) was a king of the Kingdom of Hungary (1077–1095). He was the son of Bela I, king of Hungary, and the Polish princess Richeza.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born in Poland, where his father had sought refuge, but was recalled by his elder brother Andrew I to Hungary (1047) and brought up there. He succeeded to the throne on the death of his brother Geza in 1077, as the eldest m
1 CONC ember of the royal family, and speedily won for himself a reputation scarcely inferior to that of Stephen I, by nationalizing Christianity and laying the foundations of Hungary's political greatness. Recognizing that the Holy Roma
1 CONC n Empire was a natural enemy of the Kingdom of Hungary, Ladislaus formed a close alliance with the pope and other enemies of Emperor Henry IV, including the anti-emperor Rudolph of Swabia and his chief supporter Welf, duke of Bava
1 CONC ria. He married Rudolph's daughter Adelaide, and she bore him one son and three daughters, one of whom, Piroska, married the Byzantine emperor John II Comnenus.
1 CONT
1 CONT The collapse of the German emperor in his struggle with the pope left Ladislaus free to extend his dominions towards the south, and colonize and Christianize the wildernesses of Transylvania and the lower Danube. Hungary was still
1 CONC  semi-savage, and her native barbarians were being perpetually recruited from the hordes of Pechenegs, Romanians and other races which swept over her during the 10th century. Ladislaus himself had fought valiantly in his youth aga
1 CONC inst the Pechenegs, and to defend the land against the Romanians, who now occupied Moldavia and Wallachia as far as the Olt, he built the fortresses of Turnu-Severin (Szörényvár) and Alba Iulia (Gyulafehérvár, Weißenburg).
1 CONT
1 CONT He also planted in Transylvania the Szeklers, the supposed remnant of the ancient Magyars from beyond the Dnieper, and in 1094 founded the bishoprics of Oradea (Nagyvárad, Großwardein) and of Zagreb (Zágráb, Agram) as fresh foci o
1 CONC f Catholicism to the south of Hungary and the districts between the Drave and the Sava (Slavonia). He subsequently tried to conquer other parts of Croatia after the death of his sister's husband, Croatian king Dmitar Zvonimir, tho
1 CONC ugh his authority was questioned by the Croatian nobility, the pope, the Venetian republic and the Byzantine emperor. Ladislaus made a notable incursion into the Croatian lands in 1091 and named his nephew Álmos as the viceroy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ladislaus died suddenly in 1095 when about to take part in the First Crusade. No other Hungarian king was so generally beloved. The whole nation mourned for him for three years, and regarded him as a saint long before his canoniza
1 CONC tion. A whole cycle of legends is associated with his name.
1 CONT
1 CONT C.A. Macartney, in his Hungary: A Short History, eulogizes Ladislaus thus: "Ladislas I, who, like Stephen and his son, Imre, was canonised after his death, was the outstanding personality among them: a true paladin and gentle knig
1 CONC ht, a protector of his faith and his people, and of the poor and defenceless."
1 CONT
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, which is in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Géza I King of Hungary Succeeded by:
1 CONT Coloman 
0 @N6000000002322312882@ NOTE See message #1777. He was succeeded by his brother Geza I's son, Coloman I,
1 CONT
1 CONT it internally; no other king was so generally beloved by the people. He
1 CONT introduced an elaborate legal code that brought order and prosperity to his
1 CONT dominions. Dying suddenly while preparing for the First Crusade, he was the
1 CONT ideal Hungarian knight, regarded by the nation as a saint long before his
1 CONT canonization in 1192.
