0 @N6000000003949871662@ NOTE Saint Ladislas was called (in Hungarian) Szent László. He was the king of Hungary who greatly expanded the boundaries of the kingdom and consolidated it internally; no other Hungarian king was so generally beloved by the people. T
1 CONC he son of Béla I of Hungary and the Polish princess Rycheza (Ryksa), Ladislas was born in exile. Returning to Hungary, he and his brother Géza refused to contest the throne against their cousin Salomon; however, they quarreled wit
1 CONC h him and drove him from the country in 1073. Géza took the throne, and, on his death, in 1077, Ladislas succeeded him as king of Hungary. Ladislas extended Hungary's frontier in Transylvania and occupied Croatia in 1091 to protec
1 CONC t the rights of his sister, the widow of Zvonimir, prince of Croatia. In the investiture struggle over the nomination and installation of bishops, Ladislas sided with the pope, though he also initiated a policy of reconciliation w
1 CONC ith the Holy Roman emperor Henry IV. Ladislas rooted out heathens in his dominions with severity and introduced Roman Catholicism to Croatia, founding the bishopric of Zagreb in 1091. He introduced an elaborate legal code that bro
1 CONC ught order and prosperity to his dominions. Ladislas died suddenly while preparing for the First Crusade. The ideal Hungarian knight, he was regarded by the nation as a saint long before his canonization. Ladislas I. Encyclopædia 
1 CONC Britannica. Retrieved June 25, 2003, from Encyclopædia Britannica Premium Service.
