0 @N6000000000945049489@ NOTE Albrecht tilhørte ?det askaniske hus? og var hertug av Sachsen 1212-1260. 
1 CONT Albrecht var sin fars annen sønn og ble hertug ved farens død i 1212. Hertugdømmet
1 CONT omfattet de anhaltiske stambesittelser omkring Wittenberg og de nyervervede besittelser ved
1 CONT Unterlebe.
1 CONT Høsten 1226 startet nye krigshandlinger mellom kong Valdemar Sejr og greve Henrik av
1 CONT Schwerin, da Valdemar gikk over Ejderen. Valdemar inntok Rendsborg, og samtidig fikk han
1 CONT støtte sydfra av greve Otto av Lüneburg, som fortsatt var lojal. Det var på dette tidspunkt at
1 CONT greve Henrik og hans allierte nordtyske grever måtte be den sachsiske hertug om hjelp. Den
1 CONT skulle de også få, men på hårde betingelser. Han ønsket anerkjennelse av sin lensoverhøyhet
1 CONT over hele Nord-Tyskland, eller med andre ord samme stilling som i sin tid Henrik Løve hadde
1 CONT hatt. Men uten hertugens bistand kunne grevene ikke regne med å motstå Valdemars angrep.
1 CONT De gikk derfor med på hertugens betingelser. I slaget ved Bornhøved 22.07.1227 førte
1 CONT Albrecht den venstre fløy.
1 CONT Etter at Agnes døde var han gift ytterligere to ganger.
1 CONT Han var den egentlige grunnlegger av hertugdømmet Lauenburg.
0 @N6000000000945049173@ NOTE Dronning av Danmark. 
1 CONT Jutta nevnes som vitne i det brev hvori kong Erik få måneder etter sin fars død uttalte
1 CONT sitt ønske om å få dø i Franciskanenes ordensdrakt og begraves i deres klosterkirke i Roskilde.
1 CONT Ellers er det kun opplyst om henne at hun en tid residerte på Skanderborg og derfra i høy grad
1 CONT plaget munkene i Øm kloster. Det ble klaget over at hun sendte sine svenner ut for å ta
1 CONT klosterets korn og føre munkenes gods på båter til Skanderborg. Jutta fødte sin ektefelle
1 CONT mange barn, om det var sønner blandt disse må de ha vært døde før faren. Av hennes fire
1 CONT døtre ble to dronninger, Sophie i Sverige og Ingeborg i Norge, mens Jutta og Agnes en tid ble
1 CONT satt i kloster.
1 CONT Etter drapet på Erik i 1250 vendte Jutta vistnok tilbake til Tyskland. En datter, Sophie, i
1 CONT hennes 2. ekteskap med Grev Burchard av Rosenburg, borggreve i Magdeburg, ble gift med
1 CONT Erik (Langben) av Langeland.
1 CONT Jutta ble gift tredje gang 07.05.1255 med Johan I av Brandenburg som døde
1 CONT 04.04.1266.
0 @N6000000004533589516@ NOTE <b>Jutta,</b> -1250-, Dronning, var en Datter af den sachsiske HertugAlbrecht (d. 1260) af det askaniske Hus i hans 1. Ægteskab, med denøsterrigske Hertugdatter Agnes, og ægtede 1239 den danske Konge ErikPlovpenning. Hun nævnes so
1 CONC m Vidne i det Brev, hvori hendes Ægtefællefaa Maaneder efter Faderen Valdemar Sejers Død udtalte sit Ønske om atdø i Minoriternes Dragt og blive begravet i denne Ordens Kirke iRoskilde. Ellers haves om
1 CONT J. blot den tilfældige Oplysning, at hun, medens hun boede paaSkanderborg, i høj Grad plagede Munkene i Øm Kloster; hun søgte attage det Korn, de høstede, fra dem og lod deres Gods paa Baade føretil sit Slot. J. fødte sin Mand ads
1 CONC killige Børn; det er usikkert, omder deriblandt har været Sønner, og hvis saa var, døde de i detmindste før Faderen; derimod kjendes 4 af hendes Døtre, hvoraf de tobleve Dronninger, Sophie i Sverige og
1 CONT Ingeborg i Norge (ovfr. S. 282), medens Jutta (s. ndfr.) og Agnes (I,119) en Tid vare klostergivne. Efter Eriks Drab 1250 er J. vistnoksnart draget tilbage til sit tyske Hjem, og hun ægtede siden en GrevBurchard af Rosenburg, der 
1 CONC var Borggreve i Magdeburg; en Datter
1 CONT af dette Ægteskab var Sophie, der blev gift med Erik af Langeland (IV,556).
1 CONT
1 CONT <i>Kr. Erslev.</i>
0 @N6000000006128299062@ NOTE Född  ca 1206  Död  fö 1247  
0 @N6000000002188008201@ NOTE Albert II, Margrave of Meissen
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert II, the Degenerate (de: Albrecht II der Entartete) (b. 1240 – d. Erfurt, 20 November 1314), was a Margrave of Meissen, Landgrave of Thuringia and Count Palatine of Saxony. He was a member of the House of Wettin.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the eldest son of Henry the Illustrious, Margrave of Meissen by his first wife, Constantia of Austria.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT In 1265 the Margrave Henry III granted the Landgraviate of Thuringia and the Palatinate to Albrecht and the Margraviate of Landsberg in the Osterland to his younger brother Dietrich. Henry III kept from himself the Margraviates of
1 CONC  Meissen and Lausitz as a formal power over his sons.
1 CONT
1 CONT In June 1255 Albrecht married with Margaret of Sicily, the daughter of Emperor Frederick II. As a dowry the Pleissnerland was pledged to the house of Wettin. They had five children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Heinrich (b. 21 March 1256 - d. 25 January/23 July? 1282), inherited the Pleissnerland in 1274. 
1 CONT Frederick (b. 1257 - d. Wartburg, 16 November 1323), Margrave of Meisse
1 CONT Dietrich, called Dietzmann (b. 1260 - murdered Leipzig, 10 December 1307), Margrave of Lausitz. 
1 CONT Margaret (d. young, aft. 17 April 1273). 
1 CONT Agnes (b. 1264 - d. September 1332), married before 21 July 1282 to Heinrich I, Duke of Brünswick-Grubenhagen. 
1 CONT After what was at first a happy rule and marriage, Albrecht turned away from Margaret and began a passionate love affair with Kunigunde of Eisenberg. She bore him two children: a daughter, Elisabeth in 1264, and a son, Albrecht ("
1 CONC Apitz") in 1270.
1 CONT
1 CONT When she discovered the adultery and the illegitimate births, Margaret left Wartburg on 24 June 1270 and went to Frankfurt am Main where she died on 8 August of the same year. The two younger sons, Frederick and Dietzmann were loo
1 CONC ked after by their uncle, Dietrich, Margrave of Landsberg. Henry, the oldest, disappeared in Silesia in 1282.
1 CONT
1 CONT Albrecht married with Kunigunde in 1274 and legitimised their children. When Albrecht intended leave to Apitz the Landgraviate of Thuringia and compensating his sons from his first marriage only with the Osterland (who included th
1 CONC e inheritance of their mother) and the Palatinate County of Saxony, they began the war against their father. Frederick was captured by his father and was locked on Wartburg; however, he could escape one year later and continue the
1 CONC  war against his father together with Dietzmann. During this time, in 1284, their uncle Dietrich of Landsberg died, and four years later, in 1288, also died Heinrich the Illustrious, Albrecht's father. These deaths tightened the f
1 CONC amily disputes.
1 CONT
1 CONT By the death of his father Albrecht became a Margrave of Meissen, while his nephew Frederick Tuta -son of Dietrich of Landsberg- inherited the Margraviate of Lausitz, who was lost by Albrecht's son Dietzmann in that year. Shortly 
1 CONC after Frederick capture his father Albrecht in battle. By the Treaty of Rochlitz (1 January 1289) Albrecht obtain his freedom after the renunciation of big parts of his lands. He retain for himself Meissen, but he finally sold it 
1 CONC to Frederick Tuta. When after his death (1291) his cousins Frederick and Dietzmann arbitrarily took possession of his lands, Albrecht -with financial difficulties- was compeled to sell Thuringia in 1293 to the German King Adolf of
1 CONC  Nassau; in the contract, was estipulated the king could take posesion of the lands after Albrecht's death. In the sell, Albrecht included Meissen and Osterland as his fiefs, despite the fact they were in the hands of his sons. Th
1 CONC anks to this, Adolf's successor Albrecht I of Habsburg was able to take possession of these lands, claimed the contract of sell was legitimate and lawful.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kunigunde of Eisenberg died on 31 October 1286. Four years later, on 1 October 1290, Albrecht married thirdly with Elisabeth of Orlamünde, heiress of Nordhalben and widow of Hartmann XI of Lobdeburg-Arnshaugk. The same year, Apitz
1 CONC , Albrecht's son by Kunigunde, was formally legitimazed by the Emperor and created Herr of Tenneberg.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 11 April 1291 Apitz's younger full-sister, Elisabeth, married with Heinrich III of Frankenstein; the marriage was short-living and childless; Elisabeth died on 28 September 1293[1]. Three years later (9 October 1296), Apitz mar
1 CONC ried with another member of the Frankenstein family. These marriage, like his sister, was childless.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ten years later (24 August 1300) Albrecht's eldest surviving son, Frederick, married with Elisabeth of Lobdeburg-Arnshaugk, daughter of his step-mother; this make the final reconciliation between father and son. Five years later (
1 CONC 27 June 1305) Apitz of Tenneberg died, aged thirty-five. The death of his favorite son was a terrible blow to Albrecht. He was never recovered by the lost.
1 CONT
1 CONT Two years later, in 1307, Albrecht finally resigned the Landgraviate of Thuringia and the County Palatine of Saxony to his son Frederick in exchange for an annuity. He died seven years later in Erfurt, aged seventy-four.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT List of Margraves of Meißen 
1 CONT Wettin (dynasty) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ ThePeerage.com. In this site Elisabeth's death is cited after 23 April 1326 and her husband is named Heinrich II, not III. Also, the death of Elisabeth's brother Apitz is cited on 27 June 1301, four years before of the current d
1 CONC ate of the article. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Henry III Landgrave of Thuringia
1 CONT 1288–1292 Succeeded by
1 CONT Frederick I 
1 CONT Margrave of Meissen
1 CONT 1288–1292 
