0 @N6000000000437681206@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Calixtus II, Pope of Rome
0 @N6000000005598851835@ NOTE Guy is also styled 'Guido, Comte de Bourgogne' and as a pretender to N ormandy in 1047 & later Pope Calixtus II (or Callixtus, Callistus). H e was Pope from 1119 until 1124.  In October of 1919, Guy, as Pope Cal ixtus, met with He
1 CONC nry I of England at Chaumont-en-Vixen near Gisors. There Henry I postrated himseld before the Pope.  They discussed the c laims made by William Clito and Guy advised Henry I to make peace wit h Louis VI of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the successor of Pope Gelasius II. He was the archbishop of of Vienne during the investiture controversy with Holy Roman Emperor Henr y V.  When Gelasius died while in exile in France, Calixtus was consec rated pope at Vien
1 CONC ne.  He immediately summoned a large council at Reim s (1119) that proceeded to anathematize the emperor and the antipope t hat Henry had installed (1118), Gregory VIII. Public reaction sided wi th the pope and the antipope was im
1 CONC prisoned. Henry thereupon agreed t o sign (1122) the famous Concordat of Worms, a compromise that recogni zed the rights of the church in selecting its leadership. Thus was th e investiture controversy ended and the reform program
1 CONC  of Gregory VII realized. Calixtus then called to Rome (1123) a great council in Weste rn Europe (the Lateran Council, First) to ratify the achievements of P ope Gregory VII. He was succeeded byHonorius II.
0 @N6000000002187750805@ NOTE Pope Callixtus II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Blessed Calixtus II
1 CONT Birth name Guy de Vienne
1 CONT Papacy began February 1, 1119
1 CONT Papacy ended December 13, 1124
1 CONT Predecessor Gelasius II
1 CONT Successor Honorius II
1 CONT Born  ???
1 CONT Quingey, France
1 CONT Died December 13, 1124
1 CONT Rome, Italy
1 CONT Other popes named Callixtus
1 CONT
1 CONT Blessed Pope Callixtus II (or Calistus II) (died December 13, 1124), born Guy de Vienne, the son of William I, Count of Burgundy (1057–87), was elected Pope on February 1, 1119, after the death of Pope Gelasius II (1118–19). His p
1 CONC ontificate was shaped by the Investiture Controversy, which he was able to settle through the Concordat of Worms (in 1122).
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early life
1 CONT     * 2 Church career
1 CONT           o 2.1 Archbishop of Vienne
1 CONT           o 2.2 Cardinal
1 CONT     * 3 Papacy
1 CONT           o 3.1 Concordat of Worms
1 CONT           o 3.2 First Lateran Council
1 CONT     * 4 Later life and death
1 CONT     * 5 References
1 CONT     * 6 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a member of the highest aristocracy. One sister, Gisela, was married to Humbert II of Savoy (1080–1103) and then Renier I of Montferrat; another, Maud to Eudes I of Burgundy (1079–1103). His brother Raymond was married to U
1 CONC rraca, the heiress of Castile, parents of Alfonso VII of Castile. His brother Hugh had been appointed Archbishop of Besançon. His distant cousin Henry was married to Theresa, the heiress of Portugal, parents of Afonso I of Portuga
1 CONC l.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Church career
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Archbishop of Vienne
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Investiture Controversy
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1088, he became the Archbishop of Vienne. He held strong pro-Papal views about the Investiture Controversy. As archbishop, he was appointed papal legate in France by Pope Paschal II (1099–1118) during the time that Paschal II, 
1 CONC yielding to pressure from Emperor Henry V (1105–25), was induced to issue the Privilegium of 1111, by which he yielded much of the papal prerogatives that had been so forcefully claimed by Pope Gregory VII (1073–85) in the Gregori
1 CONC an Reforms. Guido, with kin both in Burgundy and the Franche Comté, that is within the Emperor's jurisdiction and bordering it, led the pro-Papal opposition at the synod called at the Lateran in 1112; on his return to France he im
1 CONC mediately convoked an assembly of French and Burgundian bishops at Vienne, where the imperial claim to traditional lay investiture of the clergy was denounced as heretical, and a sentence of excommunication was now pronounced agai
1 CONC nst Henry V, on the grounds that he had extorted the Privilegium from Paschal II by violence. These decrees were sent to Paschal II with a request for confirmation, which they received, in general terms, for Paschal II had proved 
1 CONC loath to take this step, October 20, 1112.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Cardinal
1 CONT
1 CONT Guido was apparently made cardinal by Paschal II, who died January 21, 1118.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Papacy
1 CONT
1 CONT During the violent confrontations between Henry V and Paschal II's successor, Pope Gelasius II, the Pope was forced to flee from Rome, first to Gaeta, where he was crowned, then to the Abbey of Cluny, where he died, January 29, 11
1 CONC 19. Within four days Guido was elected Pope and was crowned at Vienne as Calixtus II. At the outset it appeared that the new Pope was willing to negotiate with Henry V, who received the papal embassy at Strasburg, and withdrew his
1 CONC  support from the antipope he had proclaimed at Rome. It was agreed that pope and emperor should meet at the château de Mousson, near Rheims, and in October he opened the council at Rheims attended by Louis VI of France (1108–37),
1 CONC  with most of the barons of France and more than four hundred bishops and abbots. Henry V arrived for his personal conference at Mousson — not alone, as had been anticipated, but with an army of over thirty thousand men. Calixtus 
1 CONC II, fearing that force was likely to be used at Mousson to extract from him prejudicial concessions, remained at Rheims. There, while Calixtus II busied himself ineffectively with attempting a reconciliation between the brothers H
1 CONC enry I of England (1100–35) and Robert II, Duke of Normandy (1087–1106), and after the council had busied itself with disciplinary regulations and decrees against lay investiture, simony, and clerical concubines, there being no co
1 CONC mpromise coming from Henry V, it was determined that the Emperor and his antipope should be solemnly excommunicated, 30 October 1119.
1 CONT
1 CONT Returning to Italy, where antipope Gregory VIII (1118–21) was supported in Rome by imperial forces and Italian allies of the emperor, Calixtus II managed to gain the upper hand amid clear popular demonstrations and the Imperial ca
1 CONC ndidate was obliged to flee to the fortress of Sutri, where he was taken prisoner through the intervention of Norman support from the Kingdom of Naples where he was transferred from prison to prison and died at a stronghold near S
1 CONC alerno. The imperial allies in Rome soon disbanded.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Concordat of Worms
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Concordat of Worms
1 CONT
1 CONT Having established his power in Italy, he resolved to reopen negotiations with Henry V on the question of investiture. Henry V was anxious to put an end to a controversy which had reduced imperial authority in Germany — terminally
1 CONC  so, as it appeared in the long run. An embassy of three cardinals was sent by Calixtus II to Germany, and negotiations for a permanent settlement of the investiture struggle were begun in October 1121 at Würzburg where it was agr
1 CONC eed that a general truce should be proclaimed in Germany; that the Church should have free use of its possessions; that the lands of those in rebellion should be restored. These decrees were communicated to Calixtus II, who despat
1 CONC ched a legate Lambert to assist at the synod that had been convoked at Worms, where, on 23 September 1122, the concordat known as the Concordat of Worms was concluded. On his side the emperor abandoned his claim to investiture wit
1 CONC h ring and crosier and granted freedom of election to episcopal sees; on the other hand, it was conceded that the bishops should receive investiture with the sceptre, that the episcopal elections should be held in the presence of 
1 CONC the Emperor or his representatives, that in case of disputed elections the emperor should, after the decision of the metropolitan and the suffragan bishops, confirm the rightfully elected candidate, and lastly, that the imperial i
1 CONC nvestiture of the temporal properties connected to the sees should take place in Germany before the consecration, in Burgundy and in Italy after this ceremony, while in the Papal States the pope alone had the right of investiture,
1 CONC  without any interference on the part of the Emperor. As a result of this Concordat, the Emperor still retained in his hands the controlling influence in the election of the bishops in Germany, though he had abandoned much in rega
1 CONC rd to episcopal elections in Italy and Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] First Lateran Council
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: First Council of the Lateran
1 CONT
1 CONT To secure the confirmation of this Concordat of Worms, Calixtus II convoked the First Lateran Council, 18 March 1123, which solemnly confirmed the concordat and passed several disciplinary decrees, such as those against simony and
1 CONC  concubinage among the clergy. Decrees were also passed against violators of the Truce of God, church-robbers, and forgers of ecclesiastical documents. The indulgences already granted to the crusaders were renewed, and the jurisdi
1 CONC ction of the bishops over the clergy, both secular and regular, was more clearly defined.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Later life and death
1 CONT
1 CONT Calixtus II devoted his last few years to reestablishing Papal control over the Campagna and establishing – with the aid of some forgeries (CE) – the primacy of his see of Vienne over the see of Arles, an ancient conflict. He rebu
1 CONC ilt the church of Santa Maria in Cosmedin, in Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT Calixtus II died 13 December 1124. His feast day is celebrated on 12 December.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
