0 @N6000000000437042336@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Count Name Suffix:<NSFX> of Flanders
0 @N6000000002187903937@ NOTE William Clito
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT William Clito (October 25, 1102 – July 28, 1128) was the son of Robert Curthose, Duke of Normandy, by his marriage with Sibylla of Conversano. He had a claim on both Normandy and England, and became count of Flanders.
1 CONT
1 CONT His surname 'Clito' was a Latin term equivalent to the Anglo-Saxon "Aetheling", and the Germanic "Adelinus" (used to refer to his first cousin William Adelin, the son of Henry I). Both 'Clito' and 'Atheling' signified 'man of roya
1 CONC l blood', or the modern equivalent 'prince'.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 History and Family
1 CONT           o 1.1 Youth
1 CONT           o 1.2 First Norman Rebellion, 1118-19
1 CONT           o 1.3 Second Norman Rebellion, 1123-24
1 CONT           o 1.4 Count of Flanders
1 CONT     * 2 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] History and Family
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Youth
1 CONT
1 CONT After his father's defeat and capture by Henry I of England at the Battle of Tinchebrai (1106) the young William fell into the king's hands. Henry placed his nephew in the custody of Helias of Saint Saens, count of Arques, who had
1 CONC  married a natural daughter of Duke Robert, his friend and patron. The decision seems to have been intended to conciliate any suspicion of the king’s intentions to his nephew. The boy William stayed in his sister’s and Helias's ca
1 CONC re until August 1110, when the king abruptly sent agents to demand the boy be handed over to him. Helias was at the time away from home, so his household concealed the boy and smuggled him to their master, who fled the duchy and l
1 CONC ost his lands as a result.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] First Norman Rebellion, 1118-19
1 CONT
1 CONT William’s first refuge was with King Henry’s great enemy, Robert de Bellême, who had extensive estates south of the duchy. On Robert’s capture in 1112, William and Helias fled to the court of the young Count Baldwin VII of Flander
1 CONC s, William’s cousin. In 1118 a powerful coalition of Norman counts and barons were sufficiently disenchanted with King Henry to ally with Count Baldwin and rebel. They took up William Clito’s cause and commenced a dangerous rebell
1 CONC ion.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Norman border counts and Count Baldwin between them were too powerful for the king and seized much of the north of the duchy. But the promising campaign abruptly ended with Baldwin’s serious injury at the siege of Arques (Sept
1 CONC ember 1118). The next year the cause of William Clito was taken up by Louis VI of France. He invaded the duchy down the river Seine, and on 20 August 1119 was met by the troops of King Henry at the Battle of Brémule, where the Fre
1 CONC nch were decisively defeated.
1 CONT
1 CONT William had ridden as a new knight amongst the king’s guard that day, and barely escaped capture. His cousin, King Henry’s son, William Adelin, the next day sent him back the horse he had lost in the battle with other ‘necessities
1 CONC ’ in a courtly gesture. The rebellion collapsed, but William continued to find support at the French court. Louis brought his case to the pope’s attention in October 1119 at Reims, and forced Henry I to justify his treatment of th
1 CONC e exiled boy.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Second Norman Rebellion, 1123-24
1 CONT
1 CONT The death by drowning of William Atheling, King Henry’s only legitimate son, on 25 November 1120 transformed William Clito’s fortunes. He was now the obvious male heir to England and Normandy, and a significant party of Norman ari
1 CONC stocrats adopted his cause in 1122, led by Amaury de Montfort, count of Evreux and Waleran de Beaumont, count of Meulan.
1 CONT
1 CONT William’s position was bolstered further by a strategic marriage to Sibylla of Anjou, daughter of Count Fulk V of Anjou. The marriage was supposed to bring William the county of Maine, between Normandy and Anjou, as her dowry. Kin
1 CONC g Henry astutely appealed to canon law, however, and the marriage was eventually annulled in August 1124 on the grounds that the couple were within the prohibited degrees of kinship.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the meantime, a serious aristocratic rebellion broke out in Normandy in favour of William, but was defeated by Henry’s intelligence network and the lack of organisation of the leaders, who were defeated at the battle of Bourgth
1 CONC eroulde in March 1124. Louis VI was distracted from active intervention as Henry I got his son-in-law, the Emperor Henry V, to threaten Louis from the east.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Count of Flanders
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis VI made great efforts to further William’s cause in 1127. In January he granted him the royal estates in the French Vexin as a base to attack down the Seine into Normandy, and he was married to the queen’s half sister. The m
1 CONC urder of Count Charles the Good of Flanders on 2 March 1127 gave King Louis an even better chance to further William’s fortunes. He marched into Flanders at the head of an army and on 30 March got the barons of the province to acc
1 CONC ept William as their new count.
1 CONT
1 CONT Initially, William did well, securing most of the county by the end of May. But English money and the emergence of a rival in Thierry of Alsace led to a deterioration in his position. In February 1128 St Omer and Ghent declared ag
1 CONC ainst him, as did Bruges in March. In May 1128 Lille too welcomed Thierry, leaving William controlling little more than the southern fringe of Flanders. However, he struck back at Bruges and at the battle of Axspoele south of the 
1 CONC town on 21 June, William defeated Thierry with his Norman knights and French allies.
1 CONT
1 CONT At this point he was joined by his father-in-law, Duke Godfrey of Brabant, and together their armies besieged Aalst on 12 July, with the probable intention of going on from there to reduce Ghent. But during the course of the siege
1 CONC  he was wounded in the arm in a scuffle with a foot soldier. The wound became gangrenous and William died at the age of twenty-five on 28 July 1128, attended to the end by his faithful brother-in-law, Helias of Saint Saens. Willia
1 CONC m’s body was carried to the abbey of St Bertin in St Omer and buried there. He left no children and was survived by his imprisoned father by six years.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Galbert of Bruges, The Murder of Charles the Good, trans. J.B. Ross (repr. Toronto, 1982)
1 CONT     * Sandy Burton Hicks, "The Impact of William Clito upon the Continental Policies of Henry I of England," Viator 10 (1979), 1-21.
1 CONT     * D. Crouch, The Normans: History of a Dynasty (London, 2002)
1 CONT     * C.W. Hollister, Henry I (New Haven, 2001)
1 CONT     * J.A. Green Henry I (Cambridge, 2006)
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Charles I Count of Flanders
1 CONT 1127–1128 Succeeded by
1 CONT Thierry
0 @N6000000005598882183@ NOTE William is styled as a man of 'considerable charm and military prowess )'.  At the time William was about five years old, he was entrusted b y his uncle Henry to the care of Curthose's son-in-law Elias de St. Sa ens - Count of Arq
1 CONC ues (?).  After 1106, William is reported to have as serted his rights as Duc of Normandie.  In this effort he was supporte d by King Louis VI of France.  In 1110-1111, Henry I ordered Robert d e Beauchamp to take his nephew Willi
1 CONC am Clito by suprise at the castle of Saint-Saens, but the child was snatched from his bed and taken eas t out of Normandie.  Helias of Saint-Saens quickly followed and becam e William's protector and tutor.  At the time of the Bat
1 CONC tle of Tincheb ray, William met his uncle Henry I.  William had a peaceful aristocrat ic upbringing in upper Normandie.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert de Belleme soon offered aid and comfort to William in hopes of enticing hime into an alliance against Henry I of England.  In 1119 Cl ito was involved in a plot to murder his uncle Henry I.  In August of 1119, William was w
1 CONC ith Louis VI against Henry I of England.  With the battle lost, William escaped on foot.   Henry I of England offered Wil liam three counties in England for his future support. In 1123, Willia m began an attack upon Henry I's terr
1 CONC itories in sourthern Normandie. After securing the title as Count of Flanders in 1127 with the help o f Louis VI, the Flemish found William unacceptable.  But prior to any action could be taken against him, while leading an assult
1 CONC  on Thierry of Alsace's castle at Alost, William was mortally wounded.  He was pos thumously enrolled as a monk at Saint-Bertin in Saint-Omer and buried.
