0 @N6000000002187747450@ NOTE Henry I of Constantinople
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT (Redirected from Henry of Flanders)
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Henry (c. 1174–1216), was the second emperor of the Latin Empire of Constantinople.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a younger son of Baldwin V, Count of Hainaut (later Baldwin VIII, count of Flanders), and Margaret I of Flanders, sister of Philip of Alsace, count of Flanders.
1 CONT
1 CONT Having joined the Fourth Crusade about 1201, he distinguished himself at the siege of Constantinople in 1204 and elsewhere, and soon became prominent among the princes of the new Latin Empire. An ambush was laid for him by Emperor
1 CONC  Alexius V at Philia, but his army routed the Greeks and sent them fleeing.
1 CONT
1 CONT When his elder brother, the emperor Baldwin I, was captured at the Battle of Adrianople in April 1205, Henry was chosen regent of the empire, succeeding to the throne when the news of Baldwin’s death arrived, He was crowned August
1 CONC  20, 1205.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was a wise ruler, whose reign was largely passed in successful struggles with Kaloyan, Tsar of Bulgaria, and with his rival, Theodore I Lascaris, emperor of Nicaea. Henry appears to have been brave but not cruel, and toleran
1 CONC t but not weak; possessing "the superior courage to oppose, in a superstitious age, the pride and avarice of the clergy." The emperor died, poisoned, it is said, by his Greek wife, on June 11, 1216.
1 CONT
1 CONT Some contemporary historians say that Henry made a peace with Bulgarians after the death of Tsar Kaloyan and even years later by The Pope ordet he contracted a marriage with the only Kaloyan's child — daughter Maria. That marriage
1 CONC  (Henry's second one) doesn't leave heirs as well as even his only daughter (by his firts wife Agness) — Isabelle, died in childbirth. Some sources claim that Maria is involved in Henry's poisoning. Others point Boniface of Montfe
1 CONC rrat as Henry's poisoner.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Queller, Donald. The Fourth Crusade: The Conquest of Constantinople (Middle Ages), 1999 
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, which is in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Baldwin I Latin Emperor
1 CONT 1205–1216 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Peter 
