0 @N6000000004303404286@ NOTE FROM CURT HOFEMAN:
1 CONT
1 CONT Luisa (Aluigia) del Vasto de Ceva 
1 CONT
1 CONT Note: SGM names her Ceva while Ancestral Roots names her Cave. (There are towns known by both names in Italy). I will go with SGM. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Aluigia del Vasto of Ceva [Ref: Richard Borthwick, 26 Jan 1999] 
1 CONT ********** 
1 CONT It would appear that even though Weis for 28:31 cited CP I:241-242 as a source of his data, he apparently transposed the toponym CEVA in CP to CAVE in AR7 & his editors missed it. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Name: Luisa, daughter of Giorgio, Marquis of Ceva [Ref: CP I:240] 
1 CONT Name: Luigia de CEVA [Ref: Turton p97 citing: Guichenon, S., _Hist. de Savoie_, 1660] 
1 CONT Name: Luisa di Ceva [Ref: Moriarty Plantagenet] 
1 CONT Name: Louisa di Giorgia, Marquess of Ceva [Ref: Ronny Bodine 18 Feb 1997 citing: volume 11 of Pompeo Litta's Famiglie celebri italiani (Milan: Paolo Emilio Giusti, 1819-1871), 1st series] 
1 CONT
1 CONT Note that in 3 of his msgs to SGM, Richard Borthwick cited the same source, 
1 CONT *Genealogie medioevali di Sardegna* eds. L L Brook, F C Casula, M M Costa, A M Oliva, R Pavoni & M Tangheroni (Publicazione della Deputazione di Storia Patria per la Sardegna, 1983), pp 109 (tav. XVIII), 131 (tav.XXIX) 
1 CONT
1 CONT but gave 3 versions of the same name: 
1 CONT
1 CONT Aluigia di Ceva [Ref: Richard Borthwick 10 Dec 1998] 
1 CONT Aluigia del VASTO (dau of Giorgio I del VASTO, M of Ceva) [Ref: Richard Borthwick 26 Jan 1999] 
1 CONT Aluiga di Ceva [Ref: Richard Borthwick 12 Aug 1999] 
1 CONT
1 CONT I am a bit curious at the use of "del Vasto" for numerous generations of this line. AFAIK, it means "the Fat" or "the Huge". In a time period where there were no surnames, why would an epithet for one person, be also used for his 
1 CONC descendants for a number of generations? Richard Borthwick is deservedly well known for his scholarship in genealogy & I know very little about Italian genealogy & naming patterns so it is likely that I'm misunderstanding this.
0 @N6000000000337139979@ NOTE (Research):Manfred Encyclop‘dia Britannica Article    born c. 1232 died Feb. 26, 1266, near Benevento, Kingdom of Naples   Italian Manfredi  effective king of Sicily from 1258, during a period of civil wars and succession disputes
1 CONC  between imperial claimants and the House of Anjou.   The son of the Holy Roman emperor Frederick II, Manfred became vicar of Italy and Sicily for his half brother Conrad IV but soon began seeking the Sicilian crown for himself. O
1 CONC n Conrad's death in 1254 a diet at San Germano ignored the imperial representative and elected Manfred. Pope Alexander IV, however, after having excommunicated Manfred twice, invested Edmund, son of Henry III of England, with the 
1 CONC Sicilian kingdom in April 1255. A papal army entered the kingdom, but Manfred resisted successfully and was crowned king of Sicily at Palermo on Aug. 10, 1258.   As protector of the Italian Ghibellines, Manfred asserted himself al
1 CONC so in Lombardy and Tuscany; and he further strengthened his position by the betrothal, in 1260, of his daughter Constance to the infante Peter of Aragon. Negotiations with the new pope, Urban IV, came to nothing; and Urban, consid
1 CONC ering Alexander IV's agreement with England void, offered the Sicilian crown to Charles of Anjou, who sailed for Rome in May 1265. Manfred, having failed to prevent Charles's army from joining him, was defeated near Benevento; he 
1 CONC fell in battle. ======================================================== Manfredo, King of Sicily 1258-1266 (1232-1266)  Born 1232 Venosa Died 26 February 1266 (in battle)   Married (1) circa 1248 Beatrice de Savoie, daughter of A
1 CONC madeo IV, Count of Savoie, Duke of Chablais & Aosta and Anne (Marguerite) de Bourgogne Died before 10 May 1259   Married (2) 2 June 1259 Trani   Elena Dukaina Angelina, Heiress of Corfu, daughter of   Michael II Komnenos Dukas Not
1 CONC hos Angelos, Archon of Epirus   and Atolia and Theodora Dukaina Petraliphaina, St.Theodora   of Arta   Born circa 1242   Died July 1271 Lucera (imprisoned)     Known as "bello e biondo", he was born illegitimate. His mother, Bianc
1 CONC a Lancia, a Sicilian noblewoman, is said to have married his father, Emperor Friedrich II, on his deathbed.   When about seventeen, Manfredo married B,atrice de Savoie and by her fathered two children. In his early years he had be
1 CONC en a great favourite of his father, sharing literature and other tastes. Before the Emperor died, he appointed Manfredo Viceroy of Southern Italy and Sicily.   On the death of his half-brother, Konrad IV, Manfredo supported   Konr
1 CONC ad IV's son, Konradin. However, as the two-year-old Konradin was in Germany with his mother, Manfredo was crowned King of Sicily in 1258, taking up residence at Barletta. As he was a widower, in April 1259 he took for his second w
1 CONC ife Helena Angela, a daughter of Michael I of Epirus, and by her fathered another three children. However, Pope Urban IV, continuing the feud against the Hohenstaufens, excommunicated Manfredo and handed the kingdom of Sicily to C
1 CONC harles of Anjou, a brother of the French King Louis IX. Charles of Anjou brought a well-equipped army to Beneventum where the two opponents met. As the battle turned against Manfredo and with his German barons defecting, he tried 
1 CONC to inspire his men by going into the   thick of the battle with great valour but was killed. The pope disallowed him a Christian burial and, as well, Charles of Anjou imprisoned Manfredo's widow, Helena, and her young children. On
1 CONC ly   Constance, Manfredo's surviving child by his first wife, escaped imprisonment.    Source: Leo van de Pas
