0 @N6000000005598851997@ NOTE Henry I of Brabant (French: Henri I de Brabant, Dutch: Hendrik I van B rabant) was (probably) born in 1165 in Leuven and died in the German c ity of Cologne on September 5, 1235. He became Duke of Brabant in 1183 /1184 and succeed
1 CONC ed his father as Duke of Lower Lotharingia in 1190. H e was the son of Godfrey III of Leuven, Duke of Lower Lotharingia andl andgrave of Brabant, and Margaret of Limburg. He went on two Crusades to the Holy Land, in 1183 and again
1 CONC  in 1197.  He was the first to use the title Duke of of Brabant, as well as that of the Duke of Lothair, or Lower Lorraine.
1 CONT
1 CONT Under Henry I, there was a town policy and town planning. Henry's atte ntion went out to those regions that lent themselves to the extensiono f his sovereignty and in some locations he used the creation of a new town as an instrum
1 CONC ent in the political organisation of the area. Amon g the towns to which the Duke gave city rights and trade privileges wa s 's-Hertogenbosch.  In 1205, Henri joined Phillippe of France and cla imed various English estates in Norm
1 CONC andie in the right of his wife.
0 @N6000000002187746517@ NOTE Henry I, Duke of Brabant
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry I of Brabant (French: Henri I de Brabant, Dutch: Hendrik I van Brabant) was (probably) born in 1165 in Leuven and died in the German city of Cologne on September 5, 1235. He became Duke of Brabant in 1183/1184 and succeeded 
1 CONC his father as Duke of Lower Lotharingia in 1190. He was the son of Godfrey III of Leuven, Duke of Lower Lotharingia and landgrave of Brabant, and Margaret of Limburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Maud of Boulogne and Alsace, daughter of Marie of Boulogne and Matthew of Alsace (also called Maud of Flanders) in 1179. He had six children by his first marriage:
1 CONT
1 CONT Marie (c. 1190 – May 1260), married in Maastricht after May 19, 1214 Otto IV, Holy Roman Emperor, married July 1220 Count William I of Holland 
1 CONT Adelaide (b. c. 1190), married 1206 Arnulf, Count of Loos, married February 3, 1225 William X of Auvergne (c. 1195–1247), married before April 21, 1251 Arnold van Wesemaele (d. aft. 1288) 
1 CONT Margaret (1192–1231), married January 1206 Gerhard III, Count of Guelders (d. October 22, 1229) 
1 CONT Mathilde (c. 1200 – December 22, 1267), married in Aachen in 1212 Henry II, Count Palatine of the Rhine (d. 1214), married on December 6, 1244 Floris IV, Count of Holland 
1 CONT Henry II of Brabant (1207–1248) 
1 CONT Godfrey (1209 – January 21, 1254), Lord of Gaesbeek, married Marie van Oudenaarde 
1 CONT His second marriage was at April 22, 1213 in Soissons to Marie, princess of France, daughter of King Philip II of France. They had two children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Elizabeth (d. October 23, 1272), married in Leuven March 19, 1233 Count Dietrich of Cleves, Lord of Dinslaken (c. 1214–1244), married 1246 Gerhard II, Count of Wassenberg (d. 1255) 
1 CONT Marie, died young 
1 CONT Under Henry I, there was a town policy and town planning. Henry's attention went out to those regions that lent themselves to the extension of his sovereignty and in some locations he used the creation of a new town as an instrume
1 CONC nt in the political organisation of the area. Among the towns to which the Duke gave city rights and trade privileges was 's-Hertogenbosch.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was buried in St. Pieters chapter church at Leuven.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also: Dukes of Brabant family tree
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Godfrey III of Leuven Duke of Lower Lotharingia
1 CONT 1190–1235 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Henry II of Brabant 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Godfrey III of Leuven, landgrave of Brabant Duke of Brabant
1 CONT 1183/1184–1235 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Henry II of Brabant 
