0 @N6000000002187590036@ NOTE Matilda of England
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Matilda of England (1156 - July 13, 1189), also known as Maud, was the eldest daughter of Henry II of England and Eleanor of Aquitaine.
1 CONT
1 CONT Matilda was a younger maternal half-sister of Marie de Champagne and Alix of France. She was a younger sister of William, Count of Poitiers and Henry the Young King. She was also an older sister of Richard I of England, Geoffrey I
1 CONC I, Duke of Brittany, Leonora of Aquitaine, Joan of England and John of England. Matilda seems to have spent much of her early life in the company of her mother, Queen Eleanor.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1165 Rainald of Dassel, Archbishop of Cologne, arrived at the court of King Henry II at Rouen, to negotiate a German match for Matilda. There was conflict during the negotiations, however, when Robert de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of L
1 CONC eicester refused to greet the archbishop, alleging him to be a schismatic and a supporter of the anti-pope, Victor IV. The original plan to match a daughter of Henry II with a son of Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor was abandoned, 
1 CONC and instead Matilda left England in September 1167 to marry Henry the Lion.
1 CONT
1 CONT She married Henry the Lion, Duke of Saxony and Bavaria, on February 1, 1168 at Brunswick. They had four sons and one daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT Matilda (Richenza) (1172-1213), married Geoffrey III, Count of Perche. 
1 CONT Henry I, Palatine Count of the Rhein (1173-1227). 
1 CONT Lothar (1174-1190). 
1 CONT Otto IV, Holy Roman Emperor and Duke of Swabia (1175-1218). 
1 CONT William, Duke of Lüneburg (1184-1213). 
1 CONT At the time of their marriage, Henry the Lion was one of the most powerful allies of Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor. Matilda governed her husband's vast estates during his absence in the Holy Land from 1172 to 1173. In 1174, Henr
1 CONC y the Lion became involved in a conflict with the Emperor Frederick, and Henry and Matilda were forced to flee Germany and take refuge in Normandy at her father's court in 1182. During this time at the royal court at Argentan, Mat
1 CONC ilda became acquainted with the troubador Bertran de Born, who, calling her "Elena" or "Lana", made her the object of his desire in two of his poems of "courtly love".
1 CONT
1 CONT Matilda, her husband, and their family remained in Normandy under the protection and support of King Henry until 1185, when they were able to return to Saxony. When her father Henry II died in 1189, Matilda survived him by only on
1 CONC e week.
1 CONT
1 CONT The picture shows an idealized portrait made between 1230 and 1240 on the tomb of Matilda and Henry the Lion in Brunswick cathedral.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT Betran de Born, Casutz sui de mal en pena 
1 CONT Bertran de Born, Ges de disnar non for'oimais maitis 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Ralph of Diceto 
1 CONT Robert of Torigny 
1 CONT Wheeler, Bonnie. Eleanor of Aquitaine: Lord and Lady, 2002 
1 CONT Diggelmann, Lindsay. [http://muse.jhu.edu/demo/parergon/v022/22.1diggelmann01.html 
1 CONT Exile and the Poetic Standpoint of the Troubadour Bertran de Born], 2005
0 @N6000000000437062296@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Duke Name Suffix:<NSFX> of Saxony
0 @N6000000002187590052@ NOTE Henry the Lion
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry the Lion (1129 – August 6, 1195; in German, Heinrich der Löwe) was a member of the Welf dynasty and Duke of Saxony as Henry III since 1142, and Duke of Bavaria as Henry XII since 1156. He held both duchies until 1180 and was
1 CONC  the most powerful of the German princes of his time, until the rival Hohenstaufen dynasty succeeded in isolating him and eventually deprived him of his duchies of Bavaria and Saxony during the reign of his cousin Frederick I and 
1 CONC of Frederick's son and successor Henry VI.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the height of his reign, Henry ruled over a vast territory stretching from the coasts of the North and Baltic Seas to the Alps, and from Westphalia to Pomerania. Henry achieved this great power in part by his political and mili
1 CONC tary acumen, in part through the legacies of his four grandparents. He was the son of Henry the Proud, duke of Bavaria and Saxony, who was the son of Duke Welf IV and an heiress of the Billungs, former dukes of Saxony. Henry's mot
1 CONC her was Gertrud, only daughter of Emperor Lothair II and his wife Richenza of Nordheim, heiress to the Saxon territories of Nordheim and the properties of the Brunones, counts of Brunswick.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry's father died in 1139, aged 32, when Henry was still a child. King Conrad III had dispossessed Henry the Proud, who had been his rival for the crown in 1138, of his duchies in 1138 and 1139, handing Saxony to Albert the Bear
1 CONC  and Bavaria to Leopold of Austria. Henry, however, did not relinquish his claims to his inheritance, and Conrad returned Saxony to him in 1142. In 1156 Henry also reacquired Bavaria by a decision of the new Emperor Frederick Barb
1 CONC arossa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry is the founder of Munich (1157/58; München) and Lübeck (1159); he also founded and developed the cities of Stade, Lüneburg and Brunswick. In Brunswick, his capital, he had a bronze lion, his heraldic animal, erected in the y
1 CONC ard of his castle Dankwarderode in 1166 — the first bronze statue north of the Alps. Later, he had Brunswick Cathedral built close to the statue.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1147 Henry married Clementia of Zähringen, thereby gaining her hereditary territories in Swabia. He divorced her in 1162, apparently under pressure from Emperor Frederick Barbarossa, who did not cherish Welfish possessions in h
1 CONC is home area and offered Henry several fortresses in Saxony in exchange. In 1168 Henry married Matilda (1156 -1189), the daughter of Henry II of England and Eleanor of Aquitaine and sister of Richard Lionheart.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry long and faithfully supported his older cousin, Emperor Frederick I (Barbarossa) in the latter's attempts to solidify his hold on the Imperial Crown and his repeated wars with the cities of Lombardy and the Popes, several ti
1 CONC mes turning the tide of battle in Frederick's favor with his fierce Saxon knights. But in 1174, Henry refused to aid Frederick in a renewed invasion of Lombardy, because he was preoccupied with securing his own borders in the East
1 CONC  and did not consider these Italian adventures worth the effort, even when Frederick offered him the rich Imperial City of Goslar in southern Saxony as a reward, a prize Henry had long coveted.
1 CONT
1 CONT Barbarossa's expedition into Lombardy ended in utter failure, and he bitterly resented Henry for failing to support him. Taking advantage of the hostility of other German princes to Henry, who had successfully established a powerf
1 CONC ul and contiguous state comprising Saxony, Bavaria and substantial territories in the north and east of Germany, Frederick had Henry tried in absentia for insubordination by a court of bishops and princes in 1180. Declaring that I
1 CONC mperial law overruled traditional German law, the court had Henry stripped of his lands and declared an outlaw. Frederick then invaded Saxony with an Imperial army to bring his cousin to his knees. Henry's allies deserted him, and
1 CONC  he finally had to submit in November 1181 at a Reichstag in Erfurt. He was exiled from Germany in 1182 for three years, stayed with his father-in-law, Henry II of England, in Normandy before being allowed back into Germany in 118
1 CONC 5. He was exiled again in 1188, and his wife Matilda died in 1189.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Frederick Barbarossa went on the Crusade of 1189, Henry returned to Saxony where he mobilized an army of his faithful and conquered and ravaged the rich city of Bardowick as punishment for her disloyalty. Only the churches we
1 CONC re left standing. Barbarossa's son, Emperor Henry VI, again defeated the Duke, but in 1194, with his end approaching, he made his peace with the Emperor, and returned to his much diminished lands around Brunswick, where he finishe
1 CONC d his days as duke of Brunswick, peacefully sponsoring arts and architecture, and died on 6 August 1195.
1 CONT
1 CONT The picture at the top right, taken from his tomb in Brunswick Cathedral constructed between 1230 and 1240, shows an idealized image. When the Nazis exhumed his corpse, they were disappointed to find a comparatively small man with
1 CONC  black hair. This, presumably, was an inheritance from the northern Italian ancestors of the Welfs, the counts of Este.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry had, among others, the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT by his first wife, Clementia 
1 CONT Gertrude of Bavaria (1155-1197), married first Frederick IV, Duke of Swabia and then King Canute VI of Denmark 
1 CONT by his second wife, Matilda 
1 CONT Henry I, Count Palatine of the Rhine (1173-1227) 
1 CONT Lothar of Bavaria (1174-1190) 
1 CONT Otto IV, Holy Roman Emperor and Duke of Swabia (1175-1218) 
1 CONT William of Winchester (1184-1213) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Benjamin Arnold, "Henry the Lion and His Time", Journal of Medieval History, vol. 22, pp. 379-393 (1996) 
1 CONT Karl Jordan, Henry the Lion. A Biography, ISBN 0198219695 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Albert the Bear Duke of Saxony
1 CONT 1142–1180 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Bernard III 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Henry XI Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT 1156–1180 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Otto III 
1 CONT
1 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Duke of Saxony (1142 - 1180). Duke of Bavaria (1156 -1180). Deposition in 1180. Source: RoyaList (Henry was the son of Henry the Proud, Duke of Saxony, and Gertrude, the only daughter of Lothair the Saxon. In 1172, Henry made a pi
1 CONC lgrimage to Jerusalem, returning the next year. Holy Roman Emperor Frederick Barbarossa twice forced Henry into exile, in 1182 and 1189. Henry spent much of the time during his exiles in England.) Source: Brian Tompsett. (Added to
1 CONC  his possesions the counties of Mecklenburg and Luneburg, by conquest from the Sclaves. He neglected his duty to Emperor Barbarossa and was publicly proscribed in 1180. He was obliged to absent himself from Germany for three years
1 CONC  and he repaired to England with his second consort Maud.) Source: University Bielefeld. **Heinrich der Löwe**. 1129 / 1131 - Geburt Heinrichs des Löwen. 4. Dezember 1137 - Tod seines Großvaters, Kaiser Lothar III. (von Supplingen
1 CONC burg). 4. März 1138 - Wahl des Staufers Konrad III. zum König. Juli 1138 - Reichsacht über den Vater Heinrichs des Löwen, Heinrich den Stolzen, Herzog von Bayern und Sachsen - der Askanier Markgraf Albrecht der Bär wird an seiner 
1 CONC Stelle Herzog von Sachsen. 20. Oktober 1139 - Tod Heinrichs des Stolzen. Frühjahr 1139 - Der Babenberger Markgraf Leopold IV. wird als Nachfolger Heinrichs des Stolzen Herzog von Bayern. 11. Juni 1141 - Tod der Großmutter Heinrich
1 CONC s des Löwen, der Kaiserin-Witwe Richenza. 1142 - Belehnung Heinrichs des Löwen mit dem Herzogtum Sachsen - Heirat seiner Mutter Gertrud mit dem Babenberger Heinrich Jasomirgott, Herzog von Bayern. 18. April 1143 - Tod der Mutter. 
1 CONC 1144 - Heinrich der Löwe entscheidet die Verhandlungen mit dem Bremer Erzbischof um das Stader Erbe im Handstreich von Ramelsloh mit Waffengewalt für sich. 1147 - Teilnahme am Wendenkreuzzug parallel zum 2. Kreuzzug. 1148 / 1149 -
1 CONC  Heirat mit Clementia, Tochter Herzog Konrads von Zähringen. 1149 - "Kleiner Investiturstreit" um nordelbische Bistüer : Heinrich der Löwe maßt sich die dem König zustehende Einsetzung der Bischöfe an. 15. Februar 1152 - Tod König
1 CONC  Konrads III. 4. März 1152 - Wahl des Staufers Friedrich Barbarossa zum König. Frühjahr 1154 - Investiturprivileg Heinrichs des Löwen für die nordelbischen Bistümer. 1154 - 1155 - Teilnahme am 1. Italienzug und der Kaiserkrönung F
1 CONC riedrichs I. in Rom. September 1156 - Belehnung mit dem um die Mark Österreich verminderten Herzogtum Bayern - Geburt der unehelichen Tochter Mathilde. 1157 / 1158 - Gründung der Stadt München. Frühjahr 1159 - Neugründung der Stad
1 CONC t Lübeck. 1159 - 1160 - Teilnahme am 2. Italienzug Friedrichs I. 1160 - 1175 - Heinrich der Löwe ernennt Braunschweig zur Welfenhochburg und erbaut dort die Residenz Burg Dankwarderode. 1160 - Eroberung des Obodritenlandes. 23. No
1 CONC vember 1162 - Scheidung von Clementia von Zähringen. 1165 - Verlobung Heinrichs des Löwen mit Mathilde, Tochter des englischen Königs Heinrichs II. um 1163 / 1169 - Errichtung des Löwenstandbildes in Braunschweig. 1166 - Beginn er
1 CONC bitterter Kämpfe mit den sächsischen Fürsten. 1. Februar 1168 - Hochzeit der englischen Königstochter Mathilde und Heinrichs des Löwen im Mindener Dom. 1170 - Ende der Kämpfe in Sachsen. 1172 - Pilgerfahrt nach Jerusalem, Treffen 
1 CONC mit dem oströischen Kaiser Manuel II. in Byzanz - Geburt der zweiten Tochter Richenza. 1173 - Geburt des ersten Sohnes Heinrich, des späteren Pfalzgrafen bei Rhein und Titularherzogs von Sachsen. ab 1173 - Neubau der Stiftskirche 
1 CONC St.Balsius ("Dom") in Braunschweig. Januar / Februar 1176 - Heinrich der Löwe lehnt es ab, Friedrich I in seinem Konflikt mit den oberitalienischen Städten Hilfe zu leisten - "Kniefall Friedrich I." in Chiavenna. 1177 - Ausbruch n
1 CONC euer Kämpfe in Sachsen - Geburt des dritten Sohnes Otto, des späteren Kaisers Otto IV. 1178 - Erbregelung Herzog Welfs VI. in Schwaben zugunsten der Staufer. 11. November 1178 - Beginn des Prozesses gegen Heinrich den Löwen. 29. J
1 CONC uni 1179 - Achtspruch auf dem Hoftag in Magdeburg. 13. Januar 1180 - Lehnrechtliche Verurteilung auf dem Hoftag in Würzburg - Verhängung der Oberacht. 13. April 1180 - Aufteilung des Herzogtums Sachsen : Philipp von Heinsberg, Erz
1 CONC bischof von Köln, wird Herzog von Westfalen; der Askanier Bernhard von Anhalt wird Herzog von Sachsen und Engern; Landgraf Ludwig III. von Thüringen wird sächsischer Pfalzgraf - Beginn der Reichsheerfahrt gegen Heinrich den Löwen 
1 CONC - Pfalzgraf Otto von Wittelsbach wird Herzog von Bayern - Steiermark wird vom Herzogtum Bayern abgetrennt. November 1181 - Unterwerfung Heinrichs des Löwen auf dem Hoftag in Erfurt. ab Juli 1182 - Pilgerfahrt nach Santiago de Comp
1 CONC ostela. 1182 / 1185 - Erstes Exil in England bei König Heinrich II. 1188 - Geburt des vierten Sohnes Wilhelm (von Lüneburg). Ostern 1189 - Zweites Exil Heinrichs des Löwen in England. 28. Juni 1189 - Tod der Ehefrau Mathilde. 1190
1 CONC  - Tod des Sohnes Lothar. 1191 - Der erstgeborene Sohn Heinrich von Braunschweig verläßt das Italienheer. Pfingsten 1192 - Achterklärung über den Erstgeborenen. 1192 - Erfolglose Belagerung Braunschweigs durch die Gegner Heinrichs
1 CONC  des Löwen. 1194 - Heirat zwischen Heinrich von Braunschweig und Agnes, Tochter des staufischen Pfalzgrafen bei Rhein Konrad. März 1194 - Treffen und Aussöhnung mit Kaiser Heinrich VI. in Tilleda. 6. August 1195 - Tod Heinrichs de
1 CONC s Löwen. 
