0 @N6000000000337158028@ NOTE (Research):Alfonso III, 1265-91, king of Aragón and count of Barcelona (1285-91), son and successor of Peter III. He was forced to grant wide privileges to the cortes of the Aragonese nobles. At first he supported the claim to Sic
1 CONC ily of his brother James (later James II of Aragón) against Charles II of Naples. Later, however, he recognized papal suzerainty over Sicily and pressed James to abandon his claim. He also made war on Castile and on his uncle, Jam
1 CONC es I of Majorca. James II succeeded him.
0 @N6000000002176686061@ NOTE He was forced to grant wide powers to nobles in 1287.
0 @N6000000002188005655@ NOTE Alfonso III of Aragon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso III of AragonAlfons or Alfonso III of Aragon (1265 – June 18, 1291, also Alfons II of Barcelona). Later to be known as "Alfons el Liberal" (The Liberal), was the king of Aragon and count of Barcelona from 1285 to 1291.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a son of Peter III of Aragon and his Queen consort Constance of Sicily, daughter and heiress of Manfred of Sicily. His maternal grandmother Beatrice of Savoy was a daughter of Amadeus IV of Savoy and Anne of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon after assuming the throne, he conducted a campaign to reincorperate the Balearic Islands into the Kingdom of Aragon - which had been lost due to the division of the kingdom by his grandfather, James I of Aragon. Thus in 1285 
1 CONC he declared war on his uncle, James I of Majorca, and conquered both Majorca (1285) and Ibiza (1286), effectively reassuming suzerainty over the Kingdom of Majorca. The notable high point of his rule saw the capture of Minorca fro
1 CONC m the Caliphate of Córdoba on 17 January 1287 - that date now serving as Minorca's national holiday.
1 CONT
1 CONT He initally sought to maintain the Arogonese control over Sicily early in his reign by supporting the claims to island of his brother, James II of Aragon. However, he later pressed his brother to retract the claims and instead sup
1 CONC ported claim from the Papal States.
1 CONT
1 CONT His reign was marred by a constitutional struggle with the Argonese nobles, which eventually culminated in the articles of the Union of Aragon - the so called "Magna Carta of Aragon", which devolved several key royal powers into t
1 CONC he hands of lesser nobles. His inability to resist the demands of his nobles was to leave a heritage of disunity in Aragon and further dissent amongst the nobilty, who increasingly saw little reason to respect the throne, and brou
1 CONC ght the Kingdom of Aragon close of anarchy.
1 CONT
1 CONT He died at the age of 27 in 1291, and buried in Barcelona. He bore no children, nor did he marry.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dante Alighieri, in the Divine Comedy, recounts that he saw Alfonso's spirit seated outside the gates of Purgatory with the other monarchs whom Dante blamed for the chaotic political state of Europe during the 13th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Alighieri, Dante, Purgatorio, Canto VII, l. 115ff. 
1 CONT Nelson, Lynn. The Chronicle of San Juan De LA Pena: A Fourteenth-Century Official History of the Crown of Aragon (University of Pennsylvania Press, 1991) ISBN:0812213521 
1 CONT O'Callaghan, Joseph. A History of Medieval Spain (Cornell University Press, 1983) ISBN:0801492645 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Peter III King of Aragon and Valencia,
1 CONT Count of Barcelona
1 CONT 1285-1291 Succeeded by:
1 CONT James II 
