0 @N6000000002187747249@ NOTE Margaret II, Countess of Flanders
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret, called of Constantinople (1202–1278) was countess of Flanders from 1244 to 1278 and countess of Hainaut from 1244 to 1246. Her coat of arms was "Chevronny or and sable".
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Biography
1 CONT She was the younger daughter of Baldwin I of Constantinople, who was also count of Flanders and Hainaut, and Marie of Champagne. He left on the Fourth Crusade before she was born, and her mother left two years later, leaving Marga
1 CONC ret and her older sister Jeanne in the guardianship of their uncle Philip of Namur.
1 CONT
1 CONT After her mother died in 1204, and her father the next year, the now-orphaned Margaret and her sister remained under Philip's guardianship until 1208, when he gave their wardship to King Philip II of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1212 Margaret married Bouchard d'Avesnes, a prominent Hainaut nobleman. This was apparently a love match, though it was approved by Margaret's sister Jeanne, who had herself recently married. The two sisters subsequently had a 
1 CONC falling-out over Margaret's share of their inheritance, which led Jeanne to attempt to get Margaret's marriage dissolved. She alleged that the marriage was invalid, and without much inspection of the facts of the case Pope Innocen
1 CONC t III condemned the marriage, though he did not formally annul it.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bourchard and Margaret continued as a married couple, having two children, as their conflict with Jeanne grew violent and Bouchard was captured and imprisoned in 1219. He was released in 1221 on the condition that the couple separ
1 CONC ate and that Bouchard get absolution from the pope. While he was in Rome, Jeanne convinced Margaret to re-marry, this time to William of Dampierre, a nobleman from Champagne. From this marriage Margaret had a son: Guy of Dampierre
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT This situation caused something of a scandal, for the marriage was possibly bigamous, and violated the church's strictures on consanguinity as well. The disputes regarding the validity of the 2 marriages and the legitimacy of her 
1 CONC children by each husband continued for decades, becoming entangled in the politics of the Holy Roman Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1246 king Louis IX of France, acting as an arbitrator, gave the right to inherit Flanders to the Dampierre children, and the rights to Hainaut to the Avesnes children. This would seem to have settled the matter, but in 1253 pro
1 CONC blems arose again. The eldest son, John d'Avesnes was uneasy about his rights, convinced William II, Count of Holland to seize Hainaut and the parts of Flanders which were within the bounds of the empire. William of Holland was, a
1 CONC s emperor-elect, overlord for these territories, and also John's brother-in-law. A civil war followed, which ended when the Avesnes forces defeated and imprisoned the Dampierres at the Battle of Walcheren.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT Coat of Arms in the Walford Roll 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Jeanne Countess of Flanders
1 CONT 1244–1278 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Guy 
1 CONT Countess of Hainaut
1 CONT 1244–1246 Succeeded by:
1 CONT John I 
0 @N6000000003051263442@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Margaret II, Countess of Flanders", retrieved 22 Dec 07]
1 CONT Margaret, called of Constantinople (1202-1278) was countess of Flanders from 1244 to 1278 and countess of Hainaut from 1244 to 1246.
1 CONT
1 CONT History and Family
1 CONT She was the younger daughter of Baldwin I of Constantinople, who was also count of Flanders and Hainaut, and Marie de Champagne. He left on the Fourth Crusade before she was born, and her mother left two years later, leaving Marga
1 CONC ret and her older sister Jeanne in the guardianship of their uncle Philip of Namur.
1 CONT
1 CONT After her mother died in 1204, and her father the next year, the now-orphaned Margaret and her sister remained under Philip's guardianship until 1208, when he gave their wardship to King Philip II of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1212 Margaret married Bouchard d'Avesnes, a prominent Hainaut nobleman. This was apparently a love match, though it was approved by Margaret's sister Jeanne, who had herself recently married. The two sisters subsequently had a 
1 CONC falling-out over Margaret's share of their inheritance, which led Jeanne to attempt to get Margaret's marriage dissolved. She alleged that the marriage was invalid, and without much inspection of the facts of the case Pope Innocen
1 CONC t III condemned the marriage, though he did not formally annul it.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bourchard and Margaret continued as a married couple, having two children, as their conflict with Jeanne grew violent and Bouchard was captured and imprisoned in 1219. He was released in 1221 on the condition that the couple separ
1 CONC ate and that Bouchard get absolution from the pope. While he was in Rome, Jeanne convinced Margaret to re-marry, this time to William II of Dampierre, a nobleman from Champagne. From this marriage Margaret had a son: Guy of Dampie
1 CONC rre.
1 CONT
1 CONT This situation caused something of a scandal, for the marriage was possibly bigamous, and violated the church's strictures on consanguinity as well. The disputes regarding the validity of the two marriages and the legitimacy of he
1 CONC r children by each husband continued for decades, becoming entangled in the politics of the Holy Roman Empire and resulting in the long War of the Succession of Flanders and Hainault.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1246 king Louis IX of France, acting as an arbitrator, gave the right to inherit Flanders to the Dampierre children, and the rights to Hainaut to the Avesnes children. This would seem to have settled the matter, but in 1253 pro
1 CONC blems arose again. The eldest son, John d'Avesnes, who was uneasy about his rights, convinced William of Holland, the German king recognized by the pro-papal forces, to seize Hainaut and the parts of Flanders which were within the
1 CONC  bounds of the empire. William of Holland was theoretically, as king, overlord for these territories, and also John's brother-in-law. A civil war followed, which ended when the Avesnes forces defeated and imprisoned the Dampierres
1 CONC  at the Battle of Walcheren.
